Part 1 . Chapter 1 to Chapter 60.

“Cars are utterly useless. And they lack dignity,” Roselyn declared softly, lifting her teacup. The delicate clink of fine bone china echoed refreshingly in the room. The cup, adorned with blue violets, was one of her prized possessions.

“Lack dignity? That’s because you haven’t seen Frauzen’s latest model. It’s so elegant and dignified that you’d fall for it at first sight,” Viscount Andover insisted passionately. 

Roselyn merely smiled at her father. It seemed that the “car fever,” which had plagued gentlemen across the empire ever since its invention two years ago, had finally reached the tranquil Andover estate.

“Chasing trends is a pastime for the nouveau riche, not a virtue for gentlemen. Cars are frivolous,” she replied.  

“Good heavens, my dear. You’re clueless! Even the Duke of Windburg rides around in one!”  

“Dearest Father, if you were the second richest man in the empire after His Majesty, I might not object. But surely you needn’t hear me say that outright?”  

The Viscount chuckled heartily from the window where he was smoking, pointing at his daughter with his cigarette in approval of her clever rebuttal. Roselyn realized that her father was nearly persuaded.

“You already own two splendid four-wheeled carriages, Father. Do you even know how much their upkeep costs annually? Adding a car to that would be extravagant. Besides, can we even trust those machines’ safety? Victor certainly isn’t ready to assume the responsibilities of the next Viscount yet.”  

“Fine, fine. Enough lecturing, Lady Viscountess,” Alfred Fairfield, the Viscount of Andover, conceded with a grin. His eldest daughter, Roselyn Eleanor Fairfield, placed her teacup back onto its saucer with a light clink, as if formally declaring the proposal rejected.

The father and daughter were enjoying their afternoon tea in the second-floor parlor of Fairfield House, a space reserved exclusively for the immediate family. To distinguish it from the grand reception room on the first floor, the household staff referred to this cozy room as the “family room.”

Unlike the downstairs parlor, which was adorned with precious furniture and famous artworks to impress guests, this family parlor held a special charm for Roselyn. It was where she felt at ease, as if her life were free of any troubles, as if simply living each day brought immense contentment. And, in truth, that was largely the case.

What trouble could Roselyn possibly have? 

The household ran smoothly, bills were paid on time, income was stable, and the staff were diligent. Her father, her three siblings, and she herself were all in good health. It was, by all accounts, a trouble-free life.

“I don’t get it. Why does Father never win against you, Sister? What’s even harder to understand is how you can stop him from buying what he wants. How does that even work? His name’s on the checkbook and the bank accounts! If I were him, I’d have bought the car already,” Andrea said, pouting.

“Andie,” Roselyn said, donning a mock-stern expression as she looked at her youngest sister. 

It occurred to her that perhaps her life wasn’t entirely “trouble-free.” If she had to name a problem—or something akin to a problem—it would be her sixteen-year-old youngest sibling.

“The Viscount is basically powerless over the Viscountess.”

“Was Father like that with Mother, too?”  

“Mother even decided the fabric and buttons for his frock coat.”  

“No way. Really, Sister?”  

Andrea’s wide eyes darted between her father and sister. Meeting her large, innocent gaze, Roselyn felt an involuntary pang in her heart.

Andrea had inherited their mother’s lustrous golden hair and doll-like beauty, but she had no memories of her. Viscountess Margaret Fairfield had died during her fifth childbirth, along with the baby, when Andrea was only three. Lucas, the third child, had been just four at the time.

Only Victor, the eldest son and Roselyn’s closest sibling, shared any tangible memories of their mother. And, of course, their father.

“Yes, Father always respected Mother’s decisions. But that was because he loved and honored her—a true gentleman through and through,” Roselyn said gently. Andrea lowered her gaze and pursed her lips before beaming proudly as she picked up her teacup.

Next week marked the thirteenth anniversary of Margaret’s passing. For thirteen years, Roselyn had acted as Viscountess in her mother’s place. Everyone at Fairfield House treated her as the lady of the manor, and even her father affectionately called her “Lady Viscountess.” This was not an exaggeration, as Roselyn held significant decision-making power and responsibility for the household’s affairs.

“Rosie, I was thinking of inviting Mrs. Selma to the memorial dinner next week. What do you think?”  

Roselyn was not surprised when her father sought her opinion on the matter. She was only slightly taken aback.

“Of course, Sister will oppose it, but she’ll agree out loud. After all, Mrs. Selma is Father’s ‘closest friend.’ How could she refuse?” Andrea teased.

“Andie,” Roselyn said, her expression turning more severe. She was dismayed by her youngest sister’s impertinence, but also perhaps trying to mask her own discomfort.

Mrs. Selma, a widow, had appeared in Andover three years earlier. After losing her businessman husband, she had moved in with her brother, Martin Collin, who worked as the accountant for Fairfield House. Martin often accompanied his grieving sister to social gatherings, including Viscount Andover’s birthday banquet.

While Roselyn didn’t begrudge her father’s friendship with Mrs. Selma, she had been shocked when the widow became his “closest friend.” It wasn’t the idea of her father having a female companion that troubled her—it was that the companion was Mrs. Selma.

Annie Selma was neither particularly refined nor cultured by Roselyn’s standards. In fact, her frivolous demeanor raised concerns.

More importantly, Mrs. Selma wasn’t a noblewoman.



“Windburg Station! Disembark here!”  

The express train from the capital rolled into the station amidst a flurry of activity. Porters barked orders as passengers poured out of the train, reuniting with waiting friends and family in a cacophony of voices. Steam billowed from the locomotive, lingering briefly before dissipating.

At the first-class carriage near the front, the door finally opened. Jared Glenn stepped out, acknowledging the conductor’s farewell with a nod.

The biting northern air greeted him with a sharp chill. It ruffled his dark hair and long coat as he stepped onto the platform. After spending time in a warmer climate, the cold of his hometown felt especially piercing.

In the capital city of Isen, spring had already taken hold, with early-season roses having bloomed and faded. 

But here in Windburg, winter lingered stubbornly, clinging to the land.

“Welcome, Your Grace.”  

 

A middle-aged man awaiting him on the platform greeted the Duke. It was Peter Morrison, the chief aide.  

 

“I’m glad to see you’ve returned safely.”  

 

“I stayed longer than I should have,” Jared replied, a hint of self-deprecation in his tone. He still couldn’t believe it had been a full month since he’d left home. Ever since becoming Duke, he had never been away from his domain for such an extended period.  

 

The train’s doors remained wide open, and Jared stood with his back to them, gazing at the bustling platform and the grand station building.  

 

Windburg. 

 

The City of Winds. 

 

Even in mid-March, the chill lingered across his domain.  

 

It was spring, and yet.  

 

“Let’s go.”  

 

His curt command prompted Morrison to clear the path without delay. As Jared strode forward, two attendants, his security detail, and porters followed closely behind. The engineer and station staff bowed deeply, paying their respects, while passersby murmured as they recognized him.  

 

“Look, it’s the Duke. The Duke of Windburg.”

 

Jared offered neither glance nor acknowledgment to anyone. As if oblivious to the whispers, he kept his gaze forward, striding steadily as he led his entourage out of the station with ease and poise.  

 



 

Windburg was a region rich in mineral resources, with an industry-driven economy. Historically, it served as the northern capital and, administratively, the provincial hub of the north. Though the feudal system had long since become a relic of the past, the duchy still carried its title. Of course, apart from a stipend drawn from the region’s taxes as a “dignity allowance,” the Duke held little real power.  

 

The Glen family, once the kings of the north, now bore the moniker “kings of the railways.” The railway rights owned by the Ducal House of Windburg accounted for nearly half of the empire’s total network. The other half belonged to the imperial family, whose tense yet symbiotic rivalry with the Glens provided endless fodder for gossips and pundits alike.  

 

Historically and financially, the Glens were the only family that could rival the imperial household. It was inevitable, then, that the Duke of Windburg, whether by preference or not, remained a constant subject of public intrigue.  

 

His residence, the Maxville Estate, was no exception.  

 

“Do you think he’ll arrive alone?”  

 

Victoria Wadsworth, the Countess of Benford, murmured the same question for the third time. Her husband, Count Benford, swallowed a sip of brandy as he observed his wife.  

 

The drive from Windburg Station to Maxville took roughly 20 minutes. At any moment, the Duke could arrive.  

 

“Well, he hasn’t said anything, so we’ll find out soon enough.”  

 

“But things seemed to go so well that evening, didn’t they? Don’t you think so, dear?”  

 

“It wasn’t bad.”  

 

“They even went for a moonlit walk. I’m sure they visited the Marystern Gardens. Where else could be more romantic for a proposal? A royal ball, the Glowflower Garden, and the air cleared between them—it was all so perfect!”  

 

“Enough of this nonsense,” the Duchess Dowager, Diane Glen, finally cut in, as though unable to bear it any longer.  

 

“If he wasn’t coming alone, he would’ve sent a letter or at least a telegram. Do you think it makes sense that he’d bring his fiancée home without any advance notice?” Diane Glen’s emerald-green eyes conveyed her exasperation as she fixed her gaze on her niece.  

 

Her words were so rational that the Benfords fell silent, though Victoria managed to detect a trace of tension beneath her aunt’s composed demeanor.  

 

After all, Diane was likely far more curious than anyone about whether Jared would bring ‘her’ home. More than curious, she was undoubtedly feeling tortured by the uncertainty.  

 

Diane Glen, the previous Duchess and Jared’s mother, had once sent away a fiancée Jared brought home three years prior, effectively blocking their marriage. Having chosen the brides for her two older sons, she felt it only fitting to select a suitable match for her youngest. Thus, when Jared defied her by proposing to a woman and demanding their engagement be announced, she’d been deeply shaken.  

 

At the time, the so-called fiancée was a woman entirely unfit to become Duchess. Even Jared himself had known this. Despite that, he had stubbornly insisted on the marriage, causing shock and outrage within the family. To this day, Diane felt faint at the memory of those events.  

 

Though she’d taken on the role of the villain to preserve the family’s and her son’s dignity, the fallout made her question whether it had truly been the right decision.  

 

Since that incident, Jared had remained unmarried. At thirty, the unmarried Duke of Windburg was the subject of endless speculation, and Diane had spent the past three years avoiding the social scene. Despite successfully averting a disastrous marriage, the family’s and her son’s dignity remained precarious.  

 

Thus, when she’d heard whispers of Jared reuniting with his former fiancée in Essen, Diane had been horrified. Yet she’d resigned herself to the notion that if they were to marry after all, it might at least resolve the situation. Jared was her only remaining son and the head of the Glen family, with no direct heirs to follow him.  

 

The Maxville Estate needed a Duchess, desperately.  

 

“Why haven’t we heard anything yet? He should be here by now.”  

 

Victoria murmured as she glanced at the grand clock across the room. Almost simultaneously, the head butler knocked lightly and stepped inside. All three nobles in the drawing room turned to him.  

 

“My Lady, I have just been informed that His Grace has passed through the front gate.”  

 

“Thank you, Sterling.”  

 

At Diane’s response, the Benfords rose from their seats. Diane Glen followed, rising gracefully after a brief pause. She glanced at her niece and nephew-in-law with a faintly self-deprecating smile.  

 

“Let’s go.”  

 

Count Benford returned her smile with a slight nod, while Victoria looked at her aunt with a mix of anticipation and restraint. Diane exhaled softly, raised her chin, and led the way out of the drawing room.  

 



 

Even after passing through the front gates, the car continued its slow progress. The estate’s vast lawns and gardens required another eight minutes of travel to reach the mansion. The gently curving driveway, rather than a direct route, was a hallmark of aristocratic design.  

 

There was no need to rush here. One could stroll at a leisurely, elegant pace. After all, nobility valued dignity over efficiency.  

 

Seated in the back of the slowly moving car, Jared gazed out the window. The early northern sunset cast a reddish glow over the grand stone building ahead. The surrounding landscape, with its endless stretches of lawn, was wide open and unobstructed. Soon, as spring deepened, fresh green buds would sprout across the grounds.  

 

As he observed the scene, Jared spoke.  

 

“Is there no way to have him dismissed by the board?”  

 

Peter Morrison, seated beside him, turned his head. Jared continued speaking, his gaze still fixed on the window.  

 

“I’d prefer not to get directly involved.”  

 

“I’ve pointed this out multiple times, but it seems the chairman is in a difficult position as well. If Your Grace were to lend your support, it would make a significant difference…”  

 

“Relay the message once more: they should resolve it themselves to the best of their ability.”  

 

It seemed much had happened during his month-long absence. The reports from his aides had been flowing nonstop since he’d stepped off the train and into the car.  

 

Among the various issues, the matter concerning the steel company irritated Jared the most. He was particularly displeased that the chairman had used his aide to make a personal appeal to him.  

 

The core of the issue was a board member accused of embezzlement. The allegations were both embarrassing and delicate, making the accused difficult to handle due to his noble status. The chairman suggested that Jared, as Duke of Windburg, could use his “social influence” to discreetly pressure the individual into resigning, thus sparing the company embarrassment and preserving the accused’s dignity; a supposed win-win solution.  

 

That damn dignity.

 

“The chairman’s intentions are clear, but I am not an absolute monarch ruling over a private domain. I cannot manipulate personnel matters from behind the scenes as I please. The punishment and dismissal of a board member seem like matters the board itself should handle.”  

 

Only after speaking calmly did Jared turn his head to meet his aide’s eyes, ensuring his intent to conclude the matter was unmistakable. To maintain a tone of refinement, he had learned to replace strong words with firm gazes.  

 

For the sake of that ‘dmned dignity.’

 

“Yes, Your Grace. I will relay your message,” Morrison replied. Having served the Duke for over three years, he likely understood Jared’s intent immediately.  

 

Jared’s gaze shifted toward the main entrance of the mansion, now visible in the distance. There stood the Duchess Dowager and the Benford Count and Countess, awaiting his arrival. Behind them, the head butler, his staff, the head housekeeper, and the maids had formed a line. Nearly half of Maxville’s household staff had paused their work to welcome the Duke home.  

 

Were they all here to see if he had brought a companion? What thoughts were running through their minds as they prepared to greet their master after his month-long absence?  

 

In moments like this, Jared often felt like an actor stepping onto a stage. He had to appear flawless under the scrutiny of every gaze, pretending not to notice their attention while projecting his best self.  

 

As if nothing in his life was amiss. 

 

As if the Duke of Windburg were already perfect just as he was.  

The destination was near.  

 

Jared fastened the buttons on his coat, which had been left undone, and straightened his posture. He readied a faint, pleasant smile for his otherwise stoic face. He rehearsed the words he would say to his mother, the look he would offer his cousin, and the polite tone he’d reserve for her husband.  

 

The moment he had everything prepared, the car came to a halt. As the butler opened the door, the performance began.  

 

Stepping out of the vehicle, Jared maintained his faint smile and glanced at the welcoming party. He noticed the quick flicker of both disappointment and relief on their faces as they realized that only he and his aide had exited the car.  

 

Pretending not to notice, Jared approached Diane Glen with steady steps.  

 

“Mother, I’m home.”  

 

“Welcome back.”  

 

He embraced his mother lightly before turning to his cousin’s husband.  

 

“Count, we meet again.”  

 

“Welcome home, Your Grace.”  

 

“Thank you.”  

 

After a brief exchange, Jared turned to his cousin.  

 

“Countess.”  

 

“Welcome back. You must be tired after the long journey.”  

 

“I’m more hungry than tired. Sterling, could dinner be served earlier?”  

 

“Of course, Your Grace. I’ll see to it immediately.”  

 

“Excellent.”  

 

After addressing the head butler, Jared finally stepped away from the stage.  

 

“Let’s head inside. Thank you all for the warm welcome.”  

 

He smiled gently, as if nothing was amiss.  

 

“It’s good to be home,” he said, though the words left him feeling slightly bitter. Still, his confident stride into the mansion masked everything perfectly.  

 



 

Roselyn remained in the greenhouse until sundown. The glass structure, tucked into a corner of the estate, was her sanctuary and workspace. If it could be described most accurately, it was less a greenhouse and more a glass barn.  

 

Here, she arranged flowers for vases and wreaths to decorate the estate. Sometimes, she perched on a work stool to read, particularly books deemed “unsuitable for a lady.” Her secret stash of such books was hidden in a drawer beneath neatly stacked terracotta pots.  

 

The greenhouse was entirely her domain; no gardeners or laborers entered. These days, however, her focus wasn’t on flower arranging or reading—it was on farming.  

 

“You’ve grown so much. How lovely,” Roselyn murmured affectionately, smiling down at the tiny plants in her pots. They were seedlings of vegetables she planned to transplant into the garden later.  

 

She loved the thought of feeding her family with vegetables she had grown herself. Though she didn’t wield knives or fire to cook like a proper noblewoman, she occasionally helped decorate cakes for tea parties or formal dinners. Mrs. Tender, the estate’s chef, had even remarked that Roselyn had a natural talent for handling cream.  

 

“It must feel cramped here, doesn’t it? I want to move you to a bigger space soon, but it’s still too cold outside.”  

 

Roselyn smiled warmly as she touched each seedling. Then, a knocking sound came from the glass door.  

 

“Miss.”  

 

“Mary, what is it?”  

 

“You have a visitor.”  

 

“A visitor? Who?”  

 

Roselyn tilted her head in surprise. She hadn’t been expecting anyone.  

 

“Baroness Elwood is here.”  

 

“Aunt Natalie?”  

 

It was an unexpected visit.  

 

“Where is she?”  

 

“I’ve shown her to the drawing room on the first floor.”  

 

“Thank you, Mary. Could you turn off the lamps here?”  

 

“Certainly, Miss.”  

 

Roselyn placed the pot she had been holding back in its spot, stepped out of the greenhouse, and hurried toward the mansion, brushing dirt off her fingers as she walked.  

 

The greenhouse, tucked into a remote corner of the garden, was a little distance from the main house. Walking across the dry grass, Roselyn stopped abruptly and looked up at the sky.  

 

The sunset was remarkably crimson—vivid and striking.  

 

Tilting her head upward, she observed the twilight sky. Darkness was beginning to descend, contrasting with the fiery hues of the setting sun. The violet of violets and the red of roses; she watched the dramatic interplay of colors for a moment before quickening her pace again.  

 

The guest was seated alone in the drawing room. Warmly positioned near the lit fireplace, she had yet to remove her coat and gloves.  

 

“Aunt Natalie, I’m sorry to have kept you waiting.”  

 

“How have you been? You look well.”  

 

“You look well too. How are the baron and my lovelies?”  

 

“They’re fine, thank you.”  

 

“What brings you here at this hour? Did you have business nearby?” 

 

“What business could there possibly be in a remote place like Andover? I came to see you, of course.”  

 

Baroness Natalie Hope of Elwood scoffed lightly as she removed her gloves.  

 

“I can’t stay long. There’s a performance in Windburg I need to attend.”  

“An opera?”  

 

“Yes. Guterheim is starring. You wouldn’t know him, but he’s become hugely popular in Essen. A former member of the Imperial Theater, and oh, how handsome he is!”  

 

The baroness wore a dreamy expression, clearly infatuated.  

 

Thanks to Elwood’s proximity to Windburg, she had easy access to the cultural offerings of the city. She often attended venues frequented by marquesses and counts, which had led her to believe that, though she was of the lower nobility, no one was closer to high society than she.  

 

She even spoke with members of the Glen family, albeit distant relatives. To her, Glen was Glen.  

 

While her vanity sometimes exceeded her refinement, the baroness had always shown care for her late sister Margaret’s children. This made her a figure Roselyn regarded with a certain gratitude.  

 

“But tell me, are you planning to become a nun? You’ll be twenty-three soon.”  

 

“I turned twenty-four last month.”  

 

“Twenty-four! My goodness, what on earth is your father doing? Leaving his daughter unmarried at this age!”  

 

The baroness’s face was one of utter disbelief, as though the sky had fallen. Was being twenty-four and unmarried truly so catastrophic? Roselyn endured her aunt’s agitated scrutiny with a faint, noncommittal smile.  

 

“Well, it’s not the end yet. Just don’t let yourself turn twenty-five without marrying. I can’t bear the thought of Margaret’s eldest daughter becoming a second-choice bride.”  

 

Her words were harsh, but Roselyn knew they came from a desire to shock her into action. She simply smiled in response.  

 

It wasn’t as though she hadn’t had suitors. As the daughter of a viscount, with a family estate and sizable farm, Roselyn had her share of proposals. Her father, Alfred, had also been savvy with investments, ensuring a generous dowry for his eldest daughter.  

 

But Roselyn had remained unmarried because of a promise to her mother.  

 

“From now on, you’ll take my place.”  

 

Roselyn still vividly remembered her mother’s pale face, her lips cracked and blue from a long and grueling labor. The desperate grip of her cold, clammy hands as she clutched Roselyn’s tightly was a memory that had never faded.  

 

“Take care of your father and your siblings.”

 

The grotesquely swollen belly. The metallic scent of blood filling the birthing room. The hot, stifling stench of death.

 

“Rose, my sweet daughter. Will you promise me?”

 

Roselyn had been eleven years old then, just moments away from losing her mother.

 

“Yes, Mother. I promise. Please don’t worry.”

 

She had made that promise, holding her mother’s damp hands tightly with her small ones. Roselyn still remembered Margaret’s faint smile; a mixture of sorrow, relief, and countless emotions she couldn’t name, along with the look in her eyes as they gazed at her daughter.

 

Recalling it all, Roselyn shook her head.

 

“Andy is still so young. He may be growing older, but he’s not mature enough yet. Until he turns eighteen, I—”

“Enough, child! You need to think of your own situation. I had my second child by the time I was twenty-four.”

 

“You’re absolutely right, Aunt. I’m already late, and another year or two won’t make much of a difference now.”

 

Roselyn’s calm rebuttal was accompanied by a faint lowering of her gaze.

 

She had taken over as the lady of Fairfield House in her mother’s stead for thirteen years. During that time, she had found various excuses to turn away suitors. She had cited their family’s standing, reputation, or finances, and when those reasons weren’t sufficient, she would express dissatisfaction with their appearance, even though she didn’t truly care about such things.

 

To Roselyn, a gentleman’s appearance ranked last among the many virtues he should possess. Physical appearance was like clothing—sufficient if it met the standards of propriety. It was nice if it was impressive, but in the end, it was just clothing, not the essence of the man himself.

 

“And your father, poor man that he is, ignoring his daughter’s age while contemplating remarriage for himself,” the baroness tutted softly, clicking her tongue.

 

Roselyn pushed aside her thoughts and refocused on her guest.

“Is it true that some woman named Selma or Sally is coming to the memorial dinner?”  

 

“Yes, it’s true.”  

 

“Oh, for heaven’s sake.”  

 

“It’s the 13th anniversary, Aunt. Father has been alone for long enough.”  

 

“I’m not criticizing that. A viscount certainly has the right to remarry. But that woman…”  

 

The baroness paused briefly, studying her niece. In that fleeting moment, Roselyn felt an unexpected sense of camaraderie with her.  

 

“Selma is just… an ordinary person.”  

 

Someone with no titled relatives, no direct noble lineage within six degrees. Just an ordinary person.  

 

Times had changed. It was no longer unusual for commoners to become famous or wealthy. Even so, many of them still sought to serve the crown, earn knighthood, or marry a noble spouse to enter high society. For Roselyn, it was proof that certain values still couldn’t be bought, no matter how much wealth one had.  

 

Some might call it an outdated sense of class or a relic of the past, but as the eldest daughter of the Viscount of Andover, Roselyn couldn’t ignore the importance of status. The Viscountess of Andover would be the mistress of Fairfield House, the one to succeed her mother. Was it really so unreasonable to hope for a lady of dignity and grace in that role?  

 

“Anyway, that’s for the viscount to decide. I’m more concerned about your future. From now on, I’ll take charge of you.”  

 

“You’ll be my chaperone?”  

 

“Who else? The viscountess is no longer with us, and your aunt lives too far away.”  

 

Roselyn’s maternal aunt, Liana, lived in her husband’s estate in the central region after marrying into another noble family. The baroness, who had successfully arranged marriages for her two daughters, was the most suitable candidate to act as her chaperone. After all, Roselyn would need one to navigate the marriage market as a noble lady. Sooner or later, she too would need to find a husband.  

 

“I’ll find the perfect social gatherings to bring you to. And I’ll be very particular, you can be sure of that. My dear Roselyn, you’re such a fine young lady. Beautiful face, graceful figure, healthy, and well-mannered. You could even become a duchess.”  

 

“Thank you for your high praise and for offering to be my chaperone. But please don’t rush, Aunt. As I’ve said, I’d like to stay here for another year or two.”  

 

“Take this as advice from someone with experience: think of your father.”  

 

The baroness’s voice turned gentle yet insistent.  

 

“Men lose all sense when they’re in love. They become reckless, desperate to stay close to the woman they desire, no matter their age.”  

 

For a fleeting moment, Roselyn felt a tight squeeze in her chest.  

 

“Alfred is probably hoping you’ll leave Andover soon. He’s too soft-hearted to say it outright, but no one knows better than he does how much you’ve kept this household running.”  

 

The conversation ended there, and for a while, the two women were lost in their own thoughts. Eventually, the baroness broke the silence.  

 

“I should be going now. Missing the show would be dreadful.”  

 

As she stood up, Roselyn followed suit, personally helping her aunt with her coat to show her gratitude. The baroness responded by affectionately patting her shoulder.  

 

“You should get into the habit of going out more, dear. Go to a play, a dressmaker’s shop, or even a department store. How do you amuse yourself in this backwater place? It’s less than an hour by train to Windburg.”  

 

“Thank you for the advice. I’ll keep it in mind.”  

 

“Such polite words.”  

 

The baroness cast her a teasing glance before making a firm suggestion.  

 

“Let’s set a date now. Come out with me tomorrow afternoon.”  

 

“Tomorrow?”  

 

“Why so shocked? Weren’t you just going to spend another day sewing and playing the piano?”  

 

Roselyn kept her mouth shut. Besides embroidery and piano, she also read novels and tended the garden, but mentioning those would only invite more scolding.  

 

“Let’s visit the department store and stop by a café. Have you ever tried coffee?”  

 

“I prefer tea, Aunt.”  

 

“Listen to this old-fashioned girl! Even noble ladies drink coffee nowadays. You shouldn’t isolate yourself too much from trends, Rose.”  

 

The baroness clicked her tongue and chuckled, though her expression betrayed a hint of pride. Elderly nobles tended to favor young women like Roselyn; those who upheld traditional values were inherently endearing.  

 

“See you at Windburg Station tomorrow at 2 p.m. You know how to take a train, don’t you?”  

 

“Aunt.”  

 

“I’m joking.”  

 

The baroness laughed lightly, and Roselyn joined her. The older woman gazed into her niece’s gray eyes, noting their calm steadiness, unmarred by any youthful frivolity.  

 

“Don’t forget, 2 p.m. See you tomorrow.”  

 

“Yes. Enjoy your evening.”  

 

“Let’s go to the opera next time.”  

 

“I’d love that.”  

 

With perfect manners and just the right amount of warmth, Roselyn once again saw off a guest at Fairfield House with grace.  

 

To meet her aunt at 2 p.m., she’d need to leave around 1. Contrary to her aunt’s assumptions, Roselyn was no stranger to Windburg, frequently visiting for sewing supplies, books, or exhibitions and concerts with her younger siblings. She merely refrained from frequent trips to avoid overstimulating her excitable sister, Andreana.  

 

After mentally organizing her schedule for the next day, Roselyn checked the time. Her father and Victor would soon return from inspecting the farm, and there were less than 30 minutes until dinner.  

 

 

 

“I’m thinking of hosting a ball.”  

 

The Duke’s sudden declaration silenced the room. Even the servants holding trays of food seemed to freeze. Victoria, holding her cutlery, stared at the head of the table.  

 

“It’s an old-fashioned idea, I know, but so is the institution of marriage itself. I figured a traditional approach would suit the occasion.”  

 

His tone was serious, yet tinged with his usual sarcasm, leaving Victoria uncertain about his sincerity.  

 

“As a means of finding a bride, this is the best I can come up with. If anyone has a more modern suggestion, feel free to share.”  

 

With that, he resumed cutting his food and eating. As his green eyes scanned the room, they met Victoria’s, and she suddenly realized—he was serious.  

 

He truly intended to host a ball at Maxville to find a bride. A so-called Duchess Selection Ball.  

 

It was like something out of a fairy tale, and Victoria was stunned. A gathering of women aspiring to become the duchess? What, was he expecting to find someone wearing a glass slipper?  

 

What on earth is going on?  

 

“That’s a wonderful idea.”  

 

Victoria turned her head in disbelief. It was the dowager duchess who had spoken.  

 

“When were you thinking of holding it?”  

 

“The sooner, the better. If I want an heir, I need to marry as soon as possible.”  

 

“True.”  

 

Lady Diane Glen nodded, placing her cutlery on her plate. She reached for her wine glass, taking a slow sip without a trace of excitement or joy. This was the declaration she had waited years for—a promise to marry after four long years as Duke.  

 

“But we can’t hold the ball immediately. The ladies will need time to prepare. A woman is at her most beautiful in late spring. Let’s make it a grand affair. How about May?”  

 

“Do as you see fit. I can’t spare the time to worry about it.”  

 

“Understood. I’ll handle it.”  

 

“Fine.”  

 

And just like that, the Duchess Selection Ball was approved.  

 

Victoria was left speechless at how quickly everything unfolded. She exchanged a glance with her husband across the table, but there was little he could do. As the Duke’s distant cousins, they had no authority to intervene in Glen family matters.  

 

What on earth is happening?  

 

As unease settled in her stomach, Victoria endured the rest of the meal, silently blaming her overly tightened corset for her discomfort. 

The dinner concluded relatively quickly. With only four attendees and no one particularly eager to carry the conversation, the atmosphere remained subdued. Once again, it fell to the dutiful nephew-in-law to ensure the table didn’t lapse into complete awkwardness by tirelessly introducing new topics.  

 

Count Wadsworth of Benford knew the contentious history between the Dowager Duchess and her son better than most. As with any family conflict, the details were intricate. In summary:  

 

When the Duke unexpectedly inherited his title three years ago, he had a lover—a woman with several disqualifying traits beyond her commoner background. While she might have been accepted as the Duke’s mistress, she could never be his Duchess. It was fortunate, at least, that the relationship ended before an engagement could be announced.  

 

But last month, the Duke reunited with this former lover. It was widely presumed that he had proposed to her again. This, in turn, likely explained why he had spent nearly a month in the capital, only to return home alone.  

 

And now, at their first shared meal since his return, he had dropped a bombshell: he intended to host a ball to find a bride.  

 

“A ball? That’s such a self-centered approach, isn’t it?”  

 

Victoria’s sharp tone directed at the Duke made Wadsworth long for a cigarette. Yet asking to retire to the smoking room would only earn him a glare from his wife—smoking rooms being the exclusive domain of gentlemen.  

 

Thus, the Count and Countess remained at the empty dining table with the Duke long after the meal had ended.  

 

“If you want to meet women, you should be more active in socializing. As it stands, you barely show your face at the imperial court’s annual banquet. Where do you expect to meet anyone?”  

 

“That’s precisely why I’m hosting a ball, Countess.”  

 

“It’s so crude, so blatant. You could easily meet ladies naturally if you put in a little effort. Summoning them to present themselves before you is just arrogant. It’s disgustingly arrogant.”  

 

“Oh dear, Count, it must be exhausting living with such a fiery, progressive wife,” the Duke remarked with a faint smile, lifting his whiskey glass. The Count, responding with a matching smile, picked up his own glass. 

 

Without taking a sip, the Duke continued, “If you’re worried that I’m playing the role of a lion surrounded by lionesses, rest assured. I’ll invite an equal number of gentlemen. When you bring together a room full of eligible bachelors and bachelorettes, sparks are bound to fly. I guarantee at least five marriages will result from this ball.”  

 

“So, the Duke is planning to play matchmaker?”  

 

“Let’s call it a contribution to the prosperity of northern society.”  

 

“How noble. Truly befitting the King of the North.”  

 

“I’ll take that as a compliment.”  

 

“From railroad king to matchmaking king, a triple crown.”  

 

“The more crowns, the better.”  

 

The Duke laughed, his composure unshaken, and took a sip of whiskey. On the surface, Duke Windburg appeared as calm and unbothered as ever.  

 

But the Count knew the truth: Jared’s heart had been broken.  

 

The urgency with which Jared sought a suitable bride and prepared for marriage stemmed from that heartbreak. Four years had passed since the accident that thrust him into the role of Duke, and there was no longer any excuse to delay his responsibilities.  

 

His first love had failed utterly and irrevocably.  

 

“Jared, there’s no need to rush,” Victoria said gently, her tone shifting. She now spoke as though addressing a younger cousin, with subtle pity and affection.  

 

Jared, the youngest among all their extended cousins, was ten years Victoria’s junior. As the baby of the family, he had grown up with the independence, adventurousness, and slight rebelliousness typical of younger sons excluded from succession. He had been a man who looked at aristocratic society with cynicism, who sneered at its norms and sought to escape its confines.  

 

That was before he became Duke, before he ceased to be “just Jared Glen.”  

 

Wat, observing his wife’s change in tone, wondered how Jared might respond. The Duke, holding his glass with steady hands, tilted his gaze toward Victoria and answered in the same vein.  

 

“I have to rush, Victoria.”  

 

“Why?”  

 

“Why ask what you already know?”  

 

The corner of his mouth twitched in a wry smile, perhaps mockery, perhaps self-deprecation.  

 

“I need an heir. The sooner, the better. What other reason could there be?”  

 

Behind the Duke’s mild expression lay an impenetrable wall. Wat could tell that his wife saw it too, evident in the silent exchange of glances between her and the Duke.  

 

“I’m thirty now. I can’t delay any longer.”  

 

His voice was low as he lowered his gaze. Under the shadow of his lashes, his green eyes blinked slowly.  

 

“I’ve indulged myself long enough. It’s time to face reality.”  

 

The murmured words seemed directed more at himself than anyone else. He didn’t seem to care whether anyone responded. Instead, he turned the crystal glass in his hand, its smooth surface catching the light.  

 

For a moment, silence reigned. Wat, uncertain of what to say, remained quiet. His wife hesitated, but ultimately said nothing either.  

 

The Duke broke the stillness.  

 

“I have a favor to ask, Victoria.”

  

“Anything. What is it?”  

 

“May I ask your daughters, Elma and Alice, to be flower girls for the wedding? It would mean a lot to me.”  

 

Looking up at his cousin, Jared’s gaze was steady. Victoria inhaled deeply before meeting his eyes. She hesitated, and Wat knew she wouldn’t voice her lingering questions. What could she possibly say at this point?  

 

“They’d be delighted, of course.”  

 

“That’s a relief.”  

 

The Duke nodded, a faint smile playing on his lips.  

 

“The flower girls are decided. Now all that’s left is to find a bride.”  

 

He said this with an air of nonchalance, his face giving nothing away. Wat and Victoria, however, couldn’t even muster a polite laugh.  

 

And so Wat knew: the subject would never be broached again. Jared had sealed his past away, and no one would be permitted to uncover it.  

 

Jared Glen had accepted his fate. Or perhaps, that fate had been sealed the day he became Duke. He was already the Duke of Windburg, the King of the North, and the master of the grand, historic Maxville estate.  

 

Whether he wanted it or not, it was already so.  

 



 

When Roselyn stepped off the train, the baron’s carriage was already waiting. As she exited the station, the coachman greeted her and led her to the carriage, where the baroness welcomed her warmly.  

 

“Right on time, my dear.”  

 

“You arrived early, Aunt. I hope you weren’t waiting long.”  

 

“Not at all. No need to worry.”  

 

With a bright smile, Roselyn took a seat across from the baroness. The warming weather made her winter coat feel slightly heavy.  

 

“The weather is lovely. Spring must be on its way.”  

 

“Spring is already here, dear. Just look at you.”  

 

The baroness, eyes sparkling with delight, appraised Roselyn’s attire approvingly.  

 

Roselyn wore a woolen winter dress with a high collar adorned with frills and a brooch—an heirloom from her late mother, decorated with pearls and a cameo. She had accessorized with gloves and a felt hat, the latter trimmed with silk ribbons and egret feathers. Her soft leather gloves bore a simple monogram of her initials. Though she wasn’t particularly fashion-conscious, she valued looking presentable and had a natural flair for it.  

 

“You look even lovelier in person. So elegant.”  

 

“Thank you. I’m glad I don’t look too provincial.”  

 

“Is that a jab at me?”  

 

“Of course not, Aunt. I don’t take your comments to heart. Besides, Andover is hardly a backwater.”  

 

“Oh, how gracious of you, Viscountess-to-be.”  

 

The baroness rolled her eyes playfully, though her smile lingered. Roselyn returned the smile, her charm effortlessly offsetting her bold words. Like most noble daughters, she took pride in her heritage and held her head high as the daughter of a viscount.  

 

The carriage departed the station, heading toward the department store. Though it wasn’t a weekend, the streets were bustling—it was, after all, the busiest district in Windburg. But the two women were too engrossed in their conversation to notice.  

 

“Have you read that author’s book? No, of course not. Someone like you wouldn’t. They say it’s even more vulgar than romance novels.”  

 

The baroness grimaced as though she might click her tongue. 

 

Roselyn, meanwhile, simply listened in silence.  

“Erica is absolutely smitten with that writer. Can you imagine her disappointment when she found out it’s a woman? A married woman with children, no less! She’s my daughter, but honestly…”  

 

“That’s so like her. After all, her husband is a writer too.”  

 

“He’s a playwright,” the baroness corrected sharply, drawing a clear line. It was an understandable reaction. Novels, particularly mysteries and romances, were not held in the same regard as refined plays, poetry, or philosophy. In the eyes of the nobility, who valued decorum above all, novels had no place in a proper library.  

 

“As if using a man’s name to publish wasn’t enough… Well, no surprise there, I suppose.”  

 

The baroness’s sigh of disapproval left Roselyn feeling slightly awkward. She couldn’t decide whether to feign ignorance or admit her knowledge of the topic.  

 

The story had made headlines a week ago: a famous thriller author had been revealed to be a woman, and she was allegedly involved with her male publisher. While the author’s popularity had already made her a frequent topic of discussion, the scandal gained extra attention because the man in question was a noble from the distant kingdom of Litton and, more importantly, the emperor’s uncle by marriage.  

 

“A young woman of uncommon nerve, clearly. If the emperor’s uncle is involved, he must be quite old.”  

 

Roselyn had read the article and been equally surprised. Yet she couldn’t help feeling a spark of pride at the revelation that the celebrated writer was a woman. She hoped the scandal wasn’t true, though, as such rumors could irreparably damage a woman’s reputation.  

 

In truth, Roselyn had read two of the author’s books herself. She had devoured them in a single night and then carefully hidden them in a drawer in the greenhouse. It wouldn’t do for Victor or Andrea to discover them; such a revelation would be disastrous to her image.  

 

Reading novels, particularly thrillers, was a secret indulgence… enjoyable but decidedly unladylike.  

 

“Regardless, there’s no denying the author’s talent. Her books have sold remarkably well. I heard they couldn’t keep them in stock in Litton.”

  

“Hmph. No matter how popular she is, a harlot is still a harlot.”  

 

The baroness’s biting words sent a chill through Roselyn. She knew it was just a metaphor, but the sting of the comment was unavoidable.  

 

The carriage moved past the congested street and finally stopped in front of the department store. Roselyn gazed at the massive building through the window—a structure that seemed to contain every imaginable good. Up close, its sheer size was overwhelming. She tilted her head back to take in its full height, marveling silently.  

 

“Roselyn, dear. Look over there.”  

 

The baroness’s hushed voice brought her attention to a different scene.  

 

“It’s the Duke of Windburg.”  

 

Following her aunt’s gaze, Roselyn spotted a black automobile parked nearby, surrounded by a small group of people. Among them, it wasn’t hard to identify the individual her aunt was pointing out.  

 

How could she not recognize him? His face was often featured in newspapers.  

 

The Duke of Windburg had become well-known three years ago when he unexpectedly inherited his title. Born as the third son of a duke, he had been far removed from succession until a tragic accident left him as the head of the family.  

 

Everyone in the empire knew the story: the Duke had been a lawyer before inheriting the title, and both of his older brothers had died in a sudden accident. As such, Jared L. Windburg was arguably one of the most recognized living nobles.  

 

“Such a handsome man. Look at that jawline and those curls. Isn’t he just so classical?”  

 

Listening to her aunt’s admiring whispers, Roselyn observed the Duke. Their carriage was close enough to provide a clear view while still offering the privacy of concealment.  

 

His ink-black hair curled softly but was neatly groomed. His strong jawline and the subtle cleft in his chin gave him an air of authority. Without the clean-shaven look, he might have seemed intimidating. His features had an undeniably classical beauty, like something out of an old oil painting depicting a prince or knight.  

 

Even his modern attire; perfectly tailored coat, polished shoes, and crisp white shirt, looked regal.  

 

Perhaps Roselyn’s perception was influenced by her knowledge of his status. She couldn’t separate the man from his position as the Duke of Windburg, master of the North in both a traditional and metaphorical sense.  

 

“So he visits the department store too. Well, it is his business.”  

 

The Duke’s enterprises spanned various industries, and this department store was one of them. Roselyn was aware that the Glen family’s influence now extended from steel production to retail.  

 

“Who’s the lady with him? I don’t recognize her.”  

 

“Probably a relative or acquaintance.”  

 

“They seem quite close.”  

 

“A close acquaintance, then.”  

 

Maintaining a neutral tone, Roselyn studied the woman accompanying the Duke. Her demeanor was strikingly natural, as though being escorted by the Duke was a regular occurrence. Her attire was luxurious, and she appeared to be older than him. While it was possible the Duke preferred older women, the dynamic didn’t feel romantic.  

 

Romantic feelings. What would you know about that?  

 

Roselyn chuckled softly at her own foolish thoughts.  

 

“Oh, that must be the Duke’s aide.”  

 

“Aide?”  

 

“Yes, the young man there. Rumor has it…”  

 

The baroness trailed off, hesitating as though she had said too much. After a moment’s pause, she relented.  

 

“Forget I said anything.”  

 

“Please, Aunt. Now I’m curious.”  

 

“Oh, I suppose there’s no harm in sharing.”  

 

Despite her reluctance, the baroness leaned in conspiratorially.  

 

“There’s gossip that the Duke… well, that he’s romantically involved with a man.”  

 

“What?”  

 

“They say he has a… condition that prevents him from being close to women.”  

 

A condition that prevented closeness with women. Roselyn didn’t immediately grasp the implication, but her broad reading; including books deemed inappropriate for young ladies, soon helped her piece it together.  

 

Perhaps it was a physical issue affecting his ability to have children. Or perhaps it was a psychological inclination toward the same gender.  

 

Goodness. Both possibilities were equally shocking, leaving her momentarily speechless.  

 

“It makes sense, doesn’t it? Why else wouldn’t he have married yet? It’s been over three years since he became Duke. Clearly, something’s wrong. Definitely wrong.”  

 

The baroness seemed confident in her deduction, her voice tinged with pity as she glanced at the tall, striking man outside the carriage.  

 

“Such a shame, really. He’s so handsome.”  

 

The air in the carriage grew awkward as the scandalous rumor hung between them. Outside, the Duke remained blissfully unaware of their conversation, let alone the damage it might do to his reputation.  

 

He stood with his mysterious companion and aide, briefly conversing with an older gentleman who seemed to have approached by chance. After a short exchange, the gentleman bowed, the Duke nodded in response, and the trio proceeded toward the department store entrance. His posture, gait, and manners as he escorted the lady were flawless.  

 

Roselyn watched the scene unfold, her gaze lingering on the Duke’s face and his calm green eyes as they regarded his conversation partner. She observed everything through the narrow curtain of the carriage window, stealing glances like a thief.  

 

The Duke, of course, never noticed her. He never so much as glanced her way. He belonged to his world, and Roselyn belonged to hers; a distance that somehow reassured her and emboldened her silent observation.  

 

He was someone completely unrelated to her, someone she would never meet or mix with.  

 

‘Such a shame. He’s so handsome.’

 

Roselyn continued watching until the Duke disappeared into the lavish department store, completely out of sight.  

Since that day, Roselyn has thought of the Duke of Windburg a few more times. There was no great reason or trigger. The morning paper was delivered to Fairfield House every morning, and she always took the third one after her father and brother. This was because the paper occasionally had articles about the Duke.

 

His name was mentioned in the business section, mainly in articles about the Duke’s businesses. There were also articles in the society section about his support for local education and various cultural facilities. His photo was also included. Roselyn had seen it.

 

The photo that the Duke had distributed when he announced his succession to the title three years ago still appeared in the pages from time to time. He was standing under the Glen’s eagle in his ducal regalia. The badges and insignia symbolizing the thirteen titles he held. Roselyn looked closely at the gaze of the man who was standing obliquely and looking out of the screen, his thoroughly aristocratic and indifferent gaze.

 

In the meantime, the days passed and spring deepened. Her seedlings grew quickly and were ready to go out into the world, and the soil in the garden became soft and warm enough. In the lord’s garden, hyacinths and tulips began to bloom, and then irises. It was the season when peony buds were blooming beautifully.

 

And this morning, Roselyn finally decided that it was time to move the seedlings she had carefully grown into the open field.

 

“Sister. Why do I have to do this?”

 

“Gardening is also a lady’s skill. You can learn diligence and sincerity. You can learn compassion while caring for delicate plants.”

 

“Do you really have to learn that?”

 

“Yes. You especially have to learn it.”

 

Roselyn handed her a pair of extra gardening gloves, saying with conviction. Andrea pouted, but didn’t seem to dislike it at all. She accepted the gloves without a word, and she was wearing a straw hat like that even though she hadn’t been asked to.

 

Roselyn smiled and put on her own hat, then put on her gloves and grabbed a trowel. She put on an apron, a woolen dress that she wore when she did gardening, and started moving the pots from the greenhouse to the garden with her sister. The pots were small and close together, so it wasn’t hard work.

 

She didn’t get any help from gardeners or laborers in the garden. Tending this small piece of land was her own accomplishment. Roselyn wanted to harvest on her own. Of course, Andrea’s help wouldn’t be much help, so she left it at that.

 

“You would have been great if you were born a farmer’s daughter.”

 

“Farming is hard work. It’s just a hobby.”

 

“You’re probably the only noble lady who grows vegetables as a hobby. But why spinach, tomatoes, and cucumbers?” 

 

“They’re easy to grow and they taste good. You should grow them with care and eat them deliciously.”

 

“What? Isn’t that too cruel? You’re telling me to learn compassion?”

 

When Andrea was horrified, Roselyn chuckled. She squatted in the garden, her shoes covered in dirt, and giggled like a girl as she faced her sister.

 

The spring sun was warm and the smell of the soil was refreshing. Her sister and the seedlings were all pretty and strong. It was a day when everything was perfect and she couldn’t help but hum.

 

If the unexpected visitor hadn’t shown up, it could have been a perfect spring day.

 

“What are you guys doing here?”

The sisters turned their heads at the same time at the voice behind them.

 

“Aunt? How are you… … . What’s going on here?”

 

“I thought you were going for a walk because you said you were at the sponsorship, but what on earth are you doing here? Did you fire the gardener?”

 

“I’m gardening. I’ll make a vegetable garden for my pastime, and grow some vegetables.”

 

“Vegetables and all, come out now. This is not the time to be doing this.”

 

Baroness Ellwood appeared impertinently, not hiding her impatience. It was very unladylike, and Roselyn knew that something important or urgent had happened that would make her give up her dignity.

 

“There’s a ball at Maxville.”

 

It was really unexpected news, really.

 

“They’re looking for a bride for the Duke. The Duchess of Windburg!”

 

Roselyn blinked at the words that came without warning. She had no idea how to react. All she knew was that the lace parasol her aunt held up was unusually white today.

 

“Did you hear, Sister? Is it true, Aunt? The Duke is getting married?”

 

“Why not? The whole society is in an uproar. We were the only ones who didn’t know.” 

 

“Oh, why this year? I could go the year after next!”

 

“Look at this reckless behavior. Don’t worry. When your sister becomes a Duchess, you’ll have the best matchmaker lined up.”

 

The Baroness and Andrea looked as if they were about to hug each other and exclaim. As if Roselyn had already received the Duke’s proposal.

 

“But my aunt, she…”

 

“Rose. Do you have anything you can wear?”

 

The Baroness cut her off mercilessly. Then, with a look in her eyes that seemed to know what Roselyn had swallowed, she continued, as if telling Roselyn not to open her mouth.

 

“I heard that invitations were sent out last month. Even the ladies who didn’t receive them sent letters and received new ones. I almost got in big trouble when I had a cold for a few weeks and couldn’t go to the gathering.”

 

“Are you feeling unwell? I heard your face looked a little down. Are you fully recovered?”

 

“I am well now. And that is not the point. I heard the news last week and sent a hasty letter to the Duke’s house. I was afraid it might be too late, but fortunately an invitation arrived at our house yesterday.”

 

What a relief, indeed. The lady, who had been boasting, urged her nieces on.

 

“Let’s go in now. Rose, show me your evening gowns. My goodness, the banquet is next week, and I can’t afford to have new ones. What can I do? The other ladies will all be wearing new clothes.”

 

The Baroness spoke with a worried expression, as if she were dying of frustration. But her face was more lively than ever. Roselyn looked at her aunt’s face, full of enthusiasm, which she had never seen before, with a feeling of slight embarrassment.

 

There’s a ball. At Maxville.

 

A banquet to find the Duchess.

 

She thought of the Duke’s face without realizing it. Roselyn thought of the classic chin, jet-black hair, and green eyes.

 

The Duke of Windburg. The man standing in his kingdom, separated from her.

 

Only then did her heart pound a little.

 



 

The history of the Dukedom is said to be about 800 years. It was where the Glenn dynasty of the Yerena kingdom, which ruled the ancient north, built a palace for their queen, and the pillars that were part of that palace still remained within the mansion grounds. Scholars who wanted to see the relic called the ‘first pillar’ sometimes requested to visit the Dukedom.

 

However, that was only the beginning of the mansion, and the entire facility was not that old. The stone building, which followed the architectural style unique to the north, retained its old-fashioned feel, but it also had modern facilities such as gas lights, boilers, and radiators, along with old items such as gold-plated candlesticks and oil lamps.

 

The fusion of tradition and cutting-edge, the harmony of the old and the new, always impressed visitors to the mansion.

 

“Oh my, look at that statue. It’s really wonderful.” 

 

The moment Baroness Elwood entered Maxville, she couldn’t help but be impressed. It was no wonder, because visiting the Duke’s residence was a rare opportunity for lower-class nobles like them. It was something that rarely happened unless you were a prominent politician or a wealthy person.

 

There were over a hundred hereditary noble families in the Empire. There was a clear difference between the lower-class nobles below Viscount and the upper-class nobles above Earl. Furthermore, a Duke was a title equivalent to royalty, and there were only four hereditary Dukes in the Empire, excluding princes. So the experience of visiting Maxville alone made today’s ball worth attending.

 

“Yes, Aunt. I like that gold candlestick.”

 

“Ask His Grace the Duke.”

 

“Shall I? I might ask him for it when I see him.”

 

“I guess I can get it as a wedding present.”

 

The Baroness chimed in with her nephew’s joke. Roselyn also had a hard time suppressing her excitement and thrill.

For three days leading up to the ball, Roselyn had stayed at Elwood Manor under her aunt’s insistence. She had endured milk baths and tea rinses for her complexion and applied expensive Marencian hair oil to enhance her glossy locks. She squeezed herself into her sturdiest corset and donned her finest evening gown.  

 

The pale blue dress she wore was undoubtedly the most beautiful in her wardrobe, and her aunt had left no room for half-measures.  

 

“You must look your absolute best. This is a ball hosted by the Duke. When will you ever have such an opportunity again?”  

 

Even without her aunt’s prodding, Roselyn would have given her utmost effort. While she dared not dream of the miracle of becoming a duchess, the ball would surely be filled with the elite of high society. Who could say if she might cross paths with someone who would change her life?  

 

“Alfred likely wishes for you to leave Andover. He’s just too soft to say it outright.”  

 

Roselyn was fully aware of her future and the expectations placed upon her as a woman. She knew her time was limited and that her destiny would inevitably lead her to marriage. If she was to marry, she wanted the best husband she could find.  

 

A man who would love her and whom she could love in return. Someone she could respect and who would respect her. Together, they would build a harmonious and comforting home.  

 

Thus, today Roselyn had to be her best self—using every resource and effort she could muster.  

 

“At least so far, I don’t see a single girl prettier than you.”

 

“I know how much you adore me, Aunt.”  

 

“This isn’t flattery. There’s no lady here who matches your beauty, elegance, or even your jewelry.”  

 

The baroness surveyed her niece with satisfaction. Her elegantly coiled hair, her ivory shoulders exposed by the gown, and her diamond-studded pearl necklace and earrings; all contributed to a striking appearance. The jewelry, heirlooms passed down from her mother and generations of noblewomen before her, adorned Roselyn like symbols of her lineage.  

 

Though she wore them tonight, they weren’t truly hers. They were family treasures—emblems of the Andover lineage.  

 

“Oh, there’s the Count of Roth’s cousin, Lady Adelaide.”  

 

“You know the southern nobles as well?”  

 

“A few. I’ve had dealings with them before,” the baroness replied, subtly flaunting her connections.  

 

“Oh, and there’s Lady Glen.”  

 

“Glen?”  

 

“Yes, Karina Glen. The Duke’s aunt.”  

 

Roselyn turned her gaze toward where her aunt was indicating, surprised. The Duke’s aunt was a pleasant-looking, plump woman, chatting animatedly with two women her age.  

 

“That lady’s cousin is a naval lieutenant, and he’s rumored to be an excellent young man. Come along; I’ll introduce you.”  

 

The baroness excelled at her role as a chaperone, scanning the grand ballroom to identify acquaintances and arranging introductions for her charge. While she held out hope for the miraculous prospect of Roselyn becoming a duchess, she was realistic enough to know how unlikely it was.  

 

Today, she prayed for luck while diligently setting her sights on more attainable candidates.  

 

“Good evening, Lady Glen.”  

 

The baroness approached naturally, striking up a conversation. Karina Glen, turning her round face toward them, hesitated as though trying to place who they were.  

 

“Baroness Elwood. We met at the art gallery fundraiser at the mayor’s residence last year.” 

 

“Oh, of course, Baroness. My apologies; I remember now.”  

 

Karina smiled broadly in recognition, though the exchange seemed somewhat exaggerated on both sides. Nevertheless, they engaged in cheerful small talk, exchanging introductions. Roselyn soon found herself drawn into the conversation.  

 

“My memory isn’t what it used to be, I’m afraid,” Lady Glen admitted.  

 

“Completely understandable. I’m no different,” the baroness replied graciously.  

 

“You’ve been well, I hope? And your husband?”  

 

“He’s been well, I suppose. Three years in the afterlife must be treating him kindly,” the baroness quipped dryly.  

 

“Oh, my goodness! What is wrong with me today?” Lady Glen exclaimed, mortified.  

 

“Don’t worry, Lady Glen. Reminding a widow of her husband’s death can sometimes bring her joy.”  

 

The women around them burst into laughter, Roselyn included, offering a polite smile. While not the most refined comment, it cleverly defused Lady Glen’s faux pas by lightening the mood.  

 

“Baroness, your graciousness is unmatched. How can I ever apologize for my slip of the tongue?”  

 

Lady Glen waved her hands in a flustered manner, the large sapphire on her finger catching the light.  

 

“Well, perhaps this will do,” she said with a smile. “I’ll introduce your niece to the Duke. Will that suffice to make amends?”  

 

Her smile broadened as her gaze turned to Roselyn.  

 

“Oh, Lady Glen, we would be deeply honored,” the baroness replied.  

 

“Nonsense. It’s my pleasure to introduce such a charming young lady to my nephew. Let’s see… Ah, there he is now!”  

 

As Karina gestured enthusiastically, everyone turned to look. Roselyn’s heart began to pound as the suddenness of the situation overwhelmed her. Even the tightly laced corset felt tighter.  

 

Being introduced to the Duke.  

 

In high society, introductions were everything. According to etiquette, an acquaintance was required to formally introduce two strangers before they could exchange pleasantries. Without a connection, even those invited to the ball might never speak with the Duke.  

 

Most of the ladies here would simply wait, hoping the Duke would notice them and initiate a conversation. Roselyn had been one of those hopefuls. Her aunt’s strategy to approach the Duke’s aunt was both clever and unexpectedly fortunate.  

 

“Come along, this way,” Karina urged, leading the group toward the Duke. Roselyn followed, feeling dazed as her aunt guided her forward.  

 

The Duke of Windburg was dressed in formal black tails, complete with a bow tie. His partner for the evening was a middle-aged woman whom Roselyn immediately recognized as the Dowager Duchess, Diane. Her platinum hair, styled elegantly, and the green eyes identical to her son’s were unmistakable.  

 

Roselyn had never seen hair like that in person; a near-mythical symbol associated with the imperial family. The Dowager Duchess, a cousin of the late emperor, was also a grandaunt to the reigning Emperor Reuben VIII.  

 

“There he is—the Duke of Windburg!”  

 

“The Duke has arrived!”  

 

“Where? Where?”  

 

The room buzzed with excitement as mothers, sisters, and aunts lit up with the opportunity to introduce their daughters, nieces, or themselves. Some considered rushing forward immediately, while others strategized for a more poised approach.  

 

“Jared!”  

 

Karina Glen’s voice cut through the murmurs.  

 

Both the Duke and the Dowager Duchess turned toward them. As the group approached, Roselyn, caught up in the unfolding events, noticed that the Duke paid no attention to her or the others. His gaze remained fixed on Karina.  

 

“Aunt Karina,” he greeted her.  

 

“This is such a splendid ball! Whoever thought of this idea deserves all the praise,” she remarked with a grin.  

 

“Thank you. Uncle?”  

 

“Oh, he’s around somewhere. I couldn’t care less.”  

 

Karina laughed lightly, bowing slightly toward the Dowager Duchess.  

 

“You look stunning, Sister.”  

 

“Thank you, Karina.”  

 

“It’s been so long since I last saw you at a ball, Duchess. You light up the entire room with your presence!”  

 

Unlike Karina’s warm demeanor, the Dowager Duchess maintained a reserved, enigmatic expression. Her cool demeanor made Roselyn slightly uneasy, but Karina seemed unfazed.  

 

“I have someone to introduce to you both. This is Lady Roselyn of Andover, and her chaperone, Baroness Elwood, her aunt.”  

 

Only when Karina completed her introduction did the Duke’s gaze finally shift, settling on Roselyn for the first time.  

The first thing that struck Roselyn about the Duke was his height. He was tall enough to stand out among the crowd filling the ballroom, his broad shoulders and upright posture making him even more noticeable.  

 

Under the bright chandeliers, his skin appeared almost unnaturally pale. Yet, the sharp angles of his jawline and the intensity of his dark brows imbued him with a vitality that kept him from seeming sickly. If anything, without his pristine, almost ethereal complexion, he might have looked untamed—wild, even intimidating to some.  

 

The Duke of Windburg exuded an air of subtle detachment. Despite the faint smile gracing his lips, there was an undeniable chill to his demeanor. His light green eyes, reminiscent of spring, carried an indifferent gaze, as if he were extending politeness merely as a formality. His aloofness, tinged with an arrogance that suggested he wasn’t particularly pleased to be addressed, was unlike anything Roselyn had ever encountered.  

 

It was impossible to discern what he was thinking. For the first time in her life, Roselyn felt the uncomfortable prick of uncertainty, as though she might unwittingly irritate someone of such high status.  

 

And then, with those soft yet cold eyes, the Duke looked directly at her.  

 

“It’s a pleasure to meet you.”  

 

The first words he spoke to her were accompanied by a faint whiff of his cologne—an unfamiliar yet captivating scent. Roselyn took a steady breath, trying to calm herself. Around her, the ballroom sparkled with the warm light of gas lamps, candles, and chandeliers. If she could distinguish between his smile and his faint sneer, the Duke would undoubtedly notice her tension and unease.  

 

That couldn’t happen. A lady had to maintain her composure.  

 

“Roselyn Eleanor Fairfield,” she said, presenting herself with her most elegant smile and a poised demeanor. “It’s an honor to be invited to such a splendid ball, Your Grace.”  

 

She summoned every ounce of grace she had cultivated over years of hosting guests at Fairfield House. For a fleeting moment, she thought she caught a glimmer of interest in his eyes.  

 

“Fairfield. A very graceful name.”  

 

“Thank you, though having more than one name has long since fallen out of fashion.” 

 

“Your father must be a man of refined taste.”  

 

“My father is a remarkable man, Your Grace, but if you assume my two names are a product of tradition, I must correct you. It’s merely the result of a failed compromise.”  

 

“A failed compromise?” the Duke asked, raising a brow.  

 

Roselyn smiled. The story behind her name was always a reliable conversation starter, leaving an impression.  

 

“I’m the eldest daughter, and firstborns often receive special treatment. When I was born, my father insisted on Roselyn, and my mother on Eleanor. Unable to settle on one, they agreed to use both.”  

 

“I see.”  

 

“But in the end, everyone calls me Roselyn, so I believe my father ultimately won.”  

 

“Indeed,” the Duke said, nodding. His expression, however, remained distant, devoid of genuine interest.  

 

“Fascinating,” he remarked, though Roselyn could tell it was a courtesy. The realization made her cheeks flush, and she quickly lowered her gaze. She had overstepped, and now she saw how foolish she’d been.  

 

‘I talked too much. I kept him too long. He has so many other women to meet tonight.’

 

“Enjoy the evening, Lady Roselyn Eleanor,” the Duke said, signaling his intent to move on.  

 

As Roselyn struggled to regain her composure, she looked up to meet his eyes one last time.  

 

‘Lady Roselyn Eleanor.’  

 

It was unusual to use both of her names in this manner. For someone unfamiliar, it was even more extraordinary.  

 

“To honor both of your parents,” he added, offering a faint, almost imperceptible smile. For a brief moment, Roselyn froze, her breath catching. The Duke didn’t hold her gaze for long. With a slight nod, he excused himself and walked away, his mother on his arm.  

 

Roselyn watched as he left, his tall frame and straight posture unmistakable amid the crowd. Nearby, a gentleman approached the Duke, accompanied by a young lady. The two men exchanged pleasantries before the Duke turned to address the woman. It was a process he would repeat countless times that evening.  

 

Would he smile at them the way he had at her?

 

Standing frozen in place, Roselyn exhaled a faint, self-deprecating laugh. Her body felt drained, as though the encounter had sapped all her energy. Her aunt’s excited whispers and effusive praise barely registered in her ears.  

 

‘Lady Roselyn Eleanor.’

 

Her gaze lingered on the Duke’s retreating figure—his inky-black hair, pale nape, and broad shoulders. He was a man who stood out no matter where he went.  

 

Roselyn found herself staring at his back for a long while, her heart still pounding against the constraints of her corset.

 

The more she reflected on the encounter, the more she found it embarrassing. She hadn’t been rude, but she had faltered. She had lost her composure, if only for a moment. For Roselyn Fairfield, it felt like the greatest humiliation of her 24 years.  

 

Why had she behaved that way? 

 

Was it his title that had unnerved her? 

 

Did she fear him as if he might actually devour her?

 

If so, how would she ever handle an audience with the Emperor?  

 

How pitiful.

 

Determined to shake off her shame, Roselyn resumed mingling. She greeted people her aunt introduced, accepted invitations to dance, and made small talk with the eligible men present. The room was full of gentlemen seeking wives, as well as women competing for the Duke’s favor. Everyone was scanning the room, waiting for the first dance to begin.  

 

It was then that murmurs rippled through the crowd. The baroness, quick to assess the situation, sighed and leaned toward Roselyn.  

 

“It’s the Lady Mendel.”  

 

Josephine Hayes.  

 

Roselyn had read about her in the society pages. The youngest daughter of the Duke of Mendel, Josephine had debuted in society earlier that year. Barely of age, she was radiant with youthful beauty and bright, intelligent eyes. Her confidence seemed to shine in her every expression.  

 

“Who would’ve thought she’d be here?” the baroness muttered, deflated.  

 

Roselyn watched as the Duke approached Josephine. The entire ballroom seemed to hold its breath. While disappointment and surprise flickered across many faces, everyone concealed their emotions with practiced elegance.  

 

The Duke spoke to Josephine first, bowing slightly before kissing her hand. She was the only woman he offered such deference to that evening. She became his first and only dance partner of the night.  

 

He danced with no one else.  

 

Though it was improper for a host to favor one guest so blatantly, no one dared criticize him. There were simply too many women in attendance for him to greet or dance with them all. If the Duke were to personally dance with each one, the ball would have to last three days.  

 

“Lady Josephine is quite stunning.”  

 

“I suppose the next Duchess will hail from the central provinces.”  

 

“They make a striking pair, don’t they?”  

 

The room buzzed with resigned murmurs. The Hayes family, part of the political powerhouse Mendel Duchy, had produced two prime ministers and three chairmen of the council. They had intermarried with the Glen family multiple times over generations.  

 

The consensus was clear: the conclusion of this ball seemed all but decided. Roselyn silently agreed. If she were the Duke, she too would choose Josephine as his wife.  

 

“Why go through all this trouble? They could’ve simply arranged the engagement in private,” the baroness grumbled to Roselyn.  

 

“Never mind that. Look around you, so many fine gentlemen are here tonight. If you find a match, we’ll owe the Duke a thank-you note.”  

 

The baroness surveyed the room with satisfaction, nodding at the eligible bachelors. Roselyn managed a small, polite smile in response. Straightening her back and lifting her chin slightly, she resolved to make the most of the evening.  

 

Her aunt was right. This might be the best opportunity of her life. Somewhere in this grand ballroom, her future husband could be waiting.  

 

She could not afford to lose hope or let her disappointment show.  

 

Steadying herself with a deep breath, Roselyn raised her chin and stepped forward, ready to face whatever the evening held.  

The ball was being held in the Great Hall on the west wing of the manor. It was the largest of the estate’s three reception rooms and reserved for grand occasions. The last time it had been used was for the 30th birthday celebration of the previous Duke, Harold Glen.

 

That day, Jared had attended as the youngest brother of the Duke. The Great Hall had been packed with the social elite and prominent figures from the business world. Maxville buzzed with excitement for the new Duke’s era, filled with hope and anticipation.

 

But less than six months later, the unthinkable happened; the young Duke’s life was cut short.

 

“It’s absurd for the eldest to inherit everything just because of birth order. Aren’t we all equally our father’s sons? Primogeniture is an outdated and unfair tradition, and it should end with our generation.”

 

Harold had been adamant about splitting the thirteen titles and a portion of the family’s wealth among his two younger brothers. Despite fierce opposition from the family elders, he stood firm. Yet, while inspecting the barony he intended to pass on to Marcus, the second brother, Harold tragically lost his life in a dynamite explosion at a mine.

 

With Harold survived only by a young daughter, the title naturally passed to Marcus. However, Marcus, too, perished in the accident, leaving the title to the third son, Jared Glen, who had never dreamed of becoming the Duke of Windburg.

 

From that moment, Jared’s life was irrevocably overturned.  

 



 

Jared stood at a second-floor window, gazing down at the ballroom below while taking a deep drag from his cigarette. The expansive Great Hall was alive with the ball in full swing. Guests danced tirelessly, their laughter and chatter filling the air as if the night would never end. Jared, however, looked down at them with an expression of weariness and detachment.

 

The drawing room was empty save for him. Though there were lounges, smoking rooms, and balconies on the lower floors, they were all occupied by guests. Having endured more than two hours of socializing, Jared needed a break. The alcohol he had consumed for the sake of politeness was beginning to take effect.

 

He desperately needed a cigarette.  

 

He lifted the half-burnt stick to his lips again, his cheeks hollowing as he inhaled deeply, his eyes fixed on the lively ballroom below.  

 

This particular drawing room was tucked away in an obscure part of the estate. Anyone unfamiliar with the layout would struggle to find it, so when the door suddenly clicked open, Jared was certain it was someone he knew.

 

And he was right.

 

“What are you doing here?”  

 

Victoria entered, shutting the door behind her. Jared didn’t respond, instead letting his gaze fall to her dark purple frock.

 

“Surveying from above now? Haven’t found her yet?”  

 

Too tired to answer her jibes, Jared merely glanced at her, cigarette in hand. Behind him, the glittering chandeliers of the Great Hall cast a warm glow through the windows.  

 

“The girl in the glass slipper, haven’t you found her yet?”  

 

Her sarcasm brought a faint smirk to his lips. His sharp, rebellious cousin never missed an opportunity to tease him, and Jared liked that about her. It was a trait they shared.

 

“Well, I suspect she’s out there somewhere, but there are far too many women to focus on their shoes.”  

 

“You didn’t try hard enough, Your Grace.”  

 

“Why didn’t you help me look?”  

 

“Why should I? You’re the one who orchestrated this extravagant mess.”  

 

Victoria scoffed, stepping closer. She opened the silver cigarette case on the windowsill and took one for herself. Jared lit her cigarette without a word, the Duke’s signet ring on his finger glinting in the light.  

 

The signet, engraved with the Duke’s crest, was something he always wore. Oddly enough, this habit had only begun recently, since his return to Windburg in March.

 

“Well, you started this, so you might as well finish it properly. Who knows? You might actually find someone extraordinary. I met Watt at a ball, after all.”  

 

“If I could live like the Count and Countess of Benford, I’d count myself lucky. What more could anyone want?”  

 

“True, I’ll admit that.”  

 

Victoria exhaled a wisp of smoke, smiling. She and Watt were known for their marital bliss, a rare and celebrated union among the aristocracy. Jared respected Watt as much as he trusted him.  

 

“Was it love at first sight?” Jared asked.  

 

“For him, perhaps. Not for me.”  

 

“So how did it happen?”  

 

“He was persistent… sending flowers to my house, asking for tea invitations. At first, it was almost comical.”  

 

“Romantic, though.”  

 

“He’s still romantic. He knows how to make a woman happy.”  

 

“That’s admirable.”  

 

“I was lucky, I suppose.”  

 

“But would you have married him if he weren’t the eldest son?”  

 

Victoria hesitated briefly, but not for long. It wasn’t a difficult question—it was the implication behind Jared’s tone that made her pause.

 

“Probably not. I was the best daughter, after all.”  

 

Her quiet admission came with a soft laugh. Jared responded with silence, the two standing side by side at the window, the massive chandeliers of the ballroom glinting behind them as they smoked.

 

Among noble children, there were always two categories: the heir and the spares. The best daughter and the surplus daughters.

 

When Jared had been a spare son, no family had been eager to arrange a match with him. The most beautiful and promising daughters were reserved for the heirs, leaving the spares to vie for lesser prospects.  

 

To be clear, Jared hadn’t been interested in those daughters, either. He had never found noblewomen particularly alluring or imagined building an ideal life with one of them.

 

The woman he had once desired as a lover had not been of noble birth. Perhaps that was part of the attraction.  

 

Jared was certain his disdain for noblewomen wasn’t prejudice but an informed perspective. As a nobleman himself, he knew them better than anyone.

 

Noble daughters were like ornamental birds, raised in strict conditions. Beyond their pleasing appearances and polite manners, they often had little to offer. Lacking independent thought, they were either too naïve or too calculating, content to exist in their comfortable environments. What appeal could such people, male or female, possibly hold?

 

“Well, look at that,” Victoria said, interrupting his thoughts. She gestured toward the ballroom. “Quite the spectacle. All these women gathered for you.”  

 

Glancing down at the swirling dancers and the glowing lights, Jared replied flatly.  

 

“They’re not here for me.”  

 

His expression didn’t change, not even a hint of his usual sardonic smile.  

 

“They’re here for the Duke of Windburg. That’s not me.”  

 

“But you are the Duke of Windburg.”  

 

“Do you think the title is me?”  

 

“What else would it be? Even your name has changed—Jared L. Windburg. It’s been four years.”  

 

“And yet, I wonder if it’s truly me.”  

 

Jared brought the cigarette to his lips, inhaling deeply. The smoke tasted unusually bitter as he exhaled slowly, narrowing his eyes.  

 

He had asked himself this question countless times over the past four years.  

 

‘Is this truly me?’

 

‘Where does “me” begin and end?’

 

As the alcohol began to settle uneasily in his stomach, Jared stubbed out his cigarette in the silver ashtray beside him. The intricate roses engraved on the tray were smeared with black ash, evoking an odd mixture of disgust and satisfaction.  

 

“If you’d married Miss Dale, would it have felt like you?”  

 

The directness of Victoria’s question left him momentarily stunned. She held her cigarette delicately, watching him intently as if daring him to respond.  

 

Jared paused, fingers still pressed against the ashtray. The silence stretched between them, sharp and heavy.

 

Victoria wasn’t one to waste an opportunity.

 

“So, tell me, what happened? Back then, I—”  

 

“As you can see.”  

 

He cut her off, raising his gaze to meet hers. Victoria’s expression remained unflinching, entirely unperturbed by his interruption. She was one of the few who didn’t fear him, which only made him more determined to find a way to end this conversation permanently.

 

What could he say to ensure she never broached the subject again? Jared didn’t want to revisit it, not ever.

 

“It’s merely the result of a failed compromise,” he finally said.

 

Why those particular words escaped him, he couldn’t understand.

“It’s merely the result of a failed compromise.”  

No, in fact, that thought had been circling in his head the whole time.  

‘It’s merely the result of a failed compromise.’  

‘It was nothing more than the result of a failed compromise. Not because I was a pathetic person, not because I was so worthless that I deserved to be abandoned—just because both sides wanted different things. That was the only reason we couldn’t be together.’

Victoria was looking at him with a serious expression. Meeting her gaze, Jared had no choice but to acknowledge it. He found himself comforted against his will. By the words of a stranger. Absurdly enough.  

“I understand curiosity, but asking a rejected man why he was rejected isn’t exactly considerate behavior, Countess.”  

Hoping it would sound like a joke, Jared turned his head toward the window. He looked down at the people dancing in sync with the lively waltz. In that large, round formation, he searched for that woman. She must be among them, eagerly searching for a suitable husband.  

Jared couldn’t clearly recall her face. He didn’t even remember what she was wearing or its color. The only vivid thing was those gray eyes looking up at him. And the name.  

Roselyn.  

Tonight, he had been introduced to countless women. Among the endless names he didn’t even try to remember, he couldn’t understand why that particular name remained in his mind. 

Was it because it was similar? 

Because it was similar.  

Crazy bstard.  

Jared twisted his lips faintly in self-loathing. His gaze, which had been scanning the crowded floor for the face of a woman he couldn’t even remember, turned cold.  

Had he gotten drunk? As if. No one would be this insane while sober.  

“I have a young lady I’d like to introduce to you.”

Karina Glenn was one of the most notorious snobs among the noblewomen he knew. If she associated with a certain type of people, it was obvious what they were like. She must have pushed her aunt into introducing her to him. Maybe she had approached his aunt for that very purpose in the first place.  

“My father is undoubtedly a great man, but if you think he gave me two names for the sake of tradition, I must correct that misunderstanding.”

She likely wasn’t saying that for the first time. It must have been a rehearsed line, prepared in advance to leave a strong impression. In high society, everyone had a few such weapons ready.  

That was the law of this world. Tone of voice, expressions, even the size of one’s smile—all were part of the script. The wittier and more considerate the words, the higher one’s elegance appeared. That was how the score of this game was calculated.  

“Out of respect for both of your parents.”

Jared Glenn was no exception.  

With cold, detached eyes, he looked down at the ballroom. He observed the women who had dressed to perfection, dancing lightly in hopes of becoming a duchess.

Whether it was family pressure or personal ambition, their goal was the same.  

To become the mistress of Maxville. To marry him and share everything he had. That alone was the purpose and meaning of their lives.  

So, they were all the same. Everyone in that hall was equally detestable. And he, who had set up this pathetic scheme as if it were a strategy, was the worst of them all.  

“Now that I’m here, though, it doesn’t seem like such a bad idea.”  

After a long silence, Victoria spoke. She pressed the half-burnt cigarette into the ashtray, put her dress in order, and took a few steps toward the entrance before turning back. Jared remained by the window, unmoving.  

“Aren’t you coming?”  

“Go ahead.”  

“Hurry up and come down. Don’t just hide here after inviting the ladies, that’s rude.”  

With a light scolding, Victoria left the drawing room. Jared knew that all of her actions were out of consideration for him. He knew that she pitied him and was nudging him so he wouldn’t wallow for too long.  

He knew. He knew it all, yet he couldn’t bring himself to go down there again.  

He just wanted to return to his room. To soak in hot water until his skin turned red and then collapse onto his bed. It seemed better to stare at the ceiling all night and fight against sleeplessness. At least then, he wouldn’t feel this miserable.  

“Haah…”  

Leaning against the window, Jared tilted his head back. He exhaled deeply and closed his eyes. The stiff tailcoat squeezing his body, the forced upright posture of a gentleman, he wanted to rip them off.  

He was exhausted. Completely.  

  

The guests at the Great Hall seemed ready to dance all night. There was no end to the people approaching Jared to talk. He had to acknowledge people he barely remembered and be introduced to women he had never met before.  

The ball finally ended well past midnight. Most guests left reluctantly, but some remained. For those without homes in Windburg City or the surrounding areas, the Duke generously provided rooms.  

In the Trissen Empire, it was customary for guests to stay at least a week when visiting someone’s house. This etiquette applied to both the host and the guests, meaning they would be present at tea parties and dinners for the next week.  

Since it was routine, Jared felt no particular way about it. Maxville was always full of guests, and he always made time for them. In truth, that was the main occupation of idle aristocrats; visiting each other and passing time with chatter, admiration, and boasting. So, nothing was different from usual.  

“Lady Josephine is quite diligent. I heard she went horseback riding early this morning. Count Benford praised her riding skills. He apparently ran into her by chance at the stables.” 

 

Listening to his mother’s chatter in his office, Jared tried to keep his dulled mind sharp.  

He had tossed and turned all night and, in the end, took a sleeping pill. Taking the pill always left his head feeling foggy all day, so he usually preferred relying on a glass or two of whiskey. But since he had already drunk last night, that method hadn’t worked. He had no other choice but to take the pill if he wanted to sleep.  

“Why don’t we invite Lady Josephine to lunch?”  

“That’s a good suggestion, but I already have a prior engagement. I need to leave soon.”  

He glanced at the clock on the mantelpiece. To make it to his lunch appointment with the Chairman of the City Council, he had to leave within ten minutes. At least that meant he could escape this tedious conversation.  

“Then let’s invite her to the banquet instead, along with the other guests.”  

“Will you seat her at the guest of honor table?”  

“If you wish, Mother.”  

He replied smoothly with a gentlemanly smile.  

The Hayes family, the ducal house of Mendel, was politically prominent, holding key positions in the legal system. Jared had known several of them. One of his professors in law school had been a Hayes, and a presiding judge at the Isen High Court, where he had worked as a junior attorney, was also a Hayes. The current Duke of Mendel, Maximilian Hayes, was the Speaker of the aristocratic Senate and the leader of the Conservative Party. His youngest daughter’s invitation had been purely his mother’s decision.  

“I’ve narrowed it down to the five most outstanding young ladies. I plan to invite them for tea so we can talk more.”  

“That sounds like a good idea, but leave Lady Josephine out.”  

“What do you mean?”  

Diane Glenn looked at him in confusion. Jared responded with an exasperated expression.  

“Lady Josephine Hayes has just come of age. Surely, you haven’t forgotten my own age.”  

“What’s wrong with being twelve years older than your bride? I’ve seen couples with twice that age gap.”  

At his mother’s shamelessness, Jared couldn’t even laugh. He had no intention of marrying an eighteen-year-old. More than that, the biggest issue was that she was the Duke of Mendel’s daughter.

“Maximilian, the Duke, is an excellent man. He will be of great help to you.”  

“There’s nothing beneficial about getting deeply entangled in politics.”  

“In times like these, nobles should stand together. I may not know much about the affairs of the parliament, but I’ve heard things are difficult in many ways. You are too indifferent to politics. Your father was very active in the Senate.”  

“Things are different now. If the Duke of Windburg is branded as a conservative, the public won’t take kindly to it.”  

“My dear, who cares what the public thinks?”  

“As the owner of department stores and hotels, I can’t afford to ignore it.”  

Even as he replied firmly, Jared maintained his smile. Becoming the son-in-law of the Duke of Mendel would restrict his political maneuverability. He would be unable to even approach the progressive faction.  

‘Pass the family law amendment. Use all your influence to make it happen.’  

Jared had a debt to repay.  

‘Duke of Windburg. That is the price of our deal.’

His creditor was the kind of person who would stop at nothing to claim what was owed. The family law amendment demanded by the creditor was a progressive bill. It was already bound to be a difficult battle, and Jared couldn’t afford to add another shackle by becoming the son-in-law of the leader of the Conservative Party.  

“What does the imperial family think about this, Mother?”  

“The imperial family?”  

“I doubt that a union between Windburg and Mendel would please Her Majesty the Empress.”  

“Has the Empress given you any indication?”  

“An indication?”  

“Regarding your marriage. Has she shown a preference for a particular lady?”  

“I haven’t received such an honor, but she has taken a great interest in my marriage.”  

“Of course. Pervrante has always had an interest in the Glen family.”  

“Even more so now. After all, someone highly respected by the imperial family currently holds the position of First Lady.”  

“Flattery…”  

Diane Glen glanced at her son with mild reproach, but she didn’t seem displeased.  

She was a woman with a strong sense of pride in her royal lineage. She knew that the current Empress wielded invisible influence over the political world and that she was far from aligned with the conservatives. Moreover, a marriage alliance between the two ducal families, two leading forces in politics and economics, was unlikely to be welcomed by the imperial family.  

The Glen family had always had a delicate, uneasy relationship with the imperial household. This quiet animosity, rooted in historical reasons, had persisted for generations and had become a sort of family tradition. Even though there had been duchesses who bore the imperial surname, that tradition had never been overturned. Like his father and brother before him, Jared was continuing that legacy.  

It wasn’t a bad tradition. Frequent trips to the capital were tedious, and he had no desire to get closer to the imperial family anyway.  

“I understand your stance. In that case, the young lady must be excluded.”  

“Thank you.”  

“That leaves an empty spot at the tea party.”  

“That’s fortunate. With fewer candidates, your decision will be easier.”  

“That won’t do. The number of guests must be odd. That way, when they pair up for a stroll, no one is left out.”  

Diane Glen muttered to herself as she reviewed the list of attendees. At the same time, Jared glanced at the clock above the fireplace. 11:45 AM. It was time for him to leave the mansion.  

He rose from his seat and walked toward the coat rack. Taking down a neatly brushed frock coat, he put it on and fastened a single button. Then he turned back to his mother.  

She remained seated on the couch, absorbed in the list. It was none of his concern. He had already decided to leave the selection of the duchess to her.  

Whether the tea party served as an interview, and whether there were four or five candidates present, it didn’t matter to Jared. If his mother was satisfied with her choice of daughter-in-law, there would be no conflicts between them, which was ultimately in his favor.  

However, at that moment, a name surfaced in his mind—one he couldn’t ignore.  

‘I have a young lady I’d like to introduce to you.’  

Her father’s title was likely that of a viscount. But he couldn’t remember the name of the estate. In his foggy mind, only the woman’s name remained. And those gray eyes.  

“Fairfield.”  

Jared made his decision the moment he uttered the name. Choosing the duchess was his mother’s prerogative. If it didn’t matter who filled the empty seat, there was no harm in placing that woman there.  

“Lady Roselyn Eleanor Fairfield. She should be on the list.”  

Hearing her son’s precise pronunciation, Diane lifted her head. She studied his expression, which was purely businesslike, then lowered her gaze back to the list and searched for the name.  

“The daughter of Viscount of Andover?”  

Andover. Jared repeated the name in his head before giving a small nod.  

“Yes. Let’s place her in the countess’ seat.”  

Diane observed her impeccably dressed son from a distance. Then, with a composed expression, she gave a nod.  

“Very well.”  

She continued in an indifferent tone.  

“Shall I set a place for you as well?”  

“No. My presence would only make everyone uncomfortable. Please make your choice freely.”  

“That’s not right. She’ll be your wife.”  

“She’ll be your successor.”  

Jared smiled. Diane Glen’s expression stiffened, and she remained silent. They stood apart, about five paces away, facing each other.  

The son stood, the mother sat. This familiar discomfort no longer bothered them.  

“I’ll be heading out now.”  

“Yes, go on.”  

Jared left the office, leaving his mother alone. The attendant waiting outside the door naturally followed him.  

Hoping to avoid running into any guests and exchanging pleasantries, Jared descended the staircase at a slightly faster pace than usual.  

‘Let’s place her in the countess’ seat.’  

Josephine Hayes was the daughter of a grand duke. Simply bypassing her in favor of another would elevate the chosen woman’s reputation.  

Final selection as one of the five candidates for duchess, such a title would prove useful in the marriage market, helping her secure a better husband. This, in turn, meant that Jared had repaid his debt.  

‘It was merely the result of a failed compromise.’  

He had no particular feelings toward Lady Andover, neither fondness nor aversion. However, it was true that, in some way, she had given him comfort.  

If he had received something, it was only right to return the favor. Jared Glen was not the type to remain indebted to anyone.  

With that thought settled, he crossed the main hall on the first floor. The sound of his shoes striking the stone floor echoed crisply.  

The grand hall, an architectural masterpiece of Maxville, had a towering ceiling reminiscent of an ancient cathedral. Among its columns, arches, and intricate statues, the sound of the master’s footsteps faded.  

He left the mansion without encountering a single person. Upon reaching the front entrance, the waiting chauffeur opened the car door for him.  

With the duke and two attendants aboard, the automobile departed without delay, heading toward the city hotel where his appointment awaited.  

  

“Maxville is enormous, isn’t it? I heard the estate alone spans over a hundred acres.”  

Listening to Lady Selma’s words, Roselyn lifted her teacup to her lips.  

Annie Selma had been visiting frequently ever since attending Margaret’s memorial banquet two months ago. The Viscount of Andover, using various pretexts, had invited his “dearest friend” to Fairfield House once again. Today, the excuse was to teach her horseback riding.  

Roselyn couldn’t help but despair upon realizing that Lady Selma didn’t know how to ride a horse. If she hadn’t learned riding, she probably didn’t know ballroom dancing or piano either. Did she at least understand the rules of chess and tennis? Those were the only things she could hope to teach quickly.  

If she was completely unskilled, how would she manage to engage in social activities as a viscountess?  

Regardless of Roselyn’s silent despair over her potential stepmother’s lack of refinement, Viscount Andover was fully engrossed in conversation with his beloved.  

“It’s actually closer to 130 acres. If you ride along the outer wall of the mansion and gardens, it takes about thirty minutes on horseback. Since Maxville isn’t open to the public, it’s understandable that you were misinformed.”  

“And how do you know that, Viscount? You’ve never been to Maxville, have you?”  

“You underestimate me, Lady Selma.”  

“Then you have been to the ducal residence?”  

“Of course. Back in the days of the former Duke Philip. I was quite a fine young man back then.”  

“You still seem youthful, my lord.”  

“I know it’s flattery, but it’s pleasant to hear.”  

Roselyn was astonished to see her father behaving like a lovestruck boy, boasting in front of a woman he fancied, while the naive lady listened with wide-eyed admiration.  

Middle-aged lovers were engaging in the same antics as youthful sweethearts.  

Whether Roselyn found it amusing or exasperating was of no consequence to them.

The three of them were having tea in the drawing room on the first floor. However, it was only a matter of time before Lady Selma pushed her way upstairs into the family quarters. No matter how she thought about it, it would be best for everyone if Roselyn left Andover before that happened.  

There was no place for her in Fairfield House anymore.  

Her younger brothers had grown up and no longer needed their sister’s care. Her younger sister would miss her for a while, but she was no longer at an age to cry and crawl into her bed at night like before.  

Their father, who had relied on his eldest daughter to manage the household, was eagerly anticipating the arrival of his new viscountess. Roselyn had no intention of engaging in a power struggle over household affairs with the new mistress of the manor.  

So, there was no place for her anymore.  

That thought left her feeling lonely. At times, it filled her with a sense of urgency and unease; an overwhelming need to leave this house as soon as possible. She did worry about Andover’s reputation and dignity in her absence, but if choosing Lady Selma was her father’s decision, there was nothing she could do about it.  

“Tell me about the ball, Lady Fairfield. What kind of man is the Duke of Windburg?”  

Lady Selma turned the conversation back to her. Roselyn pushed aside her thoughts, lowered her teacup to her chest, and smiled naturally.

“Well, I don’t suppose I can say anything definitive about someone with whom I exchanged barely three minutes of conversation.”  

“Three minutes, you say? Then you didn’t even dance with the Duke?”  

“Alas, that stroke of luck wasn’t mine, Mrs. Selma. There were many other guests the Duke had to attend to besides me.”

She made no mention that merely having exchanged three minutes with the Duke was in itself a considerable good fortune. Roselyn never cared for attracting undue praise or admiration.

“Oh dear, what a pity. Had you danced together, the two of you might have fallen in love. That’s always how it goes in the novels I’ve read. Dancing together is simply so romantic, isn’t it?”  

“In that case, I suppose I must learn to dance soon. By the way, in social dancing the partner is of utmost importance.”  

“Are you a good partner yourself, my lord?”  

“Judging by the remarks I’ve received, I’d say I’m not too bad.”  

“My word, Alfred, there’s nothing you can’t do!”

Thus, the flirtatious repartee resumed, and Roselyn subtly lifted her teacup to her lips.

As an aristocratic lady, she too had her share of interactions with men. Like everyone else, she had made her social debut at the age of eighteen and had attended various gatherings, both grand and modest.

When an exceptionally charming man invited her to dance, her heart would flutter; on such days, even after returning home and lying in bed, she would reminisce about the feel of his large hand or the timbre of his low voice. It wasn’t that these sensations were entirely new, only that they had not been experienced for quite some time.

‘Jared Glen.’  

Though she had told Mrs. Selma it was three minutes, in truth the time Roselyn spent in the Duke’s presence was even briefer, perhaps not even two minutes. And yet, every impression of that evening was entirely connected to the Duke.

Since they hadn’t danced together, there was nothing else to recall, no memory of the touch of his hand, the firm grip around her waist, or the feeling of moving in harmony. That night, Roselyn stole several glances at the Duke from afar, but he, surrounded by company, did not deign to cast her even a fleeting look.

At the ball, Roselyn had conversed with more than twenty men and danced with nine or ten. They were all genteel and courteous, and some were even appealing. Yet, she could scarcely remember their faces or the substance of their conversations, as if the memory of one person had completely eclipsed all the others.

“To honor both of your parents.”  

The Duke of Windburg dominated her thoughts all evening. He had kept her tossing and turning through the night at her aunt’s house after the ball, and even now, two days after returning home, his disregard still haunted her memory.

Come to think of it, this was a first for her too: to be so profoundly affected by someone for such a long time.

Knock, knock. 

The butler opened the drawing room door and entered at that moment.

“Sir, a visitor has arrived from the Duke’s residence at Windburg.”  

“From the Duke’s residence?”  

“Yes. They’ve brought a letter to be delivered to Miss Roselyn.”

At the butler’s words, Alfred Fairfield raised his eyebrows and then, together with Mrs. Selma, turned their gaze toward their daughter’s face. Roselyn could offer no answer to their questioning looks, she was as perplexed as they were.

“Let him in.”  

No sooner had the young lord given his assent than the man waiting outside entered. With measured, deliberate steps, the young and handsome man appeared, smartly attired in a black jacket and white shirt, and carrying a small, oval silver tray.

Glancing briefly at the three men and women seated in the drawing room, he advanced toward Roselyn without hesitation; a valet whose posture, stride, and pace spoke of impeccable training.

“Lady Diane has invited Miss Roselyn Eleanor Fairfield to Maxwell.”  

With graceful formality, he bowed and presented the silver tray. On its gleaming surface lay a white envelope. Roselyn examined its unsealed exterior for a moment before retrieving the letter enclosed and unfolding it.

The contents were straightforward; an invitation to attend a tea party to be held at the mansion in two days. Beneath the brief message was the host’s signature:

Lady Diane El Windburg.

The title “El” was once an honorific bestowed upon the four great lords of the kingdom and is now used as the official title for a duke and his consort. Casting a slightly more admiring glance at the elegant script, Roselyn folded the letter and raised her head.

“Thank you. Please let her know that I shall accept with pleasure.”  

“Of course, Miss.”

After the butler and the Duke’s valet had departed, only the three of them remained in the drawing room. No one spoke until Roselyn had reinserted the invitation into its envelope and placed it beside her teacup on the tea table. It was Viscount Andover who finally broke the curious silence.

“Speak up, Rose. Must I prepare myself to be the father-in-law?”  

Roselyn smiled lightly and replied, “It’s just a tea party, Father.”  

“Then does that mean there will be others in attendance?”  

“A tea party always includes at least three people, meaning that aside from me and the Lady, there is someone else present.”  

“Counting the Duke, that makes three, doesn’t it?”  

“Mrs. Selma, in social events the person of the highest rank is deemed the host. If the Duke were to attend the tea party at Maxwell, it would have been he, not the Lady, who sent the invitation.”  

“Ah, I see.”

Patiently, Roselyn explained the most basic of social courtesies without losing her composure. Though as a future host she had so much to learn, enough, perhaps, that even Andrea might be called upon to teach her, it did not seem an insurmountable burden.

Aside from her disbelief that the Lady had sent her an invitation, Roselyn was well aware of the significance behind it.

“I suppose I must send a telegram to Elwood.”  

“Oh no, Father. The date is far too near, and I don’t wish to trouble my aunt.”  

“So, are you saying you’ll go alone from here to Maxwell?”  

“I can take the train. It’s easy enough to secure a private carriage at Windburg Station.”  

“I really should have bought an automobile after all. Didn’t I mention, Rose? That an automobile would prove useful?”  

“A carriage will suffice, Father.”  

At this, Roselyn burst into laughter.

The Viscount gazed steadily at his daughter’s face. It was not considered proper for a man who had not been invited to accompany the ladies to a social event to appear unannounced. Had his wife been alive, she undoubtedly would have gone along.

“Bring a maid along to attend you.”  

“Of course.”  

Nodding, Roselyn lifted her teacup once more. Though she tried to act unruffled, she could not stifle the rapid beating of her heart.

What shall I wear? Who else might be there? How will the Lady perceive me?  

Why me? 

Among all those ladies, why, among the daughters of dukes, marquises, and earls, was it me, of all people?

With genuine curiosity, Roselyn pondered. But the reasons behind a selection reside only in the mind of the selector, and no matter how much the chosen one ponders, they can never know. All she could think about was what the future might hold, and that, too, was shrouded in mist.

How can one know the future? Pondering it is futile.



When the Lady’s Tea Party began, Jared was in the third-floor study. Even as the women arrived one after another, he paid them no heed whatsoever. This mansion was a place where guests were rarely absent; even now, the guests who had come for the ball were still lingering about.

For three hours, Jared had been poring over a hefty law book. On the desk were piled volumes of the family law code, commentaries, and case reports. Though he had graduated from law school and worked as a lawyer for several years, he knew little about family law.

His father, the 11th Duke of Windburg, Philip Glen, was the principal shareholder of a steel company that oversaw steel mills, shipyards, a railway operating company, and mines. He was also a businessman who had ventured into department stores and hotels in keeping with the times.

In the empire, the second richest man, he had three sons. The eldest, Harold, was designated to inherit the entire fortune. The second, Marcus, had chosen a military career at an early age.

The third, Jared, went to law school and majored in commercial law. Though he enjoyed conversation, he had never been one to engage in arguments, and his reason for becoming a lawyer was simple: he needed to secure a more useful position within his brother’s company.

Younger sons of the nobility must find their own path. As a child, Jared had thought this terribly unfair. But as he grew older, venturing beyond Maxwell, experiencing a larger world, and especially attending university in the capital of Issen, his perspective changed.

“Could there be a greater blessing than being born the third son of a noble family?”  

“Watch it, brother. If anyone hears, they’ll mock poor little brother.”

An heir has no freedom to choose his own life; safeguarding what he inherits is his very existence. He inherits both rights and duties simultaneously, yet even those rights are merely tools for fulfilling his obligations.

“You’ll come to understand soon enough, you’ll share in my burden.”

Jared Glen, the 13th Duke of Windburg, now well understood that indeed.

Knock, knock.

Someone had arrived just as Jared was taking a brief respite, gazing out the window. He had been told not to be disturbed unless something important arose. Hoping it wasn’t bad news, Jared turned his head toward the door. Expecting one of the servants, he was surprised to see none other than Head Butler Sterling.

“I apologize for the interruption, Duke.”

“What is it?”.

“Her Ladyship wishes to see you for a moment.”

Jared nearly furrowed his brow but maintained a dignified expression. He quickly deduced the situation: his mother was having tea with the candidates, and despite his having clearly declined, they’d sent someone to seat him with them. Though he wished to refuse, he had no acceptable excuse. Not wanting to embarrass his mother or stir up any conflict over such a trivial matter, he asked the head butler with a subtly irked yet gentle tone:

“And where might my mother be?”

“She is at the Mermaid Fountain.”

“With the guests?”

“Yes, Duke.”

With that, Jared rose from his chair, draped the frock coat he’d been wearing over his arm on the coat rack, and left the study through the door the butler had opened.

The Mermaid Fountain was located in the rear garden of the main mansion. In the northern clime where Windburg was situated, winters were long; by late October the first snow would fall, and heavy snowstorms were not uncommon until March. Because of such a climate, the front of the mansion was kept neat with expansive lawns and only a few evergreens, since for nearly half the year the vegetation lay in a meager state.

In contrast, the garden at the back was lavishly arranged. The grounds, a canvas for the gardeners’ skill, burst into bloom each spring and summer. Jared, who had always been an average boy more interested in guns than flowers, and whose two elder brothers were much the same, had rarely spent time among these blooms. However, the visiting noble ladies and their daughters always marveled at the garden’s beauty, so it was easy to understand his mother’s choice of this venue for the tea party.

Jared’s gaze wandered to the Mermaid Fountain, clearly visible from his vantage point. Water trickled down from the mermaid statue, and beneath a nearby awning set up on a table, several ladies were seated in a circle. Around them, clusters of flowers in white and soft pink hues were in full bloom, flowers whose names he did not know, for a man who could only distinguish roses from lilies.

“Oh, Jared, you’ve come,” called Diane Glen warmly. At her greeting, the ladies turned their heads in unison and rose gracefully from their seats. Jared approached them and, in a gentlemanly manner, bowed in greeting.

There were six ladies, each curtsying in return. Among them, when he caught sight of Miss Mendel’s face, he sighed inwardly.

It would be best to leave out Miss Mendel.

My dear mother, what ploy is this now?

“As it happens,” Diane continued, “I ran into Miss Josephine. Since the party has grown to seven, one extra guest was needed. They will soon pair off for a walk.”

“I see.”

“Fortunately, I was told you were in the study, so I sent for you. I’m glad you could come.”

“Of course.”

After replying softly, Jared sat down in the empty chair to his mother’s right, right beside Miss Josephine.

“I had been admiring the surroundings by myself, but Her Ladyship kindly included me. I do hope I haven’t caused any inconvenience,” a young lady remarked.

“Oh, not at all, Miss Hayes. Please, don’t worry.”

“Thank you for your kind words, Duke. And by the way, these peonies are simply exquisite. Their fragrance is so delightful that I can scarcely bring myself to leave this vicinity,” she added.

Peonies, yes, that was indeed the name of those blossoms. Jared nodded, as though he already knew.

“I’m pleased that my garden brings you joy.”

“Yet they say the Duke isn’t fond of flowers. I sometimes wonder if you could even tell a tulip from a daffodil.”

“Certainly I can distinguish between roses and lilies, Your Ladyship.”

“Well, aren’t you well-informed!”

The ladies laughed at Diane Glen’s exaggerated praise. With a soft smile, Jared picked up the teacup the servant had just brought him. Now that he was on stage, he had to play his part.

A devoted son, a considerate host, the perfect Duke.

Likewise, the women gathered around the table were all diligently fulfilling their roles.

Elegant ladies, courteous guests, the perfect debutantes.

Jared took a sip of his tea, its temperature just right, and absentmindedly gazed over the rim of his cup. Suddenly, he caught the eye of the woman sitting directly across from him.

Gray eyes.

The moment their gazes met, her face became vividly clear to him. He had believed he’d forgotten it entirely, yet the face that now returned his look was an exact match to the one he remembered.

Roselyn Eleanor Fairfield.

Casting aside the name that immediately sprang to mind, Jared searched his memory. 

What was her father’s title again? And the name of the estate? 

Soon enough, he recalled the appropriate honorific to use.

Lady Andover was dressed in a cream-colored dress today. A lace collar rose elegantly to just below her neck, and a solitary cameo brooch caught the light. Its edges were trimmed with pearls, a piece of jewelry quintessentially befitting a noble lady.

Cameo and pearls.

Elegant, yet predictably conventional.

“By the way, is Miss Fairfield’s name ‘Roselyn’?”  

At the Lady’s remark, the woman averted her gaze. That was when Jared realized that their eye contact had lasted rather longer than he’d intended. He naturally set down the teacup he’d held for too long.

“Miss Roselyn, do you like roses?” 

“Of course, Your Ladyship. I have yet to meet anyone who does not love roses; if you have ever encountered such a case, do tell me.”  

“That would indeed be a rare occurrence,” the Lady replied with a nod and a smile.

Jared felt a faint twinge of disdain as he pulled his lips into a subtle smile.  

“Perhaps the Duke is one of those rare individuals, since he mentioned that he isn’t interested in flowers.”

Miss Josephine took up the conversation. Since she had pointed him out, it was now Jared’s turn to respond.

“Your insight is remarkable, Miss Hayes,” he said, turning his gaze toward Miss Mendel, who sat on his right. The eighteen-year-old’s face shone with curiosity and intelligence, and Jared found himself favoring her innocent charm.

“In truth, I do not like roses,” he confessed.

“See! I guessed it correctly!” Miss Josephine laughed aloud.

Jared joined in the laughter and then turned his head forward. For reasons he could not quite explain, he longed to observe the expression on the face of the woman seated opposite him, perhaps to see if she appeared offended, or maybe to admire how well she concealed her feelings.

As expected, Lady Andover offered an elegant smile. In response, Jared returned a smile that barely concealed a hint of cynicism.

“Who would have thought one could experience such rarity here. Miss Roselyn, in future, you might regale others with this unique experience.”

“Indeed, Your Ladyship. Thanks to you, I now have another anecdote to be remembered at social gatherings.”

“That is always most welcome, a lifelong asset, indeed.”

The ladies laughed again, and the atmosphere at the tea table grew even livelier. The sound of the fountain, the fragrance of peonies, and the gentle May sunlight streaming through the awning combined to create a scene that was bright and beautiful.

Laughter and the scent of perfume floated through the air. Yet, amid it all, the subtle aroma of rosewater seemed oddly out of place. While all the women appeared to be enjoying themselves, it was only Jared who seemed perturbed.

The tea party proceeded smoothly. Her Ladyship, as host, offered cakes and cookies, and the guests partook with delight. As the number of teacups dwindled, a servant began to circulate the table with the teapot. Then a commotion suddenly broke out.

“Ah, I’m terribly sorry, Miss.”

When Jared turned his head, all eyes were on the disturbance. A servant, having spilled tea on Lady Andover’s dress, looked helpless. Calmly, Lady Andover picked up a napkin and, instead of dabbing her own dress, offered it to the servant.

“Is your hand all right?”

“I’m fine, Miss,” the servant replied.

For a moment, a brief silence fell over the assembly.

“Oh dear. Allow me to apologize on her behalf, Miss Fairfield.”

“No need to worry, Your Ladyship, it’s only tea,” she responded, her tone impeccably polite.

“May I be excused for a moment? I believe I need to wash out the stain with water,” she added.

“Of course. Let us return to the main building and use one of the lounges on the first floor.”

“Thank you.”

“Duke Jared, would you kindly escort the lady?” 

 

At her Ladyship’s request, Lady Andover looked momentarily flustered. Observing the brief flash of emotion on her face, Jared said nothing. After all, when a lady is inconvenienced by a servant’s mistake, it is only natural for the host to show consideration. Thus, he willingly accepted his mother’s suggestion.

“Of course.”

As the Duke rose from his seat, the guests at the tea party exchanged awkward glances. Perhaps they secretly wished that tea might have been spilled on their own garments, but such hopes were in vain. The servants at Maxville were hardly adept at properly handling even a single teacup. In a way, the entire commotion seemed almost preordained.

And so, Jared’s curiosity was piqued. The person his mother held in such regard,   

Not the lady in general, but this particular woman.

“Come along, Miss Fairfield.”

He prepared to escort the unfortunate lady, deliberately pretending not to notice the reddish tea stain marring her white skirt. For both the owner of the dress and the Duke himself, the fate of the garment was of little concern.

By now, she must be rejoicing in the good fortune that had finally befallen her.

Until they had left the escort, the Duke did not utter a single word. In keeping with a gentleman’s etiquette, he simply walked on the lady’s left side.

While they walked in silence, Roselyn felt so uneasy that she was on the verge of an upset stomach. The mansion’s grounds were so vast that the main building still appeared rather distant.

She was utterly confused. Everything had been a continuous series of disarray, from the absurd incident of the maid spilling tea on her dress to the situation of the Duke, haughtily escorting her.

No, Roselyn had been on edge ever since the Lady had sent the butler to fetch the Duke. When the Duke appeared and took a seat opposite her, she became so tense that her fingertips turned cold; she had to keep talking to herself just to avoid paying too much attention to him.

‘Don’t be conscious of it. Focus on the other ladies. Just imagine he isn’t even here.’

However, when at last her eyes met his, she could not avoid a shock that took her breath away.

Roselyn couldn’t decipher the meaning behind the look he had been giving her over the rim of his teacup. It seemed either like a chilly sneer or merely an unyielding stare, a look so ambiguous that she couldn’t tell whether it held favor or disfavor.

Those eyes. Green irises that had become almost transparent in the light. The slightly constricted pupils pierced her gaze like a sharp pin.

They kept watching her for a long while. Steadily, they held her gaze, then, almost as if checking her attire, glanced downward before returning to her eyes. It was an overly intense stare bordering on rudeness, yet she couldn’t even think of it as unpleasant. Truly, she felt nothing at all.

“What do you think?” 

Roselyn, who had been walking with a troubled heart while fixating on her footsteps, raised her head. The Duke, having suddenly posed the question, didn’t even look at her; he merely stared straight ahead at the main building, which was gradually drawing nearer.

“What do you mean?”

“I mean this mansion.”

 

He replied, glancing in her direction. Roselyn, conscious of her breathing, met his gaze.

“I was asking whether this place pleases Miss Fairfield.”

It seemed that the Duke had finally begun a conversation, perhaps he had been at a loss for an appropriate topic until now. If that were the case, it was fortunate; it meant he didn’t find spending time with her entirely disagreeable.

Roselyn inwardly breathed a sigh of relief and replied with a modest smile.

“Maxville is the most historic and splendid building in the north. Seeing it in person, it’s far more beautiful than I’d heard, and I can’t imagine anyone not finding it appealing.”

“Miss Fairfield does tend to make snap judgments.”

“…”

“Please don’t misunderstand, I’m not criticizing you. It’s a very common mistake to assume that others think just as you do. It’s not a fault unique to you, Miss Fairfield.”

Roselyn looked at him without showing any sign of embarrassment. Without any hesitation, the Duke continued.

“But in my opinion, there must indeed be someone who doesn’t take a fancy to this mansion.”

So she reached a conclusion: the look in his eyes was not one of favor.

“Just as there are people here who don’t like roses.”

He doesn’t like me. I don’t know why, though.

Roselyn couldn’t think of an appropriate retort, and the Duke made no effort to continue the conversation or introduce another topic. Naturally, the two of them resumed walking in silence, keeping pace with one another. It was a relief that they soon reached the main building.

The mansion’s first-floor main hall boasted an extremely high, majestic ceiling. Even on her second visit, Roselyn couldn’t help but admire its splendid beauty. Although she wondered if anyone could possibly dislike such a place, she was wise enough not to bring up the subject again and risk further provoking the Duke.

Once inside, the Duke walked a little ahead to guide the guests to their destination, allowing Roselyn a glimpse of his back. He seemed entirely indifferent to the beauty of the main hall, which reminded her anew that he was the mansion’s master, a man born and raised here.

The Duke led her to a large door set a little down a right-hand corridor, which, at first glance, appeared no different from the other rooms.

When he opened the door and stepped inside, Roselyn felt a twinge of nervousness. In the long corridor, there was no one else, just the two of them. Entering an enclosed space alone with a man naturally stirred a sense of caution.

But the man was Duke Windburg. Roselyn did not doubt his sterling reputation or gentlemanly manner, and, suppressing her uneasy feelings, she followed him inside.

The lounge was very spacious. Its interior, adorned with marble, gilded mirrors, red velvet chairs, and couches, was splendid. As she cautiously scanned the room, the Duke pointed further inside.

“If you go in, you’ll find a washbasin. I’ll wait here.”

“Thank you.”

“Not at all.”

In true gentlemanly fashion, he sat on a velvet chair, and Roselyn turned to walk in the direction he had indicated. The thought that the Duke might be watching her every move made her tense up; she struggled to ignore the gaze that clung to the back of her neck as she proceeded inside.

In the partitioned area, there was a washbasin and a mirror. Only after closing the door did Roselyn feel she could finally relax. At last, a wall separated her from the Duke. As she stood with her back to the door, an involuntary long sigh escaped her.

“Haa…”

After closing and reopening her eyes for a moment, Roselyn checked her reflection in the mirror. The skirt of her dress, around the upper thighs, was stained with reddish tea water.

This cream-colored lace dress, tailored from her mother’s heirloom to fit her, was one of the garments she cherished most. It absolutely had to be cleaned.

Hurriedly, Roselyn went to the washbasin, turned on the tap, and lifted the hem of her dress. As she diligently tried to remove the stain, she became upset, more so than when she had learned that, besides her, four other women, and even Miss Mendel, who had been invited rather late, had received the Lady’s invitation.

To think she had taken out her cherished dress, ridden the train all the way here, and ended up like this! As she chided herself while trying to remove the stain, Roselyn had no choice but to admit her true feelings.

She had been secretly elated, she had even entertained the foolish notion that being invited to the Lady’s tea party might have been a sign of that son’s favor. After all, no matter how she looked at it, she didn’t possess the qualities to catch the Lady’s eye.

He doesn’t like me, though I don’t know why.

Suppressing her chagrin, she rubbed the stained hem of her skirt with her fingertips. The thought that the Duke was waiting outside made her even more anxious. If he already disapproved of her, then being saddled with such a tedious errand, and possibly making him wait too long, was simply unacceptable.

In her haste, she kept wetting her skirt, and by the time the stain was nearly gone, a much larger area than the tea stain had become soaked, from just below her waist to above her knees. Most of her thighs, too.

“Haa, really…”

Roselyn glanced at the mirror and nervously bit her lips. She tried smoothing the fabric with her palm and dabbing away the moisture with a handkerchief, but of course it was futile. After several attempts, she decided to just leave. After all, it was spilled water, and she couldn’t keep the Duke waiting any longer.

I don’t know, please understand. After all, it was his fault in the first place.

Struggling to overcome her embarrassment and appear composed, Roselyn opened the door and stepped outside.

“Fortunately, the stain is gone.”

Her tone was not only confident but overly bright. The man, who had been sitting calmly, seemed to hesitate for a moment. Though Roselyn felt awkward, she couldn’t take back the words she had already spoken. All she could do was force an awkward smile and let the silence disperse.

“Though it got a little wet, but it will dry quickly.”  

She looked at him with a smiling face, as if nothing were wrong. As though she felt no embarrassment or awkwardness at all.

The Duke sat on the red velvet chair just as he had before. In that same posture, he shifted his gaze back and forth between her face and her dress. The moment his eyes fell on her wet skirt, Roselyn involuntarily tensed her thighs, as if she feared that her bare skin might show through the damp fabric.

“That’s a relief.”  

The Duke replied in a customary manner, his expression unchanged.

“Shall we be off then?”  

As if he had been waiting, he rose from his seat. Roselyn tried hard not to be self-conscious about her wet skirt as she followed him. Leading the way out of the lounge, the Duke held the door for her outside, and as she passed him, she could catch the stronger, more concentrated scent of his perfume.

Then, suddenly, it occurred to her that she had spritzed on rosewater today. After all, if someone doesn’t like roses, they naturally must dislike the scent of roses as well. Hoping that he wouldn’t find her own fragrance objectionable, Roselyn resolved to do her best to keep her distance from him as they walked on.

The path back to the garden seemed even longer than when they’d left. The Duke hadn’t spoken a single word to her the entire time; a behavior that was, without a doubt, discourteous. Consequently, Roselyn had to accept that he truly did not care for her.

 

Should I be the one to start a conversation? she wondered.

 

About two minutes after they had left the main building and begun walking, she seriously considered it. Proper etiquette dictated that the person of higher rank should break the silence first, but judging by the Duke’s current demeanor, it was unlikely he would speak to her before they reached the tea table. Since he had initiated conversation on the way, perhaps he now expected her to make the effort.

 

Roselyn scanned her surroundings for a suitable topic, partly hoping that a bit of conversation might soften the Duke’s apparent dislike for her. To be honest, it felt unfair that he showed no warmth toward her. To steer the conversation smoothly, one must have a knack for choosing a good subject. The best topics are those that pique the other person’s interest, but unfortunately Roselyn had no idea what the Duke cared about. In such a case, the next best option was to discuss something in their surroundings.

 

A conversation about the mansion had already ended in failure, so what else could they talk about? As her anxious eyes swept the area, she finally discovered a topic.

 

“Ah, look, there are violets over there,” she said tentatively.

 

At her gentle remark, the Duke halted his steps. He turned his head, glanced at her face, and then followed her outstretched hand to where she was pointing. Roselyn moved toward the edge of the flower bed and naturally lowered herself by bending her knees into a squat.

 

Small blue flowers, wild-growing violets in full, unruly bloom, were strikingly beautiful, so much so that for a moment she almost forgot the discomfort the Duke’s presence imposed.

 

“They’re such an uncommon, pale blue… and in such a large cluster. Are these wildflowers, or were they planted on purpose?” 

 

As she spoke, Roselyn lifted her eyes. The Duke stood tall, looking down at her. From that vantage, the already imposing man appeared even larger, and perhaps his cold, unyielding gaze, devoid of any sign of affection, made him seem even more so.

 

His unwavering, piercing stare made her feel terribly insignificant, as if she were nothing more than a wildflower left unplanted and uncared for, unnoticed by anyone.

 

“Hmm…”  

 

The Duke once again showed no interest in engaging in conversation.

 

“I don’t really know,” he replied in a dry tone.

 

Only when he answered so curtly did Roselyn realize the blunder she’d made. He had clearly stated he had no interest in flowers. Her nervousness had gotten the better of her, causing her to speak hastily, she’d been too anxious to make a good impression.

 

“If you’re curious, why not pluck one and show it to the gardener? He’ll tell you all about it,” the Duke suggested.

 

At that, Roselyn glanced down at the cluster of blossoms at her feet. Then, straightening her bent knees, she rose without picking any flower. The Duke, however, made no gesture to offer his hand to help her stand more gracefully.

 

“Thank you for the suggestion, but I don’t think that’s necessary. If I mention they’re nestled between daffodils and roses, the gardener will know. And if they weren’t deliberately planted, plucking one wouldn’t reveal the variety anyway,” she answered politely, smiling with the kind of forced laughter that one masters through years of social graces. She hadn’t wanted to force the conversation further nor did she want to stoop so far as to pluck an innocent flower. Doing so, she felt, might only render her even more pitiable.

 

“Caring for even a single wildflower, Miss Fairfield, you truly have a gentle heart,” the Duke remarked.

 

At his words, Roselyn looked up. Even as she stood straight, he continued to look down on her from above. Although she was usually taller than most women, in that moment she felt smaller than ever.

 

“Splendid,” Duke Windberg praised. Roselyn knew it was nothing more than a formality; his green eyes, as cold as they were, betrayed no hint of warmth. And yet, those eyes, translucent with light, were so beautiful that they made anyone who looked into them feel even more miserable.

 

For a brief moment, they simply regarded each other. The daffodils had already withered, and the roses had yet to bloom; soft light was falling upon the tiny buds. It was late afternoon, and the once-transparent May sunlight was gradually growing hazy with a golden tint.

 

 

 

From the moment Jared entered the dining hall and took his seat, he felt trapped, as if caught in a snare from which there was no escape, a feeling that made him all the more miserable. He’d had a premonition ever since a servant had spilled tea on a guest’s clothes; he suspected that the escorting of the lady wouldn’t end at the lounge.

 

“Now it’s time for a walk. Come along too, Jared, then you’ll have a partner,” his mother had insisted as she returned with the lady. Of course, a walk was out of the question. Jared offered a plausible excuse to decline, only to be met with his mother’s elegant yet predictable rebuke. In the end, he had no choice but to act in a gentlemanly manner.

 

“I shall invite the ladies to dinner. If that makes up for the rudeness, so be it,” he mused to himself, only then was he able to extricate himself from his seat.

 

Afterward, he pondered why his mother had chosen to involve that particular lady with him. Two possible reasons came to mind. First, because she was the daughter of a viscount; of lower nobility, she would be relatively easier to manage and could be molded to his liking. But knowing Diane Glen’s character well, Jared realized that such an inference was a stretch. His mother was not one to balk at accepting the daughter of a Duke, Marquis, or even a royal lady as her future daughter-in-law. Therefore, the more plausible reason was the second one.

 

‘She intends to seat that lady in the rank of a young noblewoman.’

 

Unfortunately, it appeared his mother had misunderstood him… she believed her son harbored affection for that lady.

 

“It is truly delightful to spend time with such young ladies,” his mother remarked, causing Jared to set aside his troubled thoughts. Although dinner had long been underway, he still found it hard to focus on the conversation.

 

“I have always envied those ladies who have daughters, for I have only a son, and today I am free to indulge that envy,” she continued. Tonight’s dinner guests numbered six, all women. Among them, Diane Glen looked especially joyous.

 

Since the moment he had announced his intention to hold a ball, his mother had been in high spirits, relishing the process of gathering noble daughters, personally scrutinizing them, and even interviewing the shortlisted candidates. Thus, by choosing the viscount’s daughter as her final prospective daughter-in-law over candidates from more prestigious families, the Lady was making quite the concession.

 

Could it be that she now intended to respect her son’s wishes? At this point, it seemed inevitable.

 

The more Jared considered his mother’s so-called “consideration,” the more his irritation grew. It felt as if he were being utterly toyed with, by these people, by the women around him, by this dmned situation that enveloped him. He wondered what kind of farce this was, and the fact that there was no escape nearly drove him to madness.

 

He could not postpone his duty as heir forever. He had to marry one of the women at this table.

 

“It seems ostrich feathers are the most beautiful. They’re so extravagant that they catch the eye wherever they’re seen,” one lady remarked.

 

“Lately, dresses in fashion also look splendid with peacock feathers,” another added.

 

“Indeed. With the fabric’s subdued color, peacock feathers provide a lovely accent,” a third concurred.

 

Jared pretended to listen attentively to the ladies’ conversation. Though he had no interest in the trivial debate over which bird’s feathers made the best adornment, and in truth, he harbored a measure of disdain for such chatter, he kept his expression neutral. To reveal any hint of contempt would be utterly unbecoming of a gentleman.

 

Conceal anger, hostility, anxiety… whatever it took.

 

“Miss Roselyn, which feathers do you like best?” one of them inquired.

 

“I like hawk feathers,” she replied.

 

“Just like me. Hawk has an elegant charm,” another commented.

 

“My mother always said the same, that the hawk is a bird of dignity, perfectly suited to a lady,” she added.

 

