Now, his mother was openly favoring one candidate for the final daughter-in-law. At this gathering, the only individuals the Lady addressed by their first names rather than by their family names were Miss Mendel and Miss Andover. Thus, the other ladies from more prominent families, who originally held better prospects, would be concealing their feelings of defeat, while the one who had unexpectedly become the center of attention would hide her delight.

 

‘To cherish even a wildflower, Miss Fairfield, you truly have a gentle nature.’

 

Jared snorted silently and glanced over at Lady Andover, who was seated a short distance away.

 

“Ah, look, there are violets over there.”

 

It was quite a creative strategy indeed; a noble maiden professing her fondness for wildflowers. Didn’t it suggest a simple, unpretentious, and human side? If her aim was to leave a deep impression on him by any means, then she had achieved her goal, albeit not in the manner he had intended.

 

Of course, Jared did not intend to reproach her for her cunning. It was an ambition he could understand entirely. After all, every noble maiden dreams of becoming a Duchess, just as every politician dreams of becoming Prime Minister.

Everyone desires to rise in status. For the daughter of a viscount, becoming a duchess was an overly ambitious leap. In the history of the Windburg dukedom, there had never been a duchess from a viscount family.  

 

Jared picked up his wine glass and cast another glance at the woman. The Andover viscount’s daughter was conversing with another lady seated at the guest of honor’s table. Her face, as she engaged with a competitor, was so serene that it showed no trace of pretense.  

 

Thus, he had no choice but to acknowledge it. The Andover viscount’s daughter was a flawless lady.  

 

Her demeanor, social skills, and refinement were impeccable. A noblewoman raised with the finest education and without any deficiencies. She was reasonably materialistic and shrewd, but within acceptable limits. At the very least, she possessed the discernment and tact to keep such qualities from showing.  

 

That should do.  

 

Jared murmured to himself as he sipped his wine.  

 

Since the moment he had decided to observe her, he had found himself thinking this way multiple times. He was trying to suppress his resistance, persuading himself. If the situation was unavoidable, he should at least choose the best option.  

 

That should do, perhaps it wouldn’t be utterly dreadful.  

 

With a sip of wine still lingering on his tongue, he continued to observe the woman. He noted her gentle smile, the attentiveness in her gaze, and the polite curve of her lips. Dressed in a cream-colored gown, she harmonized well with the centerpiece on the dining table; lush cream-colored roses, mirroring her very essence.  

 

A flower cultivated in a greenhouse. A decorative piece at a banquet table. The epitome of an elegant noblewoman.  

 

That should do.  

 

The moment the thought crossed his mind again, Josephine, seated at the guest of honor’s table, turned her gaze toward him. Jared naturally shifted his eyes to meet hers. The guest of honor’s seat was at the far end of the table, the furthest from the head seat, but directly facing it.  

 

“I heard that Your Grace plays the piano exceptionally well. Is that true?”  

 

At her question, Jared’s lips curved slightly. She was the only person here he had even the slightest fondness for. She wasn’t trying to win his favor, nor did she seem to be forcing herself to enjoy this gathering, unlike the others, she appeared to be genuinely having a good time.  

 

