The lady’s request piqued the interest of the other ladies. Some even took the opportunity to openly gaze at him. Ignoring those stares, Jared set down his wine glass.  

 

With dessert already served, dinner was nearly over. It was customary to move to the drawing room afterward for tea and conversation. And, of course, most drawing rooms had a piano.  

 

“I have learned a little as a cultural refinement, but my skills are hardly worthy of an audience. I believe it would be far more enjoyable for everyone if we were to hear the ladies play instead.”  

 

After politely declining, he continued speaking before the lady could press further.  

 

“Miss Foster, if you don’t mind, would you be the first to perform?”  

 

The lady he addressed looked startled. Jared met her eyes and held her gaze. She was the daughter of the Marquis of Falcon. Her father was an ardent conservative, making her an early disqualification as a prospective bride.  

 

“Are you speaking to me?”  

 

“Yes. Miss Agnes, you have the most beautiful hands. I have no doubt you must be an excellent pianist. If you wouldn’t mind, I would very much like to see you play today.”  

 

He smiled smoothly as he spoke. The marquis’s daughter, stunned, flustered for a moment before blushing deeply. It was the first time that evening he had addressed any of the ladies by name. Jared could feel the shift in atmosphere among the ladies at the table.  

 

He had no intention of playing the piano romantically before them tonight. He also wanted to mock his mother’s assumptions and misplaced concern. So, he spontaneously decided to put on a little performance of his own, and it turned out quite effective. Perhaps he had a talent for seduction that had gone unnoticed for the past thirty years.  

 

The Duke of Windburg, amused by his own discovery, glanced away. Diane Glenn was watching the marquis’s daughter with calm eyes. What would she do now? With her son’s attention seemingly shifting to another woman, would she reconsider her final choice?  

 

His gaze naturally moved to her again.  

 

The Andover viscount’s daughter remained unperturbed. She continued whispering with the lady beside her, smiling faintly as they conversed. Her expression of focus, the curve of her lips, nothing had changed. Whatever she might have been thinking inside, outwardly, she appeared completely composed.  

 

As if her strong candidacy had not been shaken at all. As if she felt neither anxiety nor jealousy toward her competition.  

 

And so, Jared had no choice but to give her more credit.  

 

Composure. A virtue that a noblewoman must possess. Even when faced with her husband’s mistress in the future, she would need to remain unshaken with a steel-like composure.  

 

Jared was aware of his own aversion toward this woman. Yet, his conviction that she was the best choice was only growing stronger. After all, among the five candidates, there wasn’t a single one he didn’t harbor some degree of dislike for. The fact that she seemed likely to get along with his mother and that her father had no political ties were also points in her favor.  

 

So, that should do.  

 

With that final thought, dinner came to an end. Jared finished the last of his wine, pushed back his chair, and rose from his seat.  

 

“Ladies, shall we move to the drawing room?”  

 

At his words, everyone at the table stood. It was time for the ladies to display their cultivated elegance. With six performances to sit through, it would take at least an hour.  

 

Jared, irritated by the realization that this tedious evening was still not over, masked his annoyance with an even more polite smile as he led the way to the drawing room.  

 



 

The piano performances were almost like a competition. Some even added vocals to their playing. Each performance was outstanding, and the level of skill and enthusiasm surprised Roselyn.  

 

The drawing room, where they had been led after dinner, housed a large and magnificent piano. Aside from the instrument, there were many other things that caught Roselyn’s eye; the antique décor, the furniture, and the luxurious ornaments were truly awe-inspiring.  

 

The paintings, porcelain, and candlesticks, likely centuries old, made her want to examine them more closely. She was particularly fascinated by how well these artifacts had been preserved and how seamlessly they blended with the modern interior. If possible, she would have preferred to forgo playing the piano and simply admire the room in peace.  

 

However, her turn inevitably arrived, and Roselyn had no choice but to sit at the piano. She didn’t want to go against the atmosphere and attract unwanted stares. She would rather avoid drawing any further displeasure from the duke.  

 

It was clear that the Duke of Windburg disliked her. No, hated her would be a more accurate description.  

 

Throughout the banquet, he had not spoken a single word to her, making his feelings perfectly clear. Occasionally, his gaze had lingered on her, and their eyes had met at times, but never with the customary polite smile or even a token word of courtesy. He simply watched her from his distant seat at the head of the table, his expression unreadable.  

 

Each time, Roselyn had been the first to avert her gaze. She found it uncomfortable to meet his eyes, and she had no intention of provoking him further by standing her ground in some silent battle. She just wanted to get through the evening and go home.  

 

Then, unexpectedly, all eyes turned to her once again.  

 

“Miss Roselyn, how will you be returning? Are you heading to your townhouse?”

Diane Glenn’s question was natural. After all, Roselyn was the only one here who wouldn’t be returning to her own room. The six other ladies were all from families closely associated with the Duke’s household and had already been staying as guests in Maxville before the ball.  


“I’ll be taking the train, Madam.”  


“Oh my, so your family doesn’t have a townhouse in the city?”  


Northern nobles typically kept separate residences in Windburg’s city center. It was essential for social engagements and cultural life.  


The Andover viscount did own a townhouse in the city, but Roselyn had considered it wasteful to leave it vacant for long periods. As a result, it had been rented out, and she couldn’t use it freely.  


However, there was no need to go into such lengthy explanations. Instead, she simply smiled politely and replied,  


“It is a bit inconvenient, but the distance from Andover Station to the viscount’s manor is very short. It takes less than ten minutes by carriage.”  


“I see. Then we must arrange a carriage for our guest.”  


With a nod, the hostess turned to her son. Roselyn lowered her gaze slightly, waiting for the Duke’s approval.  


Offering transportation to guests who arrived without their own carriage was a basic courtesy. Roselyn herself always provided a coach and driver for visitors leaving Fairfield House.  


But the Duke’s response was unexpected.  


“Why not stay the night? I’ll have a room prepared for you.”  


It was a much more generous gesture than simply providing a carriage, but Roselyn couldn’t help but flinch. Accepting a guest room in another noble’s home meant staying for at least a week out of courtesy. She had already spent half a day enduring discomfort, how could she possibly stay for an entire week?  


She looked up at the Duke with hesitation. His expression was indifferent, showing no real desire for her to stay. If he had only made the offer out of politeness, then it would be proper for her to decline just as politely.  


“I truly appreciate the offer, but I think it would be best for me to return tonight. I only packed for the tea party and didn’t bring any luggage.”  


“I can send someone to your manor to fetch what you need.”  


“No, Your Grace. I truly appreciate it, but there’s no need to trouble yourself.”  


As she declined a second time, the Duke nodded without resistance. So, he hadn’t been serious about the offer after all.  


What were you expecting, Roselyn Fairfield? She chided herself bitterly.  


“In that case, I have no choice. I’ll drive you home.”  


“I appreciate the thought, but I still have plenty of time before my train–”  


“Miss Fairfield is my guest. Surely, you’re not suggesting that I put a lady on a train alone at this hour?”  


The Duke’s sharp tone cut her off mid-sentence. It seemed he was displeased, and Roselyn instinctively shrank back.  


Did she offend him? Had she declined too many times?  


She met his gaze, a gaze that seemed to suppress some unreadable emotion. Only then did it occur to her that repeatedly refusing his hospitality might have come across as discourteous.  


She had been about to thank him belatedly, but he didn’t give her the chance.  


“I’ll see my guest off, Mother.”  


“Very well.”  


“Ladies, thank you for accepting my invitation tonight. Please rest well.”  


After casually addressing the room, he turned to Roselyn.  


“Shall we go, Miss Fairfield?”  


His nearly expressionless face left no room for refusal.  


In the end, Roselyn had no choice but to follow him out of the drawing room, feeling as though she were being escorted away under guard.  





The dining hall and drawing room were both on the second floor. When they stepped outside, a young butler was already waiting in the hallway. The Duke instructed him to prepare the car, then led Roselyn toward the central staircase.  


The grand, sweeping staircase led down to the main hall. At half-past eight in the evening, the household had settled into a quiet lull.  


“The car will be here shortly. Would you mind waiting here for a moment?”  


“Of course.”  


Roselyn answered briefly and then fell silent. Normally, after sitting through a long dinner, she might have added something lighthearted, perhaps that standing for a while felt refreshing. Or she might have pointed out an interesting piece of décor in the main hall to spark a conversation. That would have been enough to fill the time until the car arrived.  


But she found herself unable to call upon her usual social grace. Instead, she simply stood there in silence, beside the Duke. She was afraid of making a mistake by saying the wrong thing.  


Yes, Roselyn was afraid.  


She didn’t know why she felt so nervous. Being aware of how she appeared in someone else’s eyes was nothing new. Nobles, especially those who valued propriety, always carried that pressure, and as the daughter of a viscount, Roselyn was no exception.  


But the tension she felt next to the Duke was different. She found herself constantly wary of his mood. She hadn’t done anything wrong, yet she felt intimidated. She wanted to present herself well, but she was afraid of failing to earn his approval.  


So, she decided to remain silent. If she didn’t attempt anything, at least things wouldn’t get worse.  


She still had no idea what she had done wrong or why he seemed so indifferent toward her.  


“When would be a good time for a return visit?”  


