“My father and younger brother will be delighted to host Your Grace for dinner.”  


“The pleasure is mine.”  


His response was courteous, his gaze fixed on her. Roselyn lifted her eyes to meet his deep green ones. They no longer held outright displeasure, yet they lacked warmth as well.  


The eye contact didn’t last long. At that moment, the entrance doors swung open.  


The massive wooden and iron doors parted, revealing the outside. Near a large automobile stood a young footman and the middle-aged maid who had accompanied Roselyn. She remained standing in the main hall, gazing at those waiting for her.  


Through the wide-open doors, the May night swept in. The fragrant air of spring curled around her.  


“Travel safely.”  


At the Duke’s words, she turned to look at him again.  


It was his way of saying, This is where I leave you, go on your own now. He wouldn’t accompany her all the way to the car, but even escorting her to the entrance and waiting with her had been an act of rare courtesy.  


“I’ll see you next Thursday.”  


“Yes. Until then.”  


Roselyn gave a slight bow in farewell. The Duke returned a nod before turning away, walking back into the mansion without hesitation.  


His departing footsteps echoed with unwavering resolve.  


Hearing them fade, Roselyn finally stepped outside alone.  


The young footman greeted her with impeccable manners. It was the same man she had seen in front of the department store once before. With a respectful gesture, he opened the car door.  


For the first time in her life, Roselyn stepped into a machine-powered vehicle instead of a horse-drawn carriage.  


The space was smaller than a carriage, making the scent of its owner’s cologne all the more noticeable. It was unfamiliar, but she immediately recognized it as his scent.  


Inhaling deeply, she steadied her racing heart.  


With three passengers aboard, the car rolled down Maxville’s long, winding driveway. 


Then, cutting through the dark spring night, it sped toward Andover.

 As winter faded and spring reached its peak, the northern social scene regained its vibrancy. The social season in the capital, Isen, began in March, but those who had wintered there to escape the cold began moving into their Windburg townhouses by mid-May. They did so to prepare for the northern social season, which officially began in June.  


Spring in Windburg was fleeting yet beautiful. Perhaps it was because it was so brief that it felt even more precious. It was a season that could only be truly appreciated after enduring the long, harsh winter.  


