“How about lilies?”  


“Better to avoid those as well. He might be sensitive to strong scents.”  


Roselyn studied the dining arrangements presented by the butler, deep in thought. What flowers would be best? Something simple and scentless, something even someone indifferent to flowers would recognize.  


“Tulips, perhaps? In cream.”  


“A dignified choice. I’ll see to it.”  


“Thank you, Parsons.”  


“It’s my pleasure, my lady. The day has nearly arrived.”  


“It has.”  


Roselyn exchanged a brief smile with the butler.  


The telegram from the Duke’s estate had arrived yesterday. The message from his chief attendant stated that the Duke would arrive at 5:30 p.m. in two days, accompanied by a retinue of five.  


The only requests were that the food not be too sweet and that simple beverages be prepared. Compared to some guests who sent lengthy lists of dietary restrictions, the Duke was surprisingly undemanding, at least in that regard.  


In other aspects, however, he seemed highly particular.  


Since the tea party, Roselyn had thought deeply about his cold demeanor toward her. In the end, she concluded that she must have said or done something to offend him.  


Still, she couldn’t quite understand why he was extending such an extraordinary courtesy as a return visit.  


She wasn’t without hope but she also feared disappointment.  


After all, how could she possibly know his true intentions?  


As such, another restless day passed, and the long-awaited moment finally arrived.

“Sister, Sister! Which one should I wear?”  


“The one on the right.”  


“But Victor said the one on the left was prettier.”  


“You’d be better off not taking fashion advice from him.”  


“You’re so mean! Poor Victor.”  


“Ask Luke. He’ll pick the same one as me. But are you really walking around dressed like that?”  


“Yes. There are no guests, so what does it matter?”  


With a bold reply, Andrea spun out of her sister’s room, her golden hair fluttering behind her. The maid carrying two dresses followed closely after the youngest lady of the house.  


She runs around like that, chasing after our brothers, wearing nothing but a corset and petticoat? Roselyn pressed her fingertips to her forehead and let out a small sigh.  


Her youngest sibling was clearly excited about the upcoming event. Even Lucas, the third child, had returned home from boarding school to attend the dinner. Meeting and conversing with the Duke himself would be a valuable experience for him. If he could establish a connection, all the better.  


That was why today’s hospitality had to be flawless. There could be no room for criticism. Sitting at her vanity, Roselyn carefully reviewed every detail once more. Was there anything left that might displease their guest?  


“I do not like roses.”  


If there was anything here that might displease him, it was probably her.  


Roselyn lifted her gaze to the mirror. She examined the color and design of her dove-gray dress. Was the square neckline too deep? Was her pinned-up hair secure? Were the pearl necklace and earrings too plain? One by one, she double-checked everything.  


Even on a second look, it was all perfect. She had even chosen lilac perfume instead of rosewater, there were no oversights.  


And yet, why did she still feel so uneasy? Her anxious heart had been pounding heavily all day.  


Don’t worry. It’ll be fine. You’ve always been good at hosting guests.  


Muttering encouragement to herself, as was her habit, Roselyn finally left her room.  


After checking on her younger sister’s attire and heading downstairs together, it was precisely 5:30 p.m. when they arrived at the drawing room. The three men of the household were already there, dressed impeccably, waiting for the ladies.  


The guest arrived exactly five minutes later, at the perfectly proper time.  


“He’s here! The Duke is here!”  


“He’s arrived, Father.”  


Andrea and Lucas, standing by the window, were the first to announce the Duke’s arrival. Viscount Andover, who had been conversing with his eldest son, paused and looked at Roselyn before rising from his armchair in a bright mood.  


“Well then, children. Let’s go greet our guest.”  


And so, all five members of the Fairfield family stepped out of the drawing room.  


In front of the mansion’s entrance, the butler, head housekeeper, and several other staff members stood in a neat line. Once the viscount’s family took their places at the front, everything was set for the reception.  


Roselyn stood to her father’s right, raising her chin slightly. In the distance, two black automobiles approached.  


“That one in front is Frausen’s latest model. Still think automobiles are vulgar?”  


Her father’s quiet whisper made Roselyn chuckle. He was trying to ease her tension, and it worked.  


By the time the car came to a stop and the Duke stepped out, she was able to greet him with a natural smile.  


He wore a black frock coat with a pale blue silk tie, paired with black trousers and polished shoes; an impeccably refined gentleman’s ensemble. Considering his status, his attire was almost modest. Though undoubtedly made by the finest craftsmen with the best materials, there was no hint of ostentation.  


Definitely not the extravagant type.  


With that thought, Roselyn met the Duke’s gaze, maintaining the comfortable smile her father had given her.  


“Miss Fairfield.”  


“Welcome, Your Grace. I hope you had a pleasant journey.”  


“You live in a fine place.”  


The two exchanged polite pleasantries, nothing more than the customary lines expected of their station. There was no avoiding it, after all, they were not close enough to speak informally.  


“This is my father.”  


“A pleasure to meet you, Viscount Andover. Thank you for accepting my invitation for a return visit.”  


“The pleasure is ours, Duke Windburg. We are honored beyond words.”  


Roselyn introduced her father, and in turn, the Viscount introduced the rest of his children.  


The Duke greeted the Fairfield men with appropriate courtesy but did not offer a handshake. When introduced to Andrea, he gave a slightly warmer smile, perhaps as a gesture of consideration for the young lady, though it remained within the bounds of propriety.  


I wonder if he ever truly smiles.  


Roselyn found it impossible to imagine.  


After completing the formal welcome at the entrance, they led their guest to the drawing room. It was customary to serve tea and engage in conversation before dinner.  


Seated with the Duke were the Viscount, his heir, and his eldest daughter. The younger two siblings, being minors, were not permitted to join the adults for tea.  


“The Fairfield family seems to be a harmonious one. It is a pleasant sight.”  


“It is all thanks to my eldest daughter. My wife passed away early, but Roselyn filled the void. She was bright and mature even as a child and has managed the household affairs admirably since then. Rose, was it when you were eleven?”  


“I was eleven, but it would be unfair to count from then. The first few years were purely observational. If not for you guiding me, I wouldn’t have managed at all.”  


“But even with a conservative estimate, that means you have over ten years of experience as the lady of the house. Everyone in the region already regards her as the Viscountess.”  


“I see. You have quite the remarkable daughter.”  


The Duke’s polite response was met with an awkward smile from Roselyn.  


Her father had always been generous with praise for his eldest daughter in front of others, but today, it was almost overwhelming. Even when she subtly attempted to steer the conversation elsewhere, it inevitably circled back to accolades about her.  


Alfred Fairfield proudly informed the Duke of how refined and cultured Roselyn was, how prudent and free of vanity, and how she was a role model for all young ladies in their social circle.  


Even Victor, the eldest son, chimed in to support their father’s praises.  


Roselyn’s lips grew dry with discomfort. Wasn’t the Duke getting tired of hearing all this?  


Finally, after indulging in his fill of compliments about his daughter, the Viscount changed the topic.  


“She was delighted to be invited to Maxville. I myself had the honor of visiting Duke Philip’s estate once.”  


“Is that so?”  


“It was over twenty years ago, but I still remember the grandeur of the ducal residence. And the splendid evergreen forest surrounding it.”  


“This estate seems to have an excellent location as well. I noticed a wooded area on my way in, the scenery is quite picturesque.”  


“It’s a simple grove, but it makes for a pleasant walk. Would you care to take a stroll before dinner? A bit of fresh air before a meal helps stimulate the appetite.”  


“I would be delighted. That sounds like a fine idea.”  


The Duke placed his teacup down as if he had been waiting for this suggestion.  


He must have been growing weary of hearing about Roselyn. She noticed his teacup was still nearly full, just like hers.  


“If it wouldn’t be too much of an imposition, may I ask Miss Fairfield to be my guide?”  


At the guest’s request, Roselyn held her breath.  


She lifted her eyes nervously, but the Duke was looking only at her father.  


Of course. In this situation, it was proper etiquette to seek permission from the head of the household rather than the lady herself.  


“Of course, Duke Windburg. My daughter would be honored to accompany you. Isn’t that right, Rose?”  


Her father readily agreed, his face brimming with genuine delight. He looked immensely proud, and rightfully so.  


After all, the Fairfield family was on the verge of producing a duchess.  


Roselyn was well aware of how courtship in high society worked. She had received proposals before, and her suitors had all followed the same steps: visiting the Viscount’s estate, being hosted by her father for tea or dinner, and then requesting private time with the lady of the house.  


That was the standard procedure before making a marriage proposal.  


The only difference was that her past suitors had always been men who genuinely liked or even loved her.  


This Duke, however, showed no such sentiment at all.

Roselyn still found this apparent proposal difficult to trust. Nevertheless, she calmed her racing heart and offered a polite smile. She still couldn’t be certain of the Duke’s true intentions.

“Yes, Father, of course. Shall we go before the sun sets, Your Grace?”  

She asked with the demeanor of a gracious hostess, and only then did the Duke turn his head to look at her.  

“Very well, Miss Fairfield.”  

It was the briefest of glances, too fleeting to read any intention behind it. Without hesitation, he rose from his seat, leaving Roselyn no choice but to stand as well.



The grove was silent. The Viscount’s estate was fenced in, ensuring that no outsiders wandered through. Normally, this solitude was comforting, but today, she resented the quiet. It left no distraction, no barrier between them, nothing to fill the awkward silence.

Roselyn walked slowly along the narrow path, keeping two steps of distance between herself and the Duke, who walked on her left. He kept pace like a gentleman but made no effort to ease her discomfort.

Whenever she pointed out a brook, a tree, or a boulder, he merely responded with curt replies. The pattern repeated a few times, her reaching out, him responding briefly, without enthusiasm. He showed no real interest in any topic.

Then, suddenly, he spoke.

“I wish to hasten the wedding.”  

The words came abruptly, without preamble.  

“I would like it to be held on the 27th of next month.”  

As he added this, the Duke stopped walking. Roselyn had no choice but to stop as well. She was so stunned that an idiotic response tumbled out.  

“Are you speaking to me?”  

“Who else is here besides us?”  

He did not smile. His face was unreadable, was it simply emotionless, or was he tense? She couldn’t tell. What she did know was that this was, without a doubt, a proposal.  

As realization sank in, her heart pounded wildly. She was speechless, not out of joy, but confusion. And in that confusion, she was startled to find that she wasn’t happy.  

Why wasn’t she happy? This was a marriage proposal from the Duke of Windburg.

She stared at him blankly, unable to form a response. Seeing this, the Duke seemed to interpret her reaction differently.  

“You’re so surprised, it surprises me more. Did you not anticipate that I came here today to discuss marriage?”  

He tilted his head slightly. Roselyn swallowed, struggling to compose herself. This conversation was too important to mishandle.  

“It’s not that I didn’t consider the possibility… but your words came rather suddenly.”  

“Suddenly?” 

His tone was faintly amused. 

“I assumed you would have expected this situation. You attended the ball I hosted, and you accepted my mother’s invitation to her tea party. Surely, you weren’t unaware that we were searching for a duchess.”  

His words were somewhat cynical, but they weren’t wrong. Roselyn had known the purpose of the ball, the meaning behind this walk. She had known, even as she told herself that the position of duchess was beyond her reach, that she had hoped for it.  

She had dreamed of marrying this man.  

And yet, now, why didn’t she feel happy?  

“Did you not wish to marry me, Miss Fairfield?”  

The Duke’s straightforward question struck her like an arrow.  

“The Viscount of Andover seemed like a good father. But if, contrary to appearances, he is a man who coerces his daughter into submission, then I must assume that you accepted his will against your own. If that is not the case, however, then it is only natural for me to conclude that you also desire this marriage. Am I mistaken?”  

His tone was matter-of-fact, devoid of reproach. In its emotionlessness, it felt almost too dry. As she listened, Roselyn was reminded that he had once been a lawyer. Did he always speak so coldly?  

“If you don’t wish to marry at all, I will respect that. There must be a reason you have turned down all your past suitors.”  

“…I don’t recall ever saying that, but it seems you know quite a bit about me.”  

“I couldn’t possibly choose a duchess without such considerations. I hope you do not take offense, it only means I take this proposal very seriously.”  

He was so confident in his own certainty that she had no choice but to acknowledge it. He was sure that she would not reject him.  

And he was right.  

Because Roselyn had been just one among the many women in the ballroom that night, one of the many waiting to be chosen.  

So she had to ask.  

“Why me?”  

If he was truly serious about this marriage, if he had chosen her over all the other candidates, if he had gone so far as to investigate her background, she wanted to know why.  

Why her?  

“You know I am not from a great family, and my father cannot provide a large dowry.”  

“I have no need of a wife’s family’s support, nor am I in any position to choose a bride based on dowry.”  

“That was not my implication. I just… I fail to understand why you have chosen me.”  

The Duke fell silent. Roselyn watched as his green eyes flickered downward for a moment, as if considering something. He briefly gazed into the empty air before returning his gaze to hers. She studied his expression, his mouth set in an unreadable line, trying to decipher anything.  

“I see.” His voice was laced with something new, was it amusement or something sharper? 

“You want a more convincing proposal.”  

A faint smile appeared on his lips. For the first time, Roselyn glimpsed an emotion in him, bitterness. And that bitterness scraped against her heart.  

“If it is because you are beautiful, would that be enough?”  

He smiled as he said it. Not warmly, not sincerely. His expression was cold, arrogant, a smirk more than anything. The condescension in it sparked something in her.  

“I appreciate your high opinion of my looks, but I do not believe appearance is a valid reason to choose a spouse.”  

“Why not?”  

“Because beauty is like clothing. As long as it meets basic standards, it is sufficient. No one wishes to marry a fine set of clothes, unless they have poor taste.”  

“You must never have dealt with someone truly unpleasant to look at.”  

His sarcasm deepened. He was openly mocking her now. Roselyn was at a loss, she could tell he was teasing, but she didn’t know how to respond. She simply stared at him, stunned.  

“Why?” he asked, expression unmoving. “Do you find my face offensive?”  

She fought to steady herself. He was clearly irritated, and engaging in an argument would do her no good.  

“You are a very well-mannered man, Your Grace. I believe most people would agree, so I see no need to elaborate further.”  

“That was not the compliment I was looking for. And I don’t care about other people’s opinions.”  

The Duke adjusted his stance, straightening. At some point, he had leaned slightly toward her, perhaps without realizing it. Now, as if regaining composure, he turned his gaze toward the brook.  

After a brief pause, he looked at her again.  

“Let’s be honest. A duchess’s beauty is not something to be ignored. My mother was a great beauty, and even now, she remains so. In her youth, her looks were particularly advantageous.”  

“Lady Diane is a beautiful woman.”  

“If you continue to say such things, she will love you all the more as a daughter-in-law. She believes you possess the beauty to stand beside me as a perfect couple. I happen to agree.”  

With that, the Duke shifted his weight onto his other foot. As if to say, Is this not enough?

But Roselyn had gained nothing. They had exchanged many words, but they were empty, hollow. The Duke, too, seemed to realize this.  

“If my proposal is still not enough, then unfortunately, it seems this is not the right moment for me to offer a ring.”

Jared looked down at her as he spoke. His words, like an ultimatum, sent a chill through Roselyn.  

What are you doing, Roselyn Fairfield? He could change his mind. He could call the whole thing off!  

A voice inside her screamed in panic, but she did not speak. She didn’t say that she had heard enough and that she was ready to accept the ring. At that moment, she realized what she truly wanted, what she had been hoping to hear.  

She wanted him to say that he was drawn to her. That he was falling in love. That he wanted to marry her because of her.  

She knew it was an unreasonable hope. Love at this stage would be premature and foolish. But wasn’t it natural to expect at least some feeling between two people who would spend their lives together?  

At the very least, couldn’t he offer the hint that he found her appealing, that he could imagine a life of love with her?  

But in the end, the Duke said nothing of the sort.  

“Very well. Then I will give you time to think.”  

He offered a compromise, his expression calm.  

“I will return in three days. If you intend to refuse my proposal, send a telegram to Maxville. Will that suffice?”  

When the Duke spoke in a slightly softer tone, Roselyn felt both relief and disappointment. Three days. An unexpected grace period, but she couldn’t reject it. At the very least, it meant he was respecting her thoughts, and she tried to find comfort in that.  

“If that is what you wish, Your Grace…”  

“Let’s do that, then.”  

The man nodded readily, retrieving his pocket watch to check the time.  

Was the ring in the other pocket? The thought irritated her.  

“It’s time to head back, I think.”  

“Yes. We can return the way we came.”  

She forced herself to turn first, masking her emotions with a strained smile. But inside, her heart was pounding with a mix of excitement and anxiety. She already felt regret creeping in.  

What have you done, letting things come to this?  

The Duke still walked two steps away from her, keeping pace without any sign of displeasure at having his proposal delayed. But who knew what he was really thinking?  

And now, she couldn’t very well ask for the ring back.  

For the next three days, it wouldn’t be him agonizing over the situation, it would be her.  

She could only hope that his feelings wouldn’t change in that time.  

And if they did… then perhaps he was never meant to be hers in the first place.  

Roselyn steadied herself and continued walking along the path. The late afternoon forest was silent, even the birds had quieted. There was no wind, no rustling leaves. Only shadows shifted noiselessly in the dimming light.  



The dinner was beyond expectations. Most of the dishes were exquisite, and the servants carried out their duties with practiced ease. The table was set elegantly with candlesticks, silverware, and crystal glasses; a classic noble feast.  

Jared reached for his wine glass, letting his gaze sweep across the table. Seated at the long, rectangular dining table, he could see everyone clearly. The Fairfield siblings were arranged in perfect order, alternating male and female. Their balance alone made them seem like the ideal family.  

“This sole is our cook’s specialty,” Viscount Andover remarked. “Even I don’t know the recipe, but I can assure you, the taste is unmatched. I hope it suits your palate.”  

“It’s excellent. I’d be tempted to bring your cook to Maxville.”  

“I’ll be sure to pass on the compliment, though I’ll leave out that last part.”  

The Viscount chuckled, and Jared responded with a slight smile as he took another sip of wine.  

The Viscount’s family seemed genuinely close. Had he remained unmarried for thirteen years out of devotion to his children? If so, it was a rare thing indeed. In aristocratic society, where marriage was often a tool, such an act was almost unheard of.  

When Jared had arrived earlier that day, he had seen the Viscount and Roselyn speaking quietly at the entrance. The Viscount had said something that made Roselyn laugh, her face lighting up with joy.  

It had made him wonder, what had been said to make her smile like that?  

Jared had no sisters, so he knew little about the bond between fathers and daughters. He had never been close to sibling relationships either, which made the scene feel unfamiliar. A well-cared-for family, harmonious and affectionate. And at the center of it all was Roselyn.  

“After my wife passed, Roselyn filled the gap.”  

Perhaps that was why she seemed almost maternal toward her younger siblings. The way they treated her made it clear how much they depended on her.  

Before, Jared had assumed the Viscount’s words were simply to encourage his proposal. But now, watching them interact, he realized it wasn’t entirely pretense.  

Roselyn Fairfield was a woman deeply trusted by her family.  

“Why me?”  

