A family-oriented woman. Someone who cared for her younger siblings and would likely make a good mother.  

Would that explanation satisfy her? The virtuous Miss Fairfield?  

“Your Grace, I simply fail to understand why you chose me.”  

Her question had genuinely irritated him earlier.  

She had attended the ball with the intention of becoming a duchess, even enlisting her aunt’s help to catch his attention. And now, she dared to say she didn’t understand why he had chosen her?  

Did she just want to toy with him now that she had secured his proposal?  

“You want a more convincing proposal, then?”  

The very idea was absurd.  

They had met twice before today. This was their third meeting, and the longest time they had spent together. In what world did that warrant a grand romantic speech?  

Did she expect him to claim love at first sight? To declare that he was utterly bewitched by her beauty and grace?  

Ridiculous.  

Jared smirked slightly and set down his wine glass. Hiding his cold amusement, he glanced across the table at her. Roselyn sat diagonally opposite him, to the left of her father at the head of the table.  

“You didn’t show His Grace the Fairy Falls?”  

“It’s too far. We would have needed horses, not just a casual stroll.”  

“But it’s the best spot in the forest! You missed out, sis.”  

“Well, we’ll just have to save it for next time. That is, if His Grace visits Andover again.”  

Roselyn smoothly redirected the conversation to include him. Her subtlety was impressive, ensuring that no guest was left out.  

“I do hope you visit again, Your Grace. The Fairy Falls are quite a sight.”  

“I see. A shame to have missed them, but I imagine I’ll have the chance to see them soon.”  

“I’ll take you there myself next time. Walking with the ladies must be tedious.”  

“Luke, that’s hardly a gentlemanly remark.”  

“Says the girl who still acts like a child.”  

“I’m seventeen!”  

“And next month, I’ll be eighteen, kid.”  

As her younger siblings bickered, Roselyn gave an exasperated but fond smile before glancing toward Jared, offering him a polite, apologetic look.  

He returned it with an appropriate smile.  

As a guest, she had played the part of a guest. As a hostess, she played the part of a hostess.  

She was undoubtedly skilled in social graces. And yet, something about her bothered him.  

“Beauty is like clothing. As long as it meets basic standards, it is sufficient.”  

Something about her got under his skin. She was elegant, well-mannered; the ideal noblewoman.  

And maybe that was why he found her so irritating.  

The perfect noble lady. Too naive, too rigid, the kind of woman who lived by lessons out of a moral handbook.  

“No one wishes to marry a fine set of clothes, unless they have poor taste.”  

He had complimented her looks, and she had insulted him for it.  

Jared let out a quiet laugh.  

To satisfy her, it seemed, he would have to be a saint.  

No. Not just a saint; one who had fallen madly, desperately in love at first sight.  

With a smirk, Jared stabbed his fork into the perfectly seasoned fish.

“The wedding should be arranged as soon as possible. I would like it to take place on the 27th of next month.”  

The letter to Bishop Seamus had already been sent, requesting him to officiate the wedding in Maxville on June 27th. Jared knew his wedding would proceed as planned. That woman wouldn’t send a telegram of refusal.  

If, by any chance, she did decline, he would simply choose another from the list of candidates. The wedding would take place on that date regardless. Only the bride would change.  

“Your Grace, do such banquets take place every day in Maxville?”  

The young girl’s voice pulled Jared from his thoughts. The Viscount’s second daughter was a naive yet charming young lady. Unlike her sister, she was blonde and bore little resemblance to her. However, once she matured and shed her youthful appearance, she would likely become just as beautiful as her elder sister.  

“They almost always do, Miss Fairfield. Maxville frequently welcomes guests from various places, so there are very few days when the guest rooms are empty. As the host, it is my duty to treat visitors with hospitality, and I gladly spend time with them.”  

