“Ah, of course, you are right. You simply spoke the truth.”  

The Viscount could not hide his delight. Jared could feel the overwhelming satisfaction in the dining hall. In the midst of the joyous atmosphere he had created, he caught sight of Roselyn. She was staring down at her plate with an unreadable expression, a vague smile on her lips.  

Marriage, it seemed, was a truly difficult affair.  

As he looked at the woman who was avoiding his gaze, a new dish was brought to the table. Though he was already full, he knew he should taste it out of courtesy. He carefully transferred the smallest piece of what he presumed to be duck onto his plate and took a measured bite while contemplating an appropriate compliment for the host.  



The Duke of Windburg had given her three days to consider, but in reality, this was not a decision that required such deliberation. The very notion of “considering” a proposal from the Duke was absurd. Who would even think of weighing their options in the face of such a monumental fortune?  

For three nights, Roselyn struggled to find even a single reason not to accept his proposal.  

