If she became a duchess, she could provide for her family. Her father and her brother, Victor, would immediately gain access to the highest social circles. Lucas would secure a prestigious job after graduation, and Andrea would find a far better marriage prospect. So why should she refuse to marry this man?  

“Wasn’t it Miss Fairfield who wanted to marry me?”  

For three nights, Roselyn searched for a reason why she should not marry Jared Glen. Yet, until the morning star faded, she failed to find a single one.  

At sunrise, when the world became clear again, she tried to be rational. She considered the worst-case scenarios she might face if she married him. She speculated about why he had chosen her over the daughters of Dukes and marquesses.  

“Why me?”  

She wondered whether she would be loved if she married him, whether they could live together in love.  

“So, is my sister the very lady Your Grace has been searching for?”  

“Of course, Miss Fairfield.”  

She tried to believe that he truly meant it.  

“I am certain of it.”  

But how could he be so sure?  

Roselyn told no one that she had already been proposed to by the Duke. If word got out that she was hesitating, people would think she had lost her mind.  

Even so, her father, her siblings, and even the household servants were already convinced. They believed without question that she would become the Duchess of Windburg.  

If only there were someone she could confide in. But unfortunately, she had no mother or sister. Her aunt, Baroness Elwood, was not close enough for such a discussion, nor could she share her doubts with the head maid. Her father was an even less suitable confidant.  

Even searching the library yielded no books titled “Things to Consider When Receiving a Marriage Proposal” or “Guidelines for Dealing with a Dubious Suitor.” Why didn’t scholars focus on such practical matters? In real life, wasn’t marriage far more significant than history or philosophy?  

Thus, three nights passed, and three mornings came. In the end, Roselyn never sent a telegram to Maxville. Because, despite all her efforts, she failed to find even one definitive reason not to.

Three days was not a short amount of time. To Jared Glen, it felt even longer.  

During the three days following his visit to Andover, he met people every day. The guests who had stayed in Maxville for the ball had long since departed, but new guests had already arrived. Most of those visiting the ducal estate had received invitations from the duchess.  

Diane Glen enjoyed inviting artists, scholars, and writers to host gatherings both large and small. She believed that broadening one’s horizons and cultivating refinement through such interactions was essential. It was something she had done regularly since her days as the Duchess of Windburg.  

Naturally, these gatherings took place in Maxville’s reception rooms, galleries, and music halls, and as the master of the house, Jared could not completely ignore them. Whenever he made an appearance, he often found himself detained longer than anticipated.  

Even while handling these social engagements, he had other matters to attend to.  

“Ensure the family law amendment is passed. Use all your influence.”  

Jared was gradually preparing to step into his official duties. Passing a bill in parliament was not a simple matter of voting. Even drafting and putting a proposal to a vote required extensive groundwork.  

After years of effort, failing to secure enough votes would render all that work meaningless. The more significant the bill, the more meticulous the preparation had to be, ensuring that the votes were secured in advance.  

The Family Law Amendment was a progressive agenda item being pushed by reformists in the Imperial Parliament, bold enough to be considered a true reformation. The core of the bill was to abolish the current law of primogeniture, ensuring inheritance rights for not only younger sons but also daughters.  

According to Jared’s understanding, this movement had begun roughly three years ago. The driving force behind it was none other than Empress Rainerys, the wife of the current emperor.  

“Duke Windburg, this is the price of our arrangement.”  

Jared owed the Empress a debt.  

However, no matter how favorably he viewed the amendment, the likelihood of it passing was close to zero. Not only did it face opposition in the aristocratic upper house, but even the commoner-controlled lower house had a majority against it.  

It was hardly surprising. The two houses of parliament were composed entirely of men, and they saw no reason to divide their estates among daughters who would take their husband’s surname upon marriage.  

After careful consideration, Jared concluded that the amendment had to be drastically revised. If he wanted to negotiate with the conservatives, who refused to listen, he had to first remove the most radical clauses. To do so, he needed to persuade the reformists first.  

His first target: Baron Gunther Schwartz, the leader of the reformist faction in the upper house and a staunch ally of the Empress. Jared had sent a letter inviting him to Maxville ten days ago.  

“Your Grace, Baron Schwartz has just passed through the front gates.”  

Sterling, the head butler, appeared as Jared was listening to his senior aide’s report in the office.  

“I thought he was arriving the day after tomorrow?”  

“That was our understanding as well, but it seems he has changed his plans.”  

Jared nodded, noting the lack of surprise on the butler’s face. It was unexpected, but not unusual. Nobles had plenty of time, and were notoriously capricious.  

“I’ll go greet him myself. He’s an important guest, so handle everything with care, Sterling.”  

“Of course, Your Grace.”  

“Morrison, cancel all my appointments for today.”  

Jared rose from his chair, and Sterling helped him into his frock coat with practiced ease. Without hesitation, he strode toward the entrance.  

Welcoming the baron in person would be seen as a special courtesy. If he wanted the negotiations to go his way, he needed to play the part of a gracious host.  

“But, Your Excellency, one of today’s appointments is…”  

It was only when his aide hesitated that Jared suddenly remembered.  

“There’s a scheduled visit to Viscount Andover’s estate.”  

Was that today?  

He paused briefly, his gaze drifting into the empty air. When had three days already passed? He must have been too preoccupied with other matters.  

To be honest, he had thought about her over the past three days, more than once. Every time a servant or aide handed him a telegram, the thought had flashed through his mind: Has she finally sent a refusal? Before opening the message, he found himself momentarily tense.  

Perhaps that was why he had tried not to think about her because he didn’t want to care.  

“Why me?”  

Jared knew exactly what she wanted to hear. But those were the very words he never wanted to say. He already understood the process of attraction, of falling for someone, of surrendering to love. And he knew exactly how destructive and draining that emotion could be.  

“I love you.”  

Falling in love was a terrifying thing. It stripped away reason, making a man act against his nature.  

Jared never wanted to experience that again. He refused to drown in that abyss a second time. He had barely escaped once, he wouldn’t walk back into the swamp.  

Why had he allowed himself to get caught up in this troublesome situation?  

His jaw clenched involuntarily. With a sharp breath, he issued his command.  

“Summon Mr. Miller. Have him meet me in ten minutes.”  

“Understood.”  

With that short order, Jared left the office without hesitation. Walking down the corridor with the butler at his side, he finalized his thoughts.  

He did not want a woman who sought his affection. If that was the reason she refused, then so be it; eliminated. Roselyn Fairfield was not the woman he was looking for.  

“So, is my sister the very lady Your Grace has been searching for?”  

In fact, this might be for the best. It was a perfect opportunity to confirm whether she truly suited him.  

Jared had no intention of bringing emotions into this marriage. If she was intelligent, she would accept that condition. If not, she would walk away. If the final candidate failed the final test, he would simply move on to the next one.  

This was the most critical requirement for the Duchess of Windburg:  

To accept her husband’s terms, and expect nothing in return.  

The previous duchesses of Maxville had all understood this. His next wife would have to do the same.  



As the promised hour approached, Roselyn’s hands grew cold. She rubbed them together repeatedly, trying to warm her stiff fingers.  

The clock pointed precisely to 3 PM. Fairfield House was enveloped in its usual peaceful silence.  

A guest of great importance was expected for tea, and the kitchen had already completed preparations. The cake to be served had been personally decorated with cream by today’s supposed main guest. Every time the servants looked at the future duchess, they couldn’t help but smile.  

After meticulously decorating the cake, Roselyn realized… time had never moved so slowly.  

For the past three days, she had wondered: Had he changed his mind?  

Perhaps he had grown offended that she had postponed her answer. If he sought a compliant and obedient wife, her hesitation might have been reason enough to reconsider.  

Then, yesterday, a telegram arrived from the Duke’s aide, confirming that he would visit at 3 PM today. Roselyn was unexpectedly moved.  

Beyond relief that she had not made a grave mistake, she was touched by the duke’s consideration. He had respected her decision. He had waited. And he hadn’t needed to.  

“How are you feeling?”  

Her father’s voice pulled her back to reality. They sat facing each other in the second-floor family room.  

“I don’t know. It still doesn’t feel real.”  

“It will, once you receive the ring.”  

“Do you think so?”  

“Without a doubt. Your mother would be so happy, Rose.”  

Roselyn smiled at her father’s affectionate expression, then turned her gaze to her mother’s portrait hanging over his shoulder.  

Then, a car pulled up outside. Her heart pounded.  

Knock, knock.  

“Viscount, My Lady. Your guest has arrived.”  

The door opened but it was not the Duke.  

Instead, an elderly gentleman with white hair bowed politely.  

“A pleasure to meet you. I am Paul Miller, legal counsel of Maxville.”  

Roselyn stared at him in confusion.

The lawyer was alone. Carrying a neat briefcase, he entered the family room alone. And so, Roselyn quickly realized what this situation meant.

“Mr. Miller. We were informed that the Duke of Windburg would be coming personally.”  

Alfred Fairfield welcomed the guest naturally. Roselyn felt grateful for her father’s gentlemanly demeanor, but at the same time, she felt guilty.  

“That is correct, Viscount. The Duke was indeed scheduled to visit today to discuss the engagement. However, due to urgent official matters, he was unable to come in person. He asks for your understanding that I am here as his representative.”  

Urgent official matters. Just how urgent were they that he would send a proxy to propose? Roselyn felt at a loss about how to respond to this unusual situation. A proxy proposal? That was a custom that had disappeared after the medieval era.  

“I see. If that was the case, he could have simply sent a telegram to postpone the visit.”  

“The Duke wishes to proceed with the marriage as soon as possible. Given the already tight schedule, delaying it even a day would be difficult. His Grace did consider rescheduling, but ultimately, he decided that confirming the engagement now and proceeding with the subsequent arrangements would be the most efficient course of action.”  

“Well, that does make sense…”  

The Viscount, though displeased by this inconsiderate proposal, did not reject it outright. Nor did he accept it with gratitude. He merely looked at his daughter, and from that, Roselyn understood that he was leaving the decision to her. So, once again, the choice was hers.  

She did not know exactly why the Duke had not come. She had no idea what could be more urgent than an engagement. But one thing was clear; she was not a priority to him.  

“Lady Fairfield has a habit of jumping to conclusions.”

The Duke was sending her a message: I have no intention of accommodating you. I do not desperately want you, nor are you someone I absolutely must have. So don’t assume things or expect too much.

Faced with that cold implication, Roselyn’s heart froze for a moment. That alone was reason enough to reject him. Could she endure this? Was it worth it? Could she be happy with someone who did not put her first?  

And yet.  

“Did Lady Fairfield not wish to marry me?”

Surprisingly, Roselyn could not easily let go. She had already envisioned herself standing by his side too vividly to give up. The future she had pictured was now so close, within reach, and she could not bring herself to let it slip away. Her determination was firmer than even she had expected.  

There was still hope. Perhaps time was all that was needed. After marriage, as they got to know each other better, affection might grow. Her parents, too, had not married out of passionate love, yet they had been happy in the end.  

Roselyn convinced herself with optimism. Perhaps she truly did want to become the Duchess. More than she had ever realized.  

“Of course, Mr. Miller. I completely understand.”  

And so, the decision was made. Roselyn smiled graciously; poised, elegant, as befitting a future duchess.  

“I imagine the Duke has many official duties that take precedence over personal matters. Whatever it is, I hope it is resolved smoothly.”  

“Of course, my lady. Thank you for your understanding.”  

The lawyer bowed deeply. His polite and respectful demeanor somewhat soothed the father and daughter’s unease. Hearing his daughter’s decision, the Viscount took the lead in lightening the mood.  

“Oh dear, I’ve been so rude, making you stand here all this time. Let’s have a seat, Mr. Miller. Would you care for some tea?”  

“I deeply appreciate your hospitality, Viscount, but it would be far too generous for someone like me.”  

The lawyer declined politely, offering a modest smile. Though an experienced and accomplished gentleman, he was still a servant of the Duke. It was not his place to sit and drink tea with his master’s fiancée.  

“First, I would like to present this to you, my lady.”  

Still standing, the lawyer opened his briefcase and took something out. A small, cube-shaped box wrapped in black velvet. His expression showed neither hesitation nor regret as he handed it over.  

And yet, Roselyn felt ashamed. Ashamed of the man who had entrusted even the engagement ring to someone else. Ashamed of herself for not rejecting such a proposal. No matter how much she tried to remain composed, emotions were not so easily controlled. She could not completely suppress the disappointment and self-reproach.  

So even after she finally reached out and accepted the ring case, she hesitated to open it, embarrassed.  

“Please, take a look.”  

Only after the lawyer’s gentle prompting did she open the case. Inside was a white diamond ring. The gemstone was about the size of her index fingernail, surrounded by smaller stones that enhanced its brilliance. Elegant and refined without being overly flashy.  

“…It’s beautiful.”  

“This is the oldest diamond in the Duke’s collection. Though the design was recently updated, if it does not suit your taste, it can be reset before the wedding.”  

“That won’t be necessary. I like it just as it is.”  

“I’m glad to hear that, my lady.”  

The lawyer smiled, and Roselyn returned a faint smile of her own. Then, lowering her gaze, she looked at the ring again. Holding the open case in her hand, she hesitated, unable to close it, unable to take the ring out.  

The lawyer simply waited in silence. He did not urge her to try it on. Paul Miller, the Duke’s legal advisor, was an experienced servant who knew better than to press the matter.  

“Well then, it seems it is time to discuss the specifics with you, Viscount.”  

The elderly gentleman turned toward the bride’s father with a now-relaxed expression. The “specifics” likely referred to the dowry. Normally, this would also be the moment when a father offered words of advice and blessing to his future son-in-law. It saddened Roselyn that her father would be denied that joy.  

“Of course, Mr. Miller. Let’s move to my study.”  

“Thank you.”  

“We’ll excuse ourselves for a moment.”  

Alfred Fairfield smiled at his daughter; a look that carried both congratulations and consolation. Meeting his gaze, Roselyn gave a similar expression in return. After he stepped out of the room, the Duke’s representative bowed to her.  

“Congratulations on your engagement.”  

Roselyn nodded lightly in acknowledgment, watching the two men leave the room. And then, she was alone.  

For a while, she remained motionless, neither closing the ring case nor taking out the ring. As if, by not wearing it, she could somehow delay the engagement itself.  

Only after several minutes had passed did she finally reach out to touch the jewel. She took the engagement ring out and slid it onto the fourth finger of her left hand. It fit perfectly. That precise fit offered a small, bitter comfort.  

The oldest diamond in the Glenn family’s collection sparkled as if it were new. Roselyn ran her opposite hand over the ring, unfamiliar with the feeling of it on her finger. It would take time to get used to.  

Gazing at her mother’s portrait, she wondered.  

“Rose, my sweet daughter.”

Would her mother be happy? Roselyn was certain she would be. After all, the Fairfield family was now connected to a duke. Her father would be the father-in-law of a duke. There was no doubt; this was the right decision.  

Yes, the right decision.  

Roselyn whispered the words to herself, glancing around the now-empty family room. She touched the ring on her left hand. Her unease was natural. Had she rejected the proposal, she would have had to endure a much greater anxiety.  

So yes, it was the right decision.  

Feeling a strange mixture of emotions, she looked around the room once more, knowing she would soon leave it behind.  

She would no longer be the eldest daughter of the Fairfield house but the mistress of Maxville. A duchess. A wife. A daughter-in-law. And in time, inevitably, a mother.  

A heavy weight settled on her chest. But as a woman, it was a path she could not avoid.  

She could do it. She would do it.  

Roselyn took a deep breath and lifted the corners of her mouth. Soon, her siblings would rush in to celebrate. She had to be ready.  

Ready to acknowledge that this engagement was a joyous occasion. Ready to receive their warm congratulations.

The wedding was officially set for June 27th.  

Invitations were sent out, and news articles were published. Newspapers across the empire widely covered the Duke of Windburg’s wedding announcement, analyzing that it was undeniably a love match, given that his fiancée came from a relatively unremarkable family.  

A Duke choosing to marry for love was nearly unheard of in noble society. Even so, Maxville made no attempt to refute the absurd reports. His press officer, in fact, welcomed the misunderstanding.  

Beyond being young and handsome, Duke Jared was a figure of public fascination due to the dramatic circumstances surrounding his succession.  

In an era where fame was a valuable asset, the romantic narrative of a love match only enhanced the Duke’s public image. It could even be leveraged to benefit businesses associated with Maxville.  

Recognizing this strategic advantage, the press officer went as far as to propose an unconventional idea: releasing select wedding photographs to the media. After internal discussions, the proposal reached the Duke, who, like always, approved it without much resistance. Jared was a good employer who valued his subordinates’ opinions.  

While Maxville was busy preparing for the grand wedding, Fairfield House remained relatively quiet.  

The wedding date had been set immediately upon the engagement, exactly six weeks away. However, since the bride was requested to arrive in Maxville a week prior, only five weeks remained.  

During that time, a designer sent by the Duchess had visited Fairfield House twice, accompanied by a group of assistants. Not only was a wedding dress crafted, but also six evening gowns and two new outfits for Andrea. Roselyn didn’t dare refuse the future mother-in-law’s generous gifts.  

Apart from that, there was little else for the bride’s family to prepare. Roselyn arranged for their family’s tailor to make new formal attire for her father and two younger brothers. She also took it upon herself to instill a sense of responsibility in Andrea, the last remaining Fairfield daughter.  

She debated whether to give special instructions to the butler and head maid regarding Lady Selma but ultimately decided against it. It wouldn’t do to undermine the new Viscountess’s authority before she even settled in.  

Then, on the day one week before the wedding, the car arrived to take the bride away.  

“Victor, you’ll have to take care of our siblings now.”  

“Don’t worry. They’re practically adults already.”  

“I’d appreciate it if you remembered that they aren’t quite there yet. Andy, don’t forget to write me once a week. At least two pages, understand?”  

“Leave some nagging for Luke too, sis. I’ll pass it on when he comes home for the weekend.”  

“You’re unbelievable, Roselyn. You still treat us like we’re ten. Besides, what’s the point of letters when we’ll see each other next week?”  

Her younger siblings waved their hands dismissively at her worries. Even Victor and Andrea looked unbothered, smiling easily despite the fact that their sister was leaving home for good. Roselyn found herself a little hurt by their indifference.  

She had always taught them that displaying emotions was an undignified habit. But now, seeing their composure, she regretted it.  

In contrast, the household staff seemed far more saddened by her departure. Tender, the cook, dabbed at her tears with her apron, while Mary, the maid, stood with a red-tipped nose. Unlike nobles, they did not hesitate to show their emotions.  

“Hurry along, Rose. They’re waiting,” her father said.  

Roselyn turned to see two cars and three attendants lined up. Her luggage had already been loaded; four boxes of clothes, shoes, and hats, along with two travel bags.  

Twenty-four years in this house, and all she was taking with her were a few belongings.  

“I’ll be going now, Father.”  

“Travel safely.”  

“I’ll see you next week.”  

“I look forward to it.”  

Roselyn kissed her father and siblings on the cheek before shaking hands with each of the staff members. Finally, she turned to take one last look at the grand estate before stepping into the car.  

The Duke’s attendants treated their future mistress with the utmost respect, which pleased the Fairfield servants.  

And so, the ‘Viscountess of Andover’ left Fairfield House.  

A young girl who had lost her mother too soon was now becoming a Duchess, bringing honor to her family, a story as beautiful as a fairy tale.  



Maxville was about a twenty-minute drive from the train station. Unlike most noble estates, which were typically secluded in the countryside, the Duke’s residence was surprisingly close to the city. The location was both prestigious and convenient.  

Through the car window, Roselyn watched the dense evergreen forest pass by. The fading sunlight filtered through the thick trees. This road, cutting through the forest, was the only route connecting the mansion to the city of Windburg; a detail the Duke had casually shared during their last dinner gathering.  

As she thought of the Duke, Roselyn found herself absentmindedly touching her engagement ring again. It had become a habit, rubbing the ring on her left ring finger with her right hand. Even after five weeks, the unfamiliar weight still felt foreign, almost embarrassing.  

The reality of her engagement hadn’t fully settled in yet, despite the fact that she was en route to his home, in his car.  

A sudden wave of uncertainty washed over her, and she took a deep breath. Just then, the stone facade of the Duke’s mansion came into view.  

The car passed through the towering black iron gate, which resembled the entrance of a grand aviary. The wrought-iron fence, adorned with gilded spikes, encircled the entire estate.  

As the vehicle approached, the gates opened, and two uniformed guards saluted, rifles in hand. A level of formality she hadn’t received on her previous visits.  

Even after entering the estate, the car continued down a long driveway. As it passed the meticulously manicured lawns, Roselyn kept her eyes on the entrance of the mansion.  

Would he be there to greet her? Or had he once again delegated the task to someone else?  

This was their first meeting since the engagement was announced. She didn’t want to endure another slight.  

Thankfully, it seemed her fiancé did not regard this marriage with such indifference.  

A welcoming party awaited her at the entrance. The Duchess, the head butler, a middle-aged woman who appeared to be the housekeeper, and over a dozen staff members stood in formation. At the very front stood the Duke himself.  

His composed posture and impeccable appearance reassured Roselyn, despite herself.  

As the car came to a stop, a valet stepped forward to open the door. Roselyn gracefully stepped out and offered a practiced smile. She met the Duke’s gaze with unwavering poise.  

Shy, but confident. Like a true fiancée.  

The Duke simply looked at her without moving. The distance between them made conversation impossible, and he showed no intention of stepping forward. It was only natural that she would be the one to approach.  

Maintaining her composed expression, Roselyn walked towards the welcoming party. The engagement ring on her left hand felt all the more noticeable. The very ring she had willingly accepted. The proof of her formal betrothal.  

Yet, it felt more like a mark of shame.  

And the man who had made her feel that way stood there, utterly unbothered.  

“Welcome, Roselyn,” he said.  

Roselyn.  

She hesitated for a brief moment but did not let it show. Should she call him by his name as well?  

After a second’s consideration, she decided against it. She wasn’t ready for that yet.  

“You look well, Your Grace. I’m glad to see you.”  

“The pleasure is mine.”  

“Thank you for sending the car.”  

“It was only proper.”  

The formal greetings proceeded smoothly. Roselyn then turned to the next most senior figure.  

“Your Grace, Duchess.”  

“Call me Mother,” said Diane Glenn warmly.  

Roselyn, having bowed respectfully, met the Duchess’s gaze.  

“There’s no need for formality. We are family now. I am truly delighted you’re here, Roselyn.”  

“Thank you for your warm welcome.”  

“Of course, dear.”  

The Duchess smiled, her eyes crinkling with affection.  

“Come inside. While your things are being settled, I’ll show you around. There are many people to meet, and much to discuss! Forgive me if I seem overly eager, but I simply cannot contain my joy today.”  

“Of course, Mother. I’m looking forward to it.”  

Roselyn returned her smile, then turned towards the Duke.  

“If it’s not too much trouble, may I ask Your Grace to guide me through the estate?”

Even as their eyes met, Roselyn couldn’t discern what lay behind his gaze. If he had hesitated, smiled awkwardly, or shown any sign of discomfort, she might have been able to make a guess. But she didn’t get the chance because the Duchess spoke first.  


“That’s a wonderful idea. Jared, wouldn’t it be a thoughtful gesture to show your fiancée around the house yourself today? Of course, she won’t be able to see everything at once.”  


Diane Glen turned to her son, leaving him little room to refuse both his fiancée’s request and his mother’s suggestion.  


“Of course, Mother.”  


As expected, the Duke accepted with ease, turning to Roselyn.  


“Let’s start with the west wing. Since it’s sunset, you’ll be able to see a nice view. The sky should be beautiful in this clear weather.”  


“Enjoy your time. Tell me at dinner just how charming of a guide your fiancé was.”  


With a pleased expression, the Duchess led the way inside, followed by the household staff. The remaining servants were busy unloading the luggage from the cars. Jared briefly glanced at them before looking back at Roselyn.  


“Shall we?”  


With gentlemanly courtesy, he gestured for her to enter first. Roselyn responded with a smile before stepping through the grand entrance into the main hall. Towering ceilings, low-hanging chandeliers; this was her third visit to the mansion, and it was beginning to feel somewhat familiar.  


“This way.”  


Jared led her to the left, down the western corridor, where portraits began to appear. Lining the right-hand wall were the faces of those long past, framed in ornate gold, each labeled with a name and lifespan beneath.  


“This is the Portrait Gallery. A hall dedicated to our ancestors and other notable figures.”  


He introduced it in an even tone as they walked. From his father, Duke Philip, to monarchs painted on wooden panels centuries ago, walking through this hall felt like stepping through time. Roselyn had been here before.  


“It’s an impressive gallery. I saw it last time during the ball.”  


“That makes sense. The Great Hall is at the end of this corridor. Since guests have to pass through here, it’s an ideal place to flaunt our family’s history.”  


He smiled, but it was laced with irony.  


Roselyn wasn’t sure whether she should laugh along, so she hesitated. Fortunately, he wasn’t looking at her.  


A brief silence followed before she spoke again.  


“This must be Duke Irving.”  


She stopped in front of a particular painting, prompting Jared to pause beside her. Complimenting an esteemed ancestor was usually a welcomed topic among nobility.  


“Do you admire him?”  


“He was the first Duke of Windburg, wasn’t he? I also love the statue of him in Irving Square. He looks so kind and benevolent.”  


“I see. But since you’re about to become part of this family, there’s something you should know. Irving isn’t a particularly respected figure among us.”  


“Why not?”  


“He submitted to the Empire without a fight.”  


Jared’s answer was immediate. Roselyn pressed her lips together, glancing at him.  


The Trissen Empire had originally been a southern kingdom, expanding northward three centuries ago to establish an empire.  


The Glen family had once ruled over the northern lands as a grand feudal house. Instead of waging war, Irving Glen had surrendered to the advancing emperor and was granted the title of duke in return. His decision, meant to spare civilians from bloodshed, was still widely respected, enough for the city to dedicate a square to his memory.  


“Our family isn’t particularly fond of the Imperial family. Only three of our dukes, including him and his son, were moderates. The rest were all hardliners.”  


“Which side are you on, Your Grace?”  


“Who knows?”  


He didn’t answer. He didn’t even turn it into a teasing challenge for her to guess.  


“You’ll find out in time.”  


He smiled faintly, his expression unreadable, then resumed walking.  


Roselyn followed quietly. More portraits lined the walls; men in crowns, men in armor. Jared never once spared them a glance.  


He remained silent, occasionally opening doors on the left to show her various rooms. The drawing room, the tea room, the music room; each uniquely elegant.  


What about the rooms he didn’t open? What lay in the eastern wing? The upper floors? The idea of exploring this vast, historic estate filled Roselyn with a quiet thrill.  


“The family quarters are on the second floor, and guest rooms are on the third. There is one master suite for only the most distinguished guests. Until the wedding, you will be staying there.”  


The most distinguished guest.  


Something about that phrase stirred her emotions. By the time she collected her thoughts, they had reached the entrance to the Great Hall.  


Jared pushed open one of the grand arched doors, allowing her to enter first.  


The vast ballroom was empty. The orchestra’s platform stood bare, the once vibrant space now eerily silent. Without the tables, chairs, and revelry of the ball, the space felt even larger than she remembered.  


Roselyn slowly walked into the vacant hall.  


Golden twilight streamed through the west-facing windows and glass doors, illuminating the artwork along the opposite wall. It felt entirely different from the night of the ball; no music, no people, no laughter.  


She turned toward the west.  


The red glow of sunset streaked above the distant treetops, its radiance swiftly fading into the encroaching dusk.  


“It’s beautiful,” she murmured.  


“A decent view,” Jared agreed.  


He stepped up beside her, close enough for her to catch the faint, rich scent of his cologne.  


“This is the largest hall in the mansion. The wedding reception will be held here. The ceremony itself will take place at the chapel on the estate. I’ll take you there soon, it’s practically an ancient relic, only used for weddings and funerals.”  


He spoke with the same casual tone for weddings and funerals.  


Listening to his low voice, Roselyn debated whether this was the right time to bring it up.  


‘The Duke was supposed to visit today to discuss the engagement, but an urgent matter arose, preventing him from coming in person.’  


Ideally, he would explain first. But he showed no inclination to do so.  


If she waited any longer, it would only become harder to broach the subject.  


She couldn’t just let it go. She wouldn’t.  


So she gathered her courage.  


“The urgent matter that day, was it resolved?”  


She stared at the darkening forest, acutely aware of the ring on her left hand.  


Jared didn’t answer immediately, caught off guard by her sudden question. But he understood her meaning right away because he didn’t hesitate for long.  


“It wasn’t bad. My fiancée wished for a smooth resolution, and it seems that worked in my favor.”  


There was a slight amusement in his tone. Enough that Roselyn turned her head and met his gaze.  


“I forgot to express my gratitude. Thank you for your understanding.”  


“Understanding your situation doesn’t mean my family and I weren’t hurt by it.”  


“I can imagine. I appreciate your patience.”  


He nodded, accepting her words as natural but without offering an apology.  


“May I ask what it was about?”  


“I’m afraid I can’t share that.”  


“…I see.”  


