The weather was clear, and the afternoon sun shone brightly, there was no reason not to walk. Northerners, accustomed to long winters and harsh cold, had an ingrained habit of savoring the sunlight whenever possible.  

 

The only person who could not enjoy the beautiful day was the bride.  

 

Roselyn had to travel by car from the main building. With her crinoline-supported gown puffed up extravagantly and her veil draped over her shoulders, walking was out of the question. There was no way she could risk getting a speck of dust on the pristine white wedding dress, which had been crafted over five weeks by Windburg’s finest designer.  

 

Draped in layers of white, the flowing gown, the veil, the satin shoes, Roselyn sat in the waiting room.  

 

“Did you know? A bride who marries in June is destined to be happy,” Andrea said, gently stroking the roses in a vase.  

 

The waiting room assigned to the sisters was a small chapel annex repurposed as a bridal chamber, filled with vases overflowing with roses, their scent thick in the air.  

 

“Everyone calls you the ‘Bride of Roses,’ you know. June is the season of roses, and your name means ‘rose.’ Maybe that’s why they decorated the entire venue with them. You wouldn’t believe how beautiful the ceremony hall is. You’ll be amazed when you see it!”  

 

“Really? Now I’m looking forward to it.”  

 

“You can look forward to it all you want.”  

 

Andrea confidently reassured her, beaming. She had not left her sister’s side the entire time.  

 

“The Imperial Princess is here too, you know.”  

 

“You mean the Marchioness of Dazzley?”  

 

“Yes. She’s the Emperor’s aunt, right? Then again, my brother-in-law is the Emperor’s cousin. Ahh, this is just unbelievable! My sister is going to be a Duchess! Your name is in the newspapers, can you believe it?”  

 

Andrea exaggerated her astonishment as if she were performing a play. She giggled as she playfully shook Roselyn’s shoulders.  

 

Watching her sister’s excitement, Roselyn allowed herself a small smile.  

 

Her vision was slightly obscured by the veil covering her face, but she would have to keep it on until her groom lifted it himself.

 In stark contrast to the extensive newspaper coverage, the wedding itself was not a grand affair. Only about three hundred carefully selected guests had received invitations from the ducal household.  

 

Roselyn immediately understood what “carefully selected” meant the moment she saw the guest list. It was a meticulously curated selection of the empire’s highest-ranking nobility. Among them, eight bore the imperial surname.  

 

She tried to take comfort in the fact that such distinguished figures had gathered to witness her wedding, and that amidst them were her father and younger brothers.  

 

Some of the guests made their way to visit the bride. Among them were the Count and Countess of Benford, who arrived with their two daughters, the designated flower girls. The Count held his youngest son in his arms, while the Countess busied herself adjusting her daughters’ outfits, offering them words of encouragement.  

 

A devoted husband and their beloved children. As Roselyn watched Victoria surrounded by her family, she sincerely wished: 

 

May our marriage be like theirs. May I have such a happy future too.  

 

After the lively family departed, leaving only the sisters behind, a new visitor arrived in the waiting room.  

 

“Hello, Duchess.”  

 

A bright and youthful face. It was Josephine Hayes.  

 

“Lady Josephine, thank you for coming.”  

 

“Congratulations on your wedding, Miss Fairfield. Or should I still call you that for now? Losing one’s family name must feel a little bittersweet.”  

 

Josephine’s cheerful tone carried no malice, just pure excitement and delight.  

 

From the moment they first spoke at the First Lady’s tea party, Roselyn had taken a liking to the young lady. Having just come of age, Josephine was like a younger sister to her.  

 

“This is my second eldest brother.”  

 

“Russell Hayes. Congratulations on your wedding, Your Grace.”  

 

“Thank you for coming, my lord.”  

 

Roselyn inclined her head slightly in greeting to the Duke of Mendel’s second son.  

 

“My father sent his congratulations as well. I already conveyed them to the Duke of Windburg, but I must deliver them to you personally. May you both find happiness and may the House of Glen prosper.”  

 

“Thank you. Please extend my sincere gratitude to the Duke of Mendel.”  

 

“Forgive me, Miss Fairfield. My brother is wonderful, but he’s far too formal. Sometimes, he sounds like an old man.”  

 

Josephine playfully interjected, and Russell simply smiled without refuting her. He must be fond of his sister, Roselyn mused as she shifted the conversation back to the young lady.  

 

“You only recently returned from Mendel, yet you traveled all this way for the wedding.”  

 

“It didn’t feel far at all. I’ve been looking forward to this day! As soon as the ceremony is over, I’ll be heading to Isen.”  

 

Josephine paused momentarily, then, as if unable to hold it in any longer, burst out excitedly,  

 

“I’m going to university!”  

 

Roselyn was taken aback.  

 

“A young lady of noble birth, attending university?”  

 

“I made a deal with my father. In exchange for attending the Duke of Windburg’s ball, I asked that if I didn’t receive a marriage proposal, he would make a donation and allow me to enroll. When I heard that you two were engaged, I was thrilled! My father, on the other hand, was probably less so.”  

 

Josephine lowered her voice mischievously.  

 

“But that part about my father is a secret.”  

 

“A secret you just revealed is no longer a secret, Jo.”  

 

“Miss Fairfield is a lady. Of course, she’ll keep it.”  

 

Russell looked exasperated, clearly struggling to handle his younger sister. Roselyn understood the feeling all too well.  

 

“My apologies, Your Grace. My sister can be quite thoughtless.”  

 

“Not at all, my lord. That’s incredible, Lady Josephine. You’ll be a university student!”  

 

“I start in September. I want to study history. I plan to earn a doctorate and become a scholar.”  

 

“And marriage?”  

 

Andrea asked, prompting Josephine to tilt her head.  

 

“Is marriage necessary?”  

 

The two young ladies exchanged glances, while Roselyn observed them through the veil.  

 

“I have no desire to marry. I don’t want to spend my days idly waiting for the social season in my husband’s mansion. That sounds terribly dull, doesn’t it?”  

 

Russell’s expression finally turned serious. Such words, spoken before a bride on her wedding day, were hardly appropriate.  

 

Yet Roselyn was less concerned about Josephine’s indiscretion and more about her brother’s obvious discomfort. She sought a way to smooth over the situation.  

 

“Excuse me.”  

 

Fortunately, Edith entered at that moment.  

 

“The ceremony is about to begin.”  

 

With that, the Hayes siblings had a convenient excuse to take their leave. She’s going to get scolded by her brother as soon as they step outside, Roselyn thought with an amused smirk, adjusting her veil.  

 

“Is marriage necessary?”

 

The ceremony was about to begin.  

 

“Do your best, Sister.”  

 

Leaving those words of encouragement, Andrea exited, and only Edith remained. She would escort the bride once the guests were seated and the venue was ready. Viscount Andover would be waiting at the entrance to give his daughter away, while the Duke of Windburg would receive his bride inside the chapel.  

 

As she mentally ran through the procedures, Roselyn gazed at her reflection in the mirror. Her face, obscured by the white veil, felt strangely unfamiliar.  

 

In the windowless waiting room, her only company was the attendant.  

 

And roses. Hundreds of roses, placed in vases throughout the space.  

 

“Everyone calls you the Bride of Roses.”  

 

She became keenly aware of the flowers surrounding her. Only now did she notice their overwhelming fragrance. It was likely due to the confined space. The ceremony hall would be larger, so the scent would be more pleasant.  

 

“I do not like roses.”  

 

Even on her wedding day, she had yet to see the groom. Andrea had mentioned that he looked splendid in his ceremonial attire, exactly as he appeared in the newspapers.  

 

Perhaps it was due to tradition that they hadn’t met before the ceremony. Or perhaps it was because he had chosen not to. She didn’t dwell on the latter possibility, if he had wanted to see her, he would have come.  

 

Jared was not one to be bound by tradition.  

 

He was not the type to strictly follow rules; if anything, he was cynical about them. It was ironic that the head of an ancient noble house, with a thousand years of history, possessed a rebellious streak. Just as it was contradictory that a third-born son had become the head of the family.  

 

Jared Glen was full of contradictions. Those inconsistencies unsettled Roselyn.  

 

It was like realizing, too late, that words she had taken as jokes were, in fact, cynicism.  

 

A chill ran down her shoulders. A sudden sense of loneliness crept in. She found herself dwelling on his words, his actions, his expressions.  

 

“If it’s truly irreparable, then we’ll think about it then.”  

 

What expression had he worn when he said that?  

 

“We’ll need a lot of practice, that’s for sure.”  

 

Was that more of a joke? Or cynicism?  

 

From within the veil, Roselyn bit her lip. She clasped her cold fingers together, trying to shake off the unease. She couldn’t understand why she felt so shaken. Why, at this moment, everything seemed like a bad omen.  

 

Would he be different as a fiancé?  

 

How much more would he change as a husband?  

 

“My lady, it’s time to go.”  

 

Edith checked the time. The moment Roselyn signed the marriage contract, the attendant had started addressing her as “my lady.” Legally, she was already Jared Glenn’s wife, the Duchess of Windburg.  

 

Without a word, Roselyn rose. Beneath her veil, she took a deep breath.  

 

There is no turning back now.  

 

It was natural to feel uneasy before such a life-changing event. Steeling herself, she carefully lifted the voluminous dress and calmly stepped forward.  

 



 

Jared was surprised by how little the groom had to do at a wedding.  

 

Throughout the ceremony, his responsibilities were minimal; receiving the bride from Viscount Andover and escorting her to the officiant.  

 

After reciting the vows read by Bishop Seamus and exchanging rings, the bishop declared their union sealed. Legally and now religiously, their marriage was complete.  

 

His most important duty was the final step; the highlight of the wedding.  

 

Lifting the bride’s veil and kissing her before an audience of hundreds.  

 

It was, in a way, a preview of their wedding night.  

 

A ridiculous tradition, but one that could not be ignored.  

 

So Jared kissed her, deliberately, for all to see.  

 

Their lips touched briefly. He held the moment for two seconds, then pulled away.  

 

It was, ultimately, a simple task. Nothing more than holding his breath for a few moments.

At that moment, she, too, seemed to have held her breath. Through their touching lips, he felt a faint tremor. Even as the applause of the guests erupted and he opened his eyes, she remained with hers shut, her long lashes gently resting against her cheeks.  

 

And when she finally opened them, looking up at him, Jared realized, just a little too late, that in the sunlight streaming through the chapel’s stained-glass windows, her gray eyes shone like silver. Just like the veil he had only moments ago lifted from her face.  

 

Facing those eyes, Jared once again held his breath, amidst the storm of applause.  

 



 

After the ceremony, they cut the wedding cake and toasted with champagne in the garden reception. Greeting each guest with his bride took considerable time and energy.  

 

By the time they finally arrived at the banquet hall, Jared had all but lost his appetite. He forced himself to eat a modest amount before moving to the Great Hall at around 9 PM. The wedding had started at 4 PM, five long hours had passed.  

 

“Are you tired?”  

 

Jared spoke to the woman focused on their dance.  

 

They were now in the middle of their third waltz with the guests at the reception. The slow-paced Andante required intricate movements, making casual conversation difficult. But since they had practiced for today, it posed no real challenge.  

 

His bride met his gaze without flinching.  

 

“I’m fine.”  

 

“Then smile. Happily.”  

 

Jared curved his lips in a demonstration.  

 

“The photographer seems to want that.”  

 

At his words, Roselyn subtly glanced toward the photographer before returning her gaze to him. Under the hazy glow of the chandeliers, her eyes had returned to their usual gray.  

 

Calm, composed gray eyes.  

 

Jared maintained his smile as he held her gaze. She mirrored his expression. A flash went off.  

 

I hope the photographer is satisfied.  

 

Jared smoothly transitioned into the next movement, crossing arms with his partner.  

 

He wondered if modern cameras could capture movement accurately now. He had no idea how much the latest models had improved.  

 

Technology had brought both convenience and nuisance. Automobiles were undoubtedly more practical than carriages, but cameras were far more intrusive than painted portraits. Those dmned machines were dragging the nobles of high society further into the public eye. Ever since photographs had started appearing in newspapers, a lady’s beauty had become more important than ever.  

 

If one could not resist the tide of change, it was wise to make the best use of it.  

 

“Are they really going to send those photos to the newspapers?”  

 

“My attendants believe it would be beneficial.”  

 

“And do you agree?”  

 

“Yes.”  

 

Jared answered firmly, wrapping his arm around his partner’s waist. The sudden closeness pressed their bodies together, her gown brushing against his legs.  

 

“Why? Does it bother you?”  

 

He stepped forward, drawing her closer. She didn’t lower her gaze. In this movement, they had to look directly into each other’s eyes. Jared found the Andante’s intricate steps rather entertaining.  

 

“If you believe it’s necessary, then I don’t mind.”  

 

He looked down at her demure response. ‘I don’t mind’ was a polite way of saying ‘I don’t like it, but I’ll accept it.’ That was a response he could live with.  

 

“Good.”  

 

It would be even better if things continued this way. He withdrew his hand from her waist.  

 

The Andante flowed smoothly. The man extended his partner’s hand, spinning her outward before letting go momentarily as she twirled in place. The fabric of her dress billowed like a blooming flower.  

 

In the sea of people, the white of her wedding gown stood out unmistakably. Roselyn had kept that dress pristine for over five hours; perfectly white, perfectly poised.  

 

Keeping such a woman by his side was no loss. In fact, it was an advantage. She might shine brighter than his mother, perhaps even outshine his sister-in-law as the most admired duchess.  

 

If he was to surpass his father and brother as Duke, she certainly wouldn’t be a hindrance.  

 

“With all the attention on this “love match,” the wedding itself is receiving great interest. If you leverage this wisely, it will indirectly benefit your businesses. And directly boost sales at the department stores.”

 

This was the golden age, money was more powerful than anything else. The Glen family had absorbed fallen nobles and accumulated thirteen noble titles. It was the only family today that could rival the imperial house, all because of wealth.  

 

Jared’s wise predecessors had understood that securing gold was the key to maintaining power. As a third son without an inheritance, Jared had learned firsthand the force of money. Dignity, honor, and self-respect–at their core, they all depended on wealth.  

 

More than anything, he needed a goal. He couldn’t spend his life aimlessly guarding this mansion like some powerless watchdog. If he was going to be a duke, he might as well be the most accomplished one.  

 

“The future duchess is a beauty. If you utilize your relationship well, you can maintain the public’s interest for a long time.”

 

Jared had agreed with his aide. His fiancée was a beautiful woman. That night at his ball, there had been other women worthy of being called beauties, but he could no longer recall a single one of their faces.  

 

Had he simply chosen the most beautiful one? How very intellectual of him.  

 

Jared smirked at his own self-awareness and turned his gaze to the woman who had forced him to reevaluate his reasoning.  

 

Tonight, Roselyn was particularly stunning. The guests’ praises of her beauty were not mere flattery. While her bridal adornments played a part, Jared thought the true allure came from her gown—specifically, the way it bared her shoulders.  

 

The deep neckline emphasized her elegant neck. The prominence of her collarbones and the delicate structure of her shoulders exuded sensuality. A few stray strands of her updo had come loose, drawing his attention.  

 

Her fine, straight brown hair was so smooth it looked like it would slip through his fingers like sand.  

 

As he studied her, Jared found himself wondering, why had such a woman never married until now? Was it solely because of her responsibilities to her younger siblings?  

 

He was curious, but he wouldn’t ask. She would only turn the question back on him.  

 

And that was a topic he had no desire to discuss, ever.  

 

The slow, intricate dance continued. Jared caught her hand as she twirled back toward him, briefly pulling her into his arms before releasing her for the next movement. Their eyes met once more.  

 

“You look beautiful tonight.”  

 

He smiled.  

 

“With such a stunning bride, it would be a shame not to show off a little.”  

 

Like a man in love.  

 

“Smile, Roselyn.”  

 

Happily.  

 

Jared knew she was the true star of today. The wedding’s central figure, the one everyone wanted to see, was not the groom but the bride. The young and beautiful duchess, dressed in white.  

 

The woman loved by the Duke.  

 

Flash.  

 

A camera went off. Jared hoped the photographer had captured the perfect shot. If it added more value to this marriage, all the better.  

 

“You look a little tired as well, Your Grace.”  

 

Roselyn spoke cautiously, observing his face. He thought he had mastered the art of concealing exhaustion. Perhaps she only said it to highlight her own attentiveness or simply to fill the silence as they danced.  

 

“I’m fine, don’t worry. You must be exhausted.”  

 

“I’m fine, too.”  

 

She smiled bravely, and Jared returned the gesture.  

 

Roselyn had not made a single mistake today. She had played the perfect bride for five hours straight.  

 

Yet, for some inexplicable reason, watching her maintain such flawless composure left a bitter taste in Jared’s mouth.  

 

Unwavering grace. The epitome of nobility.  

 

It annoyed him.  

 

“I’m glad.”  

 

He feigned a warm smile, forcing himself to find comfort in the thought. It was fortunate, after all.  

 

The most important part of the wedding was yet to come.  

 

One final group dance, and the ball would conclude.  

 

Then, the two of them would retire to the bridal chamber.  

 

And it would be problematic if his bride was too exhausted before their wedding night had even begun.

The master suite of the ducal couple was composed of four main spaces: the outer sitting room, the inner bedroom, and two separate dressing rooms, each with its own entrance and bathroom, designated for the Duke and Duchess to prepare themselves.  

 

It was in her dressing room that Roselyn removed her wedding dress. She soaked in a hot bath for a long while before slipping into a négligée and robe. The peach-colored silk nightgown, adorned with lace, was a gift from the designer who had crafted her dress. She had mentioned it was currently fashionable among young noblewomen.  

 

The nightwear was too elaborate, making her self-conscious, but the designer had insisted, “The wedding night is meant to be special.” And Roselyn did want this first night with her husband to be something special.  

 

She repeatedly adjusted the unfamiliar garment, took several deep breaths, and finally stepped into the bedroom.  

 

Despite all her mental preparations, the room was empty.  

 

Someone had left the lights on, but there was no one in sight. Two armchairs sat before the darkened fireplace, clearly new. When she turned to the other side of the room, she saw something even more unexpected.  

 

Two beds.  

 

A husband and wife sleeping in the same bed was common sense. People even referred to marriage as sharing a bed.  

 

She had been told this room had belonged solely to the Duke before their marriage, so she had naturally assumed there would be a single, large bed. She had even felt a vague excitement at the thought of sharing that bed with him.  

 

So, what was she supposed to make of this sight?  

 

Which one was hers? She hesitated but found no clues, both beds were identical. The bedside tables were empty, offering no indication. Unsure of what to do, Roselyn turned toward her vanity, the only furniture she was certain belonged to her.  

 

Sitting before the mirror, she hesitated before picking up a bottle of toner. As she applied it to her face, she listened intently, but she heard no approaching footsteps. She hurriedly opened the jar of cream, but even after finishing her skincare routine, he still hadn’t arrived.  

 

Now, she sat alone at the vanity, battling an onslaught of thoughts. Her fingers felt cold with a vague sense of dread. Though it was late June, the early summer warmth did nothing to ease the chill creeping over her skin beneath her négligée and robe.  

 

Instinctively, her gaze drifted toward the unlit fireplace.  

 

Her thoughts wandered back to the ceremony. She recalled nearly tearing up as she let go of her father’s hand and the tension she had felt reciting her wedding vows. The way Jared had slid the wedding ring onto her finger, how his fingers had pressed against hers, fitting the ring snugly into place.  

 

Lowering her eyes, Roselyn stared at the platinum band on her left ring finger. She remembered placing an identical one on his hand. In that moment, she had felt something unexpected, a peculiar sense of awe. As if it were a mark claiming him as hers.  

 

When he had lifted her veil, she had been momentarily blinded. The world had brightened in an instant. She could still recall the first moment their eyes met, how the light behind him had been so intense that it nearly overwhelmed her.  

 

“You may now kiss the bride.”  

 

Even with her eyes closed, the afterimage of that brightness remained.  

 

When his lips touched hers, she had forgotten to breathe. For a brief moment, everything had stilled. The warmth of his lips, the sensation against hers.  

 

Her first kiss.  

 

Just as that memory swelled in her mind, she heard the click of a door opening.  

 

She flinched, her shoulders tensing, and turned her head. The first thing she noticed was his wet hair. The damp strands were darker than usual, and beneath them, his face appeared even paler.  

 

He stood in the doorway, looking at her. His robe reached down to his knees, leaving his legs bare. The moment she noticed, she quickly averted her gaze, her heart pounding wildly.  

 

“You’re already here.”  

 

“I just arrived.”  

 

Her response was automatic, accompanied by a small, reflexive smile. He observed her for a moment before silently stepping further into the room.  

 

Roselyn hesitated, then slowly rose from her seat.  

 

Jared moved confidently, as if this space belonged to him, which, of course, it had for years. He strode toward the left side of the room and opened a cabinet built into the wall. Watching him, Roselyn realized it was a liquor cabinet.  

 

That meant the bed on the left was his.  

 

He selected a crystal glass, turned it upright, and uncorked a bottle of whiskey. Without turning, he asked,  

 

“Would you like a drink?”  

 

Roselyn hesitated. A drink might help ease her nerves, but the words wouldn’t come easily. She had noticed at a glance that the cabinet contained only whiskey, a strong liquor, which she didn’t enjoy.  

 

“I’m fine,” she declined politely.  

 

Jared turned slightly, their eyes meeting for a brief moment. He seemed to nod faintly before shifting his attention back to his glass. She thought she saw a fleeting smile on his lips, but she wasn’t sure, it vanished as he raised the glass to his mouth.  

 

He drank nearly half the whiskey in a single gulp. The sharp clink of the glass being set down broke the silence.  

 

A soft sigh escaped him.  

 

And then, suffocating silence.  

 

Roselyn watched his profile. His left hand was bare.  

 

He had removed his wedding ring after the ceremony.  

 

She knew men didn’t always wear their wedding bands daily, but for some reason, it still stung a little. Her gaze dropped to the signet ring on his pinky finger, and unconsciously, she found herself touching her own ring.  

 

Then, Jared turned and approached the right-side bed.  

 

Her heart pounded.  

 

It’s about to happen.  

 

That ambiguous yet undeniable something between them was drawing near.  

 

He would know what to do. It was absurd to expect a thirty-year-old man to be inexperienced. The fact that at least one of them knew what was supposed to happen should have been reassuring.  

 

Yet, Roselyn felt a pang of disappointment.  

 

He was not her first, but she was not his either.  

 

“Come here.”  

 

She lifted her eyes at his command.  

 

Across the room, they faced each other.  

 

His gaze was calm, without tension or anticipation, only obligation and weariness.  

 

Swallowing hard, Roselyn took slow steps toward him.  

 

Tightly wound nerves, a touch of fear, a hint of curiosity, and a sliver of expectation pressed down on her.  

 

He knows what to do. Just follow his lead. Don’t think too much, just go along with it.  

 

She repeated the thought over and over with every step.  

 

When she reached him, she caught the faint scent of whiskey on his breath. Unlike during the day, his usual cologne was barely noticeable. He must not wear it to bed.  

 

That thought had barely registered before his hand reached for the knot of her robe.  

 

Startled, she lifted her gaze.  

 

The gas lamp on the nightstand illuminated everything. His expression betrayed nothing; no hesitation, no reassurance.  

 

Nothing.  

 

His pupils were slightly dilated, but that was all.  

 

Roselyn was the one who faltered.  

 

He did not smile. He did not whisper soothing words.  

 

He simply gave her a moment to brace herself.  

 

Then, he resumed.  

 

Untying the knot, he slipped the robe from her shoulders. The silk garment slid soundlessly to the floor.  

 

His hands moved to the straps of her négligée.  

 

The moment his fingertips brushed her bare shoulder, she shuddered involuntarily.  

 

Now, only a single layer of lace and silk remained between her and him.  

 

Jared slowly lowered one strap.  

 

Roselyn had no idea what expression he wore at that moment, she couldn’t bring herself to look.  

 

Standing still, allowing him to undress her, was already unbearable.  

 

Why standing?  

 

Why must she be undressed at all?  

 

She had no answer, but she had to believe there was one.  

 

She had to trust, had to believe that her husband had a reason for all of this.  

 

Because if she didn’t, she wouldn’t be able to endure it.

As he pushed off the strap on the other shoulder, the negligée that had been precariously hanging slipped off. Almost instinctively, she crossed her arms over her chest. It wasn’t intentional, it was reflexive.  


Roselyn shuddered in sheer disbelief. She felt like she was about to cry, now more out of fear than anything else.  


In the silent bedroom, the only sound that echoed was her own trembling breath.  


She couldn’t stop shaking. Standing there, half-nked, in this unfamiliar room, in front of a man who was still so unfamiliar to her, it felt unreal. She squeezed her eyes shut, gripping onto the only hope she had: that he would say something. Anything.  


“Lie down.”  


His voice, when it finally came, felt almost like salvation. He pulled back the covers, making space for her on the bed with an unexpected gentleness. As she cautiously and quickly climbed onto the bed, still keeping her arms tightly around her chest, he turned away, granting her a moment of privacy.  


The warmth of the blanket reaching her chin brought her some comfort. She even found the courage to peek at his back as he adjusted the brightness of the gaslight before turning back to her.  


In the dimmed light, she looked up at him, trying desperately not to think about the negligée barely clinging to her waist or her exposed skin.  


Jared watched her silently before sitting down at the edge of the bed. Then, he spoke.  


“You can close your eyes.”  


There was a hint of self-mockery in his low voice.  


“It might be better if you do.”  


Following his advice, Roselyn shut her eyes. She clutched her hands together at her chest, bracing herself for what was to come.  


When he took her wrist and gently pulled it away, she tensed. The moment his hand cupped her exposed right breast, she squeezed her eyes even tighter.  


A chill ran down her spine at the foreign touch.  


His touch was gentle, careful. She could tell he was being cautious. Slowly, he removed her other arm from her body as well. Now, Roselyn lay completely exposed, her fingers curled into fists.  


No one had ever touched her like this before.  


She had never touched anyone like this before.  


She had never even imagined it.  


She didn’t fully understand the purpose of this act, but the sensations, her body’s involuntary reactions, were enough for her to guess.  


No one had ever told her in detail about the intimate affairs between a man and a woman, but she had read about them in novels. Sweet, hot, tantalizing. She had never understood those vague adjectives before, but now she did. She also understood that this was exactly what Jared intended.  


Roselyn turned her face away, pressing her lips together. A rush of emotions and sensations flooded her all at once; relief and fear, shame and pleasure, chills and heat.  


And when his hand slipped between her thighs, it all reached its peak.  


“Ah–”  


She gasped, startled, instinctively trying to close her legs, but it was useless.  


She opened her eyes in alarm and saw him. The man who was touching her.  


Jared remained composed. Unlike her, he was not breathing heavily, not clenching his eyes shut, not making strange noises.  


He handled her body with complete control. Though it was hers, he seemed to know it better than she did. Every time his large fingers moved, she flinched, her throat tightening.  


Sweet, hot, tantalizing.  


There were no better words for it.  


By the time she was gasping for breath, Jared began to undress.  


She already knew there was nothing underneath his robe.  


And so, she did not dare open her eyes.  


She simply breathed in raggedly as he spread her legs apart, unable to even think of resisting.  


When he climbed over her, his heated, solid body pressing against her bare skin, her heart pounded so violently she felt dizzy.  


The scent of soap and his body filled her nose.  


Still avoiding her gaze, Jared finally spoke.  


“It will hurt at first, but it’ll be fine soon.”  


His deep voice.  


Roselyn listened to it as she stared at his dark hair.  


“Endure it.”  


She squeezed her eyes shut again, bracing herself.  


She still didn’t fully understand the details of ‘it.’  


But he had warned her it would be painful, and so she focused only on that.  


Lying beneath him, completely exposed in her most vulnerable place, she waited.  


Her whole body trembled with tension.  


“Ah–”  


Sharp pain.  


Unbearable pain.  


She flinched instinctively, trying to recoil.  


“Not yet.”  


Jared held her down by the shoulders.  


Roselyn opened her eyes, unable to even breathe.  


