“My situation?”

“You need an heir.”

Roselyn spoke, her expression composed, her gray eyes blinking slowly. Her face was so solemn that she did not avert her gaze. In that moment, an intense emotion surged through Jared.

“Yes. That’s right.”

Shame. Or perhaps, anger.

I desperately need an heir.

The searing, immediate rush of emotion… he couldn’t quite discern what it was.

That’s why I married you.

A crooked smile tugged at his lips. He felt satisfied with the words that had slipped out before he could stop them.

Did he want to wound her? Probably.

Roselyn was silent for a moment. It seemed his words had stung. Jared watched her face, expecting to see her shaken. He anticipated the sight of her hurt and faltering.

But his wife did not waver.

“And because I am now your wife, that responsibility is also mine. I understand that having a child as soon as possible will put everyone at ease. Since they are all eagerly awaiting good news, I hope Your Grace will also make an effort.”

She spoke with astonishing composure. The same words he had heard time and time again from his mother, uncles, great-uncle, and grandaunt; words that had hounded him for years.

So please, fulfill your duty, Duke of Windburg.

That damn duty.

Jared gazed at her, suppressing his silent fury. The elegant blue dress she wore, the pristine white hands resting gracefully on her lap. The diamond on her left ring finger glinted; a stone that had shone unchanged for three hundred years. He found its brilliance revolting.

And then, he reminded himself who this woman truly was.

The woman who had placed that ring on her own hand. The woman who had accepted that kind of proposal, eager to become a duchess. What had he been thinking, feeling any sympathy or consideration for her?

“I knew everything, and I still married him. Becoming a duchess was my lifelong goal.”

She did not need his consideration. What she needed was a child. She had secured the Glen name; now she needed to bear a son. Only then would she become the Lady Dowager after his death.

Sympathy, for a woman like this? How absurd.

Mocking himself, Jared shifted his weight onto his other foot. His wife still sat there, poised like a doll, waiting for his response. The blue satin dress, such a noble color, suited her perfectly.

The Duchess of Windburg.

“You’re right, Roselyn. Thank you for your excellent advice.”

Jared smiled. The anger faded, and his mind turned cold. Without hesitation, he turned, opened the bedroom door, stepped halfway through, and then looked back.

“Come in.”

Roselyn didn’t seem to understand at first. She stared blankly at him, then glanced at the open bedroom, before finally returning her gaze to his face.

“…Right now?”

“You told me to make an effort.”

At last, a flicker of uncertainty crossed her face. Jared watched her, sitting like a portrait on the drawing-room couch. Her perfectly straight shoulders, the delicate curve of her nape, the sensual shadow of her collarbones. Keeping his gaze on those elegant lines, he spoke.

“Let’s do it now. The effort.”

There was no reason he couldn’t. He could make an effort—however much the duchess desired.

Thud.

The sound of the bedroom door closing made her body tense. Roselyn took a slow, deep breath, trying not to let her nerves show. It was just past 8 a.m., early even for the day’s duties to begin.

The bedroom curtains were drawn wide open. Sunlight streamed through the large glass windows, flooding the room. She had asked her attendants to prepare earlier than usual today. Noblewomen typically did not leave their chambers before ten.

Standing in the bright center of the bedroom, Roselyn hesitated. Was there anyone still in the dressing room? The maid who had helped her dress had left, but the duke’s valet might still be finishing up. She was concerned, but she had no courage to ask. Instead, she simply watched the man’s back.

Jared was silently removing his morning coat. He didn’t even glance in her direction, but she could feel the chill in the air. Was he angry? Why? She tried to guess what had upset him, but she had no time.

He finished hanging his coat over a chair and turned toward her. His white shirt, gray waistcoat, and silk tie remained perfectly in place. The very image of a gentleman. His cold eyes locked onto her, pressing down like a weight. Fear prickled at Roselyn’s spine, and she felt compelled to speak.

“…Should I lie down?”

“Undress.”

He tilted his head slightly.

“You should be naked before lying down.”

His face was devoid of any amusement. Roselyn stared at him under the stark morning light. Never had she imagined she would be asked to undress herself like this at 8 a.m., in such a bright room. It was entirely unexpected. She had never even considered this possibility.

How had things come to this? Should she have chosen a better moment to bring it up? But every night, he came back too late, and in the mornings, he always left before she could speak to him. During the day, they were never alone.

Now, finally, she was with her husband. She had no reason to refuse. This was their shared duty, an obligation they were bound to fulfill together.

This is my responsibility too. I know that having a child soon will reassure everyone.

This is my role. This is what I must do.

With that resolve, Roselyn reached for the front of her dress. She loosened the satin ribbon and began unbuttoning it, one by one. When she reached her waist, she remembered the layers beneath; corset, petticoat, chemise, drawers, stockings.

“You don’t have to take everything off.”

As if reading her thoughts, he stepped closer. Roselyn resisted the instinct to step back. There was nowhere to run, trapped between the two beds.

“We don’t have time for that.”

Jared pulled at the corset ribbons without hesitation, loosening the laces just enough.

“Ah,”  

Everything was unfamiliar. Everything began abruptly.  

His large hands roamed inside the corset, kneading and pressing roughly. His touch was urgent, unrelenting. So different from their wedding night, when he had treated her with care. The sharp pain flared, forcing Roselyn to bite her lip to keep from reacting.

Pain and pleasure were unexpectedly close. At the end of the stinging pain, there was a strange shiver. The pain was bearable, but the pleasure was not, it was harder to suppress.

Even as she held her mouth shut, sounds escaped. Her body twisted involuntarily. And at some point, she realized; his arms were wrapped around her waist, and her hands were gripping his shoulders.

The two of them stood close, facing each other.  

As if they were dancing.

Her breath came in ragged gasps. Her head felt light, and her mouth was dry. Roselyn had to wet her parched lips several times with her tongue, her eyes shut tightly.  

And then, just as Jared bent over, lifting the many layers of her skirts all at once and slipping his hand inside her drawers, she thought it was finally time to lie down on the bed.  

But he didn’t tell her to lie down.  

“Turn over.”  

His voice was thick with heated breath.  

Roselyn opened her eyes at the incomprehensible command. She had no time to consider how disheveled she must look, with her dress undone to the waist. She panted through parted lips, looking at him with questioning eyes.  

But Jared offered no explanation. He simply grabbed her shoulders, turned her around, and pressed her upper body onto the bed. The forcefulness of his touch stunned her. The unexpected situation made her heart leap with fear.  

What is he trying to do?  

She had no idea. She was completely unprepared.  

And then, she realized.  

“Ah!”  

He entered her all at once. Without hesitation.  

Pain did not register. What consumed Roselyn was shock. Then shame. And finally, fear.  

She couldn’t see him.  

An invisible force was shaking her, dominating her. That realization brought forth a deep, primal terror. He could see her, control her, possess her, yet she couldn’t even lay eyes on him.  

The sheer distance between them overwhelmed her. Jared felt too far away; she couldn’t even hear his breathing. His body heat was absent. She couldn’t even reach out to hold onto him.  

So she endured it alone.  

Biting her lip.  

Between pain and pleasure.  

Struggling to swallow both down as much as possible.  

After what felt like an eternity, he finally stopped. Roselyn collapsed forward onto the bed.  

No words were exchanged.  

Only her ragged breaths filled the room. She didn’t even want to imagine how she must look right now. She wanted to fix the dress that had bunched up around her waist, but it was impossible. The man behind her still held her in place.  

The strength in his arms around her waist was almost brutal.  

Roselyn didn’t even try to escape. Her whole body trembled, her limbs too weak to support her without gripping the mattress.  

And then, Jared pulled her upright.  

Only then did she hear his breath. She felt the warmth of his chest pressing against her back, his arms wrapping around her shoulders.  

A strange sense of relief flooded through her.  

His hot breath brushed against her right ear, trailing down her neck and dampening her shoulder.  

Jared held her like that for a moment, as if he were about to say something. As if he were about to whisper something into her ear.  

But in the end, he said nothing.  

He let her go.  

The moment their bodies separated, Roselyn had to brace her legs.  

She had nothing to lean on now, she had to stand on her own.  

She couldn’t afford to collapse. She couldn’t afford to stumble.  

“I should leave first.”  

Jared had regained his composure with surprising speed.  

What kind of expression was he making now?  

Was he still looking at her with those cold eyes?  

She wanted to know, but she couldn’t bring herself to look at him.  

“I’ll see you tonight.”  

Right now, it was better not to see his face at all.  

After he left, Roselyn remained standing there.  

She didn’t bother fixing her dress, which had come undone to her waist. It was already a mess, she would have to change anyway. More than anything, she needed time to steady herself.  

That’s why I married him. 

She stared at nothing, slowly closing and reopening her eyes.  

She felt an unfamiliar sensation trailing down her legs.  

At that moment, Roselyn instinctively understood something.  

And she was relieved.  

And then, almost immediately, she felt miserable for feeling that way.  



“How are you any different from Jared?”

Karina Glen began her complaint over breakfast.  

Even though her son and daughter-in-law were present, she paid no mind. She held them both in high regard, after all. She expected them to take her side.  

“That again? I’m getting tired of this.”  

“You may be tired of it, but listen anyway! You’re the Duke’s second son, and he’s the third! Doesn’t that make you closer to the title than he is?”  

“I am the son of the 10th Duke. Jared is the son of the 11th. So of course, he is closer.”  

“Oh, for heaven’s sake! You’re so frustrating!”  

Karina thumped her chest in exasperation.  

Kylius Glen chuckled at his wife’s dramatics.  

How had his mother ever chosen such a woman to be his bride? Even in the afterlife, she must find it embarrassing.  

“Please, my dear, Karina. Do not say such things in public.”  

“Why? Are you afraid Jared will send an assassin after us?”  

“No, but people’s ears are frightening. There is nothing I fear more than bringing dishonor to our family name.”  

Kylius Glen responded calmly, wiping his mouth with a napkin as he finished his meal.  

The Glen family was known for many things, one of which was the strong bond between its siblings.  

Despite reigning as rulers for a thousand years, there had never been a case of brothers fighting for the title of Duke. In contrast to countless historical instances where younger brothers killed older ones or uncles usurped their nephews, the Glen family upheld a rare and noble tradition.  

Kylius was the younger brother of the late Duke Philip. They had once had a sister as well, but she had died young, leaving only the two of them to grow up together.  

Perhaps that was why he had grieved so much when two of his brother’s sons died, leaving only the youngest.  

But his support for Jared had nothing to do with personal affection. It was about protecting the unbroken line of succession that had been maintained since Sheldon Glen, the first ruler of the North.  

The truth was, Philip’s sons had all been problematic.  

The eldest, Harold, had foolishly attempted to divide the family’s titles and lands among his brothers. He had died for it.  

Kylius firmly believed that Harold had been cursed for trying to fracture the family’s inheritance.  

And he had made sure to impart this belief to Jared.  

He had hoped his nephew, as the new duke, would dedicate his life fully to the family.  

But then, that boy had nearly upended everything by insisting on a ridiculous marriage. And for four years after that, he had remained stubbornly unmarried, as if to protest.  

To wed at thirty was already disgracefully late. But at least now, he had come to his senses.  

“I don’t understand why you dislike Jared so much,” Kylius said. “He’s doing well as Duke. The issue that troubled you so much has been resolved. You should feel relieved now.”  

“Then why did you pressure him to get married in the first place?” Karina scoffed. “You should have just let him stay single forever! Let him waste away over that pathetic novelist or whatever she was!”  

“Enough, Lady Glen. Maintain your dignity.”  

“Hmph! I’m Lady Glen indeed! But I’m not the Duchess or the Dowager, am I?”  

Kylius cast a disinterested glance at his wife before rising from his seat. It was a familiar sight in this household; the husband leaving the table first. Neither their son, his wife, nor the servants paid any mind.  

“I must have been insane. What was I thinking, introducing him to a woman? I thought he wouldn’t care for a viscount’s daughter. Turns out, he has no eye for women either.”  

Karina grumbled bitterly, stabbing at the remaining sausages on her plate.  

“There’s no cure for lovesickness. If he had wasted away and died, at least I might have become the duchess. I could have seen my son take the title, even if only from the afterlife.”  

“Don’t be too disappointed, Mother.”  

Her son’s soothing voice made Karina look up.  

Unlike his father, he never disregarded her. He was intelligent and considerate.  

“Just because the new duchess is married doesn’t mean she will bear an heir.”  

Alexander Glen smiled like a true gentleman.  

Even now, with two sons of his own, he remained as handsome as ever; once the darling of high society, with his father’s dark hair and his mother’s blue eyes.  

“You’re right, of course.”  

Karina nodded in satisfaction.  

Not every woman was fortunate enough to have such an outstanding son.  

“Marriage doesn’t guarantee a son.”

“But Mother, a novelist? Are you saying the woman Duke Jared was supposed to marry was a novelist?”

Naomi Glen’s eyes sparkled with curiosity. Karina was pleased to see that her eldest daughter-in-law was interested in her story. Since she had taken the time to visit despite being busy looking after her children, it seemed fitting to repay her with an entertaining tale.

The husband, in the end, did not appear last night either.  

Roselyn had waited for him since around ten o’clock. Out of habit, she lay in bed and tried to read a few pages of a book, but she couldn’t focus at all. With her eyes on a thick, tedious philosophy book she had brought from the study, she strained her ears, focusing all her attention on the possibility of hearing his approaching footsteps.  

As the night deepened and she finally became certain that he would not come, a long sigh escaped her lips before she even realized it. She couldn’t tell why. Whether it was relief or disappointment. Whether she was glad or sorrowful.  

And then, when she woke up this morning, for the first time, she saw her husband asleep.  

Jared was in his bed. Though the blanket covered him up to his chest, his bare shoulders revealed that he was unclothed. He lay on his side, facing away from his wife’s bed, and Roselyn could see his broad shoulders, his back, and his disheveled hair.  

The early morning bedroom was dim, with all the windows covered. Even when she quietly got out of bed, Jared remained deeply asleep. On the bedside table beside him was an empty glass. A round, short crystal glass. Roselyn stared at it briefly before quietly heading to the dressing room.  

Even after finishing her morning routine alone, without an attendant, and stepping into the drawing room, the house remained silent. Her husband was still asleep in his bed, so she walked more carefully than usual. She slipped out of the room soundlessly, like a cat.  

She considered waiting for him to wake up, but recalling the events of the previous day, she couldn’t bring herself to do so.  

“Let’s do it now.”  

Roselyn didn’t want to go through it again. It might be something that needed to be done, but it wasn’t what she wanted. She didn’t want the fear, the self-loathing, the humiliation that came with that man.  

Then what is it that I want?  

As she momentarily stopped writing and fell into thought, there was a knock at the door, followed by it opening.  

“You called for me, Madam?”  

Roselyn, seated at her desk, lifted her head.  

She was in the east drawing room on the second floor, which served as the duchess’s office. The room had furniture for about ten people, a fireplace, and a few paintings hanging on the silk-covered walls. It was similar in size to the family room at Fairfield House but far more lavishly decorated.  

“Edith, come in.”  

Roselyn, seated at a small, elegant desk by the window, welcomed her attendant.  

“Could you send these out? I just finished them.”  

She gestured toward the newly sealed letters. A neat stack of thirty envelopes lay in a box on the desk. They were embossed with the silver insignia of the eagle of Glengar.  

“You wrote thirty yesterday as well. You write letters quite quickly.”  

“I have to get through them fast. It’s something that needs to be done anyway.”  

Roselyn smiled, flexing her sore right hand.  

Sending thank-you letters to those who had accepted their invitations was only proper etiquette. She had never hosted an event for as many as three hundred people before, but she still believed she should personally extend her gratitude. Since these were the first letters she was writing under the duchess’s name, she didn’t want to delegate the task.  

Moreover, it was their wedding, after all. It was only natural to thank those who had come to celebrate their marriage.  

“Just two more days, and I’ll be done. I have sixty-five letters left.”  

“You’re impressive, Madam. But next time, it may be best to have them written for you and simply sign them. You can’t possibly keep doing this for every grand event.”  

“I’ll consider it if the scribe has better handwriting than I do.”  

Roselyn chuckled as she dipped her fountain pen into the inkwell.  

Her gold-trimmed fountain pen, engraved with her initials, was a gift for her fifteenth birthday. That was also the year she had begun writing thank-you letters to guests of Fairfield House for the first time.  

“From now on, I’ll send the letters, Father. In Mother’s place.”  

Writing letters was a lady’s duty. Roselyn had spent countless hours perfecting her penmanship, dedicating herself to fulfilling the role expected of her.  

“I will send these out today.”  

“Thank you.”  

“It’s my pleasure. And, Madam…”  

Roselyn, who had been dipping her pen, looked up. She noticed for the first time that Edith was holding a large envelope.  

“This was sent from the attendants’ office. It contains photographs for the newspaper, and they’d like your opinion.”  

Edith placed the envelope on the desk. Roselyn lowered her pen and looked at it.  

“If you select three or four, they will send them as is.”  

Even before Edith finished speaking, Roselyn opened the envelope and took out its contents.  

Despite the large envelope, there were only seven photographs inside. The photographer had followed them all day, flashing his camera countless times, so there must have been dozens of pictures. These must have been the ones the attendants had selected. Roselyn examined them one by one, carefully.  

There was no color in the printed images. That lack of color made the scenes feel like something out of a dream. The temple on the Maxville estate, the outdoor banquet hall, the Great Hall. Familiar yet unreal.  

In that world, Jared was looking at her. With a solemn expression as he slipped the wedding ring onto her finger, raising a champagne glass while gazing at her, whispering something as they danced at the reception. Standing close, looking down at her with a smile.  

In that colorless world, he was smiling. His eyes were fixed on her with unwavering attention.  

A face filled with happiness. Utter happiness. Like a lover.  

“Smile, Roselyn.”  

The woman smiling back at him in the photograph looked just as happy. As if she had never known fear, self-loathing, or misery. Roselyn envied the woman in the picture. And in that moment, she realized… this. This is what I want.  

I want you to look at me like this. To be engrossed with me. To smile at me, happily, utterly happy.  

Like a lover.

“Why me?”  

Suddenly, Roselyn felt impatient.  

“I think we need to know each other.”  

She hadn’t meant to say it like that.  

She should have met him this morning. She had run away, afraid of what he might say, of what kind of situation she might have to endure. But she shouldn’t have done that. They needed to talk about what had happened yesterday.  

It had only been about a week since their marriage, and Roselyn knew that beginnings were important. If things weren’t set right from the start, they would only become more difficult later. The longer she waited, the harder it would get.  

“Edith, is the Duke at the estate?”  

She had to be brave now.  

“As far as I know, he is.”  

“Find out if he has a dinner appointment.”  

There were still wedding guests staying at Maxville. Roselyn had breakfast with some of them this morning, and a tea gathering was scheduled for later. But as far as she knew, none of the remaining guests were particularly important.  

“If he has no prior engagement, tell him I would like to have dinner together.”  

She hesitated, then added, “Just the two of us.”  

The words made her tense. He might refuse. She might get hurt again. But even if she did nothing, she would still end up hurt.  

So, now. Now was the time.  

“Understood, madam.”  

Edith nodded, then left with the thirty letters.  

Once the attendant was gone, Roselyn was alone again. She remained at her elegant desk, holding the seven photographs in her hands. There were still sixty-five more letters to write, a tea gathering to attend, and five guests to entertain, but for now, she set aside the pressing concerns of reality.  

A world that resembled reality, yet was not real.  

She stared at that world for a long time, looking at the man and woman in the photographs. She wanted this monochrome world to become real. And to make that happen, she needed to be braver now.  

  

Jared had not refused the Duchess’s invitation for any particular reason. The real reason was simple; her attendant already knew he had no prior dinner plans.  

Edith Cheshire was competent. That was why he had allowed her to return, despite her past service to Veronia. Keeping capable people around made many things easier.  

So, in the end, Jared was sitting here because of a courtesy he had extended to his wife.  

“I was thinking we should cover the unused rooms with dust sheets.”  

As Roselyn spoke, Jared sliced into the grilled trout with his knife. The well-cooked pink flesh fell apart delicately.

The two sat across from each other in the middle of an eight-person dining table. Placing her husband in the position closest for conversation, the Duchess spoke only about household matters. It seemed she needed a warm-up before addressing the real issue, so Jared humored her with appropriate responses.  

“There are 412 rooms in the main building, but fewer than a hundred are actually in use. And yet, we still manage the unused rooms daily. Even considering the guests that come and go, it’s an enormous waste.”  

Roselyn had already grasped the general workings of the ducal estate. It had been less than a week since she had become Duchess, yet she carried herself with the demeanor of a seasoned mistress of the house. According to his mother, she had shown interest in the estate’s affairs even during her time as a fiancée.  

His mother seemed pleased with the new Duchess’s proactive nature. She showed no signs of resentment in handing over control of the household to her daughter-in-law. Then again, there was no reason for his mother to feel slighted, she had long since lost any real authority over the estate.  

“It’s an interesting suggestion, but if we cover the unused rooms with dust sheets, the maids will have less work to do,” Jared remarked.  

“That’s why I think we can reduce the number of staff slightly,” Roselyn replied as if she had been waiting for this moment.  

“I took a brief look around, and I noticed quite a few unnecessary positions. For instance, there are three people solely in charge of monitoring fire hazards. Maybe that was necessary before gas lamps, but as far as I know, this is the only household that still maintains such a role.”  

Jared lifted his eyes from his fork, still holding a piece of meat.  

What surprised him wasn’t just how naturally their conversation flowed, like that of a true married couple, but the boldness of her proposal. It wasn’t just that she suggested dismissing employees; it was the reasoning behind it. Efficiency. Wasted wages. Such a thought process was distinctly un-aristocratic.  

“You’re suggesting we lay off staff?”  

“I can’t justify paying wages to people who don’t do their fair share.”  

“Roselyn, do you know why this estate is called Maxville?”  

Jared found himself genuinely intrigued.  

“No, but I do know that the estate originated from a royal palace.”  

“That’s correct. This area was uninhabited until the queen’s retreat was built here. The palace needed farmers and artisans to supply its goods, so a village formed around it. That village became Maxville, and the estate took its name from it.”  

“That’s quite an interesting origin,” she admitted.  

“Most of the people who work in this house, as well as the merchants we trade with, have had ties to our family for generations. Many of them have inherited their positions. The fire watchmen you mentioned, for example, are doing the same work their fathers did.”  

“I didn’t know that.”  

“Well, now you do. And that’s why it is an unspoken rule in this estate that once someone is employed, their job is guaranteed unless they commit a serious offense.”  

Efficiency was not a noble concern, tradition and duty were. Jared intended to uphold the policies of his predecessors.  

“Fortunately, I can afford to maintain our current staff. But those fire watchmen you wish to dismiss? If they lose their jobs here, they lose their livelihoods. I don’t think that’s what you want.”  

“You’re right. I hadn’t thought of it that way.”  

Roselyn nodded, looking at him with her composed gray eyes, not the gaze of someone who had simply been persuaded.  

“Then why did you replace the butler and housekeeper?”  

Jared paused for a moment.  

“I heard that all the senior staff at Maxville were replaced a few years ago, right after you inherited the title.”  

She was correct. It had been three years since he had dismissed the head butler, three assistant butlers, and the head housekeeper.  

The reason was simple; they had betrayed their master.  

Back then, Jared had been away from the estate for a long time as the third son of the Duke. The staff at Maxville had remained loyal to the Duchess, his mother, and out of their allegiance to her, they had driven away his lover. Without his permission, they had even provided her with a carriage.  

At midnight.  

To the train station.  

So she could leave him forever.  

“They committed a serious offense and were punished accordingly.”  

“So you fired them all?”  

“The butler resigned on his own. The others were reassigned to my other estates. I own twelve others besides this one.”  

Roselyn, holding her cutlery, stared at him as if she wanted to ask what exactly that “serious offense” had been. Jared did not want that question spoken aloud. He turned away and picked up his wine glass.  

As he took a sip, a strange silence settled between them. He had no desire to prolong this topic any further.  

“Now, let’s get to the real conversation. I doubt you invited me to dinner just to discuss household expenses.”  

They had their own matters to deal with. The past was none of her concern.  

“So, where shall we begin? Yesterday morning? Or last night?”  

His gaze settled on her over his wine glass. Roselyn tensed slightly at the abrupt shift in topic. The curiosity about the past vanished from her face, and that satisfied Jared.  

The Duchess briefly lowered her gaze, as if steadying herself, before looking at him again.  

“I thought you would come back early last night.”  

“I intended to, but I changed my mind.”  

“May I ask why?”  

“I didn’t see a need to do it twice in one day.”  

He didn’t bother with subtlety; there was no need. After all, the only ones present in the dining room were the two of them and the head butler, Sterling. Household staff were professionals, and Sterling, after three years of service, had proven himself exceptionally discreet.  

But Roselyn looked visibly flustered. She glanced at the butler, who pretended not to have heard anything, then quickly composed herself, her face turning red.  

Jared knew she blushed easily. At social gatherings, she carried herself as gracefully as a woman twice her age, but in private, she often failed to conceal her inexperience.  

She had been the same yesterday morning.  

She had entered his room with a determined expression, only to freeze stiff once inside. She had the audacity to lecture him about his duty as a husband, and yet, when it came time to act, she had been terrified. Her contradictions had irritated him. Perhaps that was why he had been so cruel.  

“Undress and lie down.”  

He could admit it, he had been unnecessarily harsh with her.  

That unbearable heat rising in his gut, was it anger or desire? He hadn’t been able to tell. And in the end, it hadn’t mattered. What difference did it make? After all, everyone expected him to do this.  

So it didn’t matter whether it was fury or lust that drove him.  

“I don’t think I need to try twice in one day. What do you think?”  

Jared emphasized ‘try.’ It was a convenient word.  

“You were right, Roselyn. Thanks to your advice, we succeeded. And we will continue to try in the future. However, I think we should find a more efficient approach. Frequent attempts won’t necessarily increase the likelihood of pregnancy.”  

His tone was neither gentle nor harsh. He spoke as if discussing a matter of business; a professional debriefing on an operation.  

He wanted it to sound like that. A conversation of mutual respect, assessing the best course of action.  

“So I think once a week should be enough.”  

He did not want emotions involved. He didn’t want to hear that she had been hurt, afraid, or miserable. As she had pointed out, this was a duty. A role.  

For three years, he had learned to separate his official duties from himself.  

When he addressed the emperor on the anniversary of the founding, it wasn’t him speaking.  

When he stood before the nobility, it wasn’t him standing there.  

Likewise, this obligation to produce the next Duke was not his burden.  

“Yesterday was Tuesday. So every Tuesday it is.”  

He was demonstrating the correct attitude for this situation.  

“What do you think?”  

Surely, the intelligent Duchess would understand his meaning.

As expected, Roselyn was adjusting to this peculiar conversation. She had accepted the rigid proposal of setting a fixed day and frequency for their marital relations. Though she still couldn’t look directly into her husband’s eyes.  

“I think it’s a reasonable approach. And…”  

She nodded but trailed off momentarily. Jared waited patiently as she hesitated, her gaze lowered.  

“At night, preferably.”  

Her voice was quiet, as if conscious of the butler standing in the corner of the dining room.  

Jared couldn’t help but chuckle. The low sound made Roselyn look up at him. Gray eyes beneath long lashes. He ignored the emotions within them.  

“Alright. Tuesday nights it is.”  

“And… how about having dinner together on those nights? Just the two of us.”  

She quickly added the condition. Jared, a faint trace of amusement on his lips, looked at her.  

She never missed a chance to negotiate. If she insisted, he supposed he might as well play along.  

“Alright. As long as there aren’t any important guests.”  

“Even if there are, please make time.”  

That was an unexpected counter.  

“I believe I have the right to have dinner with my husband at least once a week.”  

She stated it without hesitation, as if she had anticipated his attempt to evade.  

“I’ll make an exception for His Majesty the Emperor. Other than that, please set Tuesdays aside for me.”  

His Majesty, huh. She came prepared.

The woman who had just made such a bold remark was now looking at him with a serious expression. Her flushed face reminded him of the first day she had arrived as his fiancée.  

“Please make time.”

And for what, exactly?  

“I think we need to know each other.”

Jared knew what she wanted. He also knew how arranged marriages like theirs usually played out. Wealthy, noble couples rarely became lovers. In this world, “spouse” and “lover” were not synonyms.  

He never thought his father or brother had treated their wives cruelly. After all, their wives had married them with clear objectives in mind. That was the fundamental limitation of arranged marriages. You could never be lovers when you came together for a purpose. A spouse chosen for practicality could never compare to a person chosen for passion.  

And Jared no longer wanted either. The first was empty. The second, painful.  

“You’re right, Roselyn. Of course, you have that right.”  

“Then you accept my proposal?”  

She pressed for confirmation. He still held his wine glass, watching her.  

Dinner with his wife every Tuesday. That meant he could count on at least one relatively calm evening per week. It was far easier to engage in conversation with her than to endure endless chatter about politics, business, and social affairs from other nobles.  

It was actually a good excuse, he could use these weekly dinners to decline tedious invitations. And appearing as a devoted husband would only add to his public image.  

Not a bad proposal. Not bad at all.  

“Alright. I’ll set Tuesday evenings aside from now on.”  

“Thank you.”  

Roselyn’s face lit up with a radiant smile. Unlike her usual composed and graceful expressions, her lips parted fully, her delight uncontained. She looked like a child receiving a gift, unable to stop smiling. Her open mouth and exposed teeth strangely caught his attention.  

Jared watched her as if seeing something unfamiliar. Aside from their ceremonial kiss at the wedding, he had never kissed her. Kissing was for lovers, it had nothing to do with their duty to produce an heir.  

“You’ve improved your negotiation skills. It was hard to refuse.”  

“I failed once before. The greatest lessons come from past mistakes.”  

Jared fell silent for a moment at her words. She was still smiling, as if truly happy to have secured his time. As if it actually mattered.  

You won’t find much use for it anyway.

Impressive, Duchess.

He concealed his thoughts behind a smile and lifted his wine glass. Across from him, she mimicked his action. The wine tasted especially sweet as it touched his tongue.  

A mutually satisfying agreement. A reconciliation, even six days into their marriage.  

All in all, it had been a successful dinner.  



The agreement was honored.  

Jared proved his reliability over the next two Tuesdays. He had dinner with his wife and, as promised, fulfilled his duty afterward.  

The two nights were similar to the previous ones, yet also different. Roselyn was still shy, but less afraid. Jared was as controlled as before, but she could sense something restrained in his movements and his breathing.  

After two Tuesdays, Roselyn found herself growing accustomed to the intimacy between a man and a woman.  

As her fear of the unknown faded, curiosity took its place. There were so many things she wanted to ask, but no one to ask them to. But really, who could she ask about such things? Even if her mother had been alive, she doubted she would have brought it up.  

In truth, Roselyn knew nothing.  

She had learned piano, social dancing, horseback riding, and foreign languages, but no one had ever taught her about men. Having younger brothers didn’t help, and her father had only taught her about a gentleman’s manners and character, never what a man became when alone with a woman.  

If anyone had given her even a hint, it had been the novelists.  

In romance novels, lovers kissed passionately. They “melted into each other with rapture” or “surrendered to ecstasy”, phrases that had once seemed vague. Now she understood what those writers had been describing.  

But novels exaggerated.  

Reality was neither rapturous nor ecstatic.  

Jared never kissed her. Not on the lips. Not anywhere.  

The only times he touched her were during social dances and when he came to her bed on Tuesday nights.  

Close people touch each other. They kiss cheeks and foreheads to express affection, just as she did with her younger brothers, just as her father had done to her.  

So Jared was not close to her.  

He was polite, respectful, but distant.  

Just like now, sitting side by side in the backseat of a car, keeping to their own spaces.  

“I’ll try to make it quick. Won’t keep you there too long.”  

Jared’s voice snapped Roselyn out of her thoughts. She had been staring absentmindedly at the empty space between them. If they had taken a carriage, at least they would be seated facing each other. But in a car, with its forward-facing seats, even turning to look at him was difficult.  

“Edith said we’ll be there for about two hours.”  

“You’ll want to leave in thirty minutes.”  

His voice was laced with boredom. He hadn’t even turned to look at her, his gaze fixed outside the window.  

Dressed in a tuxedo, he looked especially handsome tonight. It was their first time attending an event together since the wedding, and Roselyn had taken extra care with her appearance.  

“Just one more week to endure. July is almost over.”  

“I appreciate the encouragement, but don’t get your hopes up. In a place like Maxville, the social season never really ends.”  

In the north, the peak of the social season was June and July, but unlike in the past, Jared’s social circle was no longer limited to aristocrats. Politicians, businessmen, artists, and scholars had formed a new kind of high society. The more distinguished guests visited the estate, the stronger its reputation became.  

To remain the unofficial ‘king of the North,’ he had to entertain guests year-round.  

“Do you know Mayor Grant well?”  

“Well enough. I know he has a terrible drinking habit in private.”  

“You must see him often, then.”  

“He governs my territory, after all.”  

Jared gave a dry chuckle.  

Tonight’s event was a ball hosted at the opera house by Windburg’s mayor. An annual charity event, one that Jared attended every year. The citizens of Windburg liked knowing that their elected mayor and their Duke got along well.  

“It’s a rather grand event. The cause is respectable, and it’s the perfect place for us to make our first public appearance as a couple.”  

Roselyn nodded.  

She understood why Jared had chosen the mayor’s ball for their first outing together. It was to show respect for the world beyond the aristocracy. He cared about public opinion and valued their goodwill.  

Nearly a month into their marriage, Roselyn was slowly beginning to understand her husband.

Jared was highly engaged in business. He received regular reports from all the enterprises under his name and personally attended important meetings. Unlike most nobles who were content to remain in the background as mere shareholders or investors, he was directly involved.  

Roselyn wondered where his ambition for business stemmed from. Perhaps it was because of his experience as a lawyer. It was well known throughout the empire that Jared had worked in the legal department of a transportation company before inheriting his title. She assumed that his unconventional approach came from that unique past.  

Hereditary nobles did not work. They held titles but never professions. Jared was likely the only noble patriarch who had earned money through his own labor. Roselyn admired that progressive mindset, and yet, at the same time, it unsettled her.  

“There are plenty of people eager to see you,” Jared remarked. 

“Everyone’s been waiting for me to introduce my wife. Morrison said newspapers and magazines are practically begging for an interview.”  

He emphasized begging with a smirk, clearly amused.  

Their bold decision to provide wedding photos to the press had been effective. The images had appeared in last week’s daily papers, drawing a strong response, and another feature had been published just three days ago.  

Roselyn thought of the three pictures she had personally chosen. Even to her, they looked beautiful enough to keep, but she hadn’t. Because they were staged. Fake.  

“I don’t think an interview would be a good idea,” she said.  

“I agree. I don’t want reporters swarming Maxville either.”  

“That’s a relief.”  

“Occasionally attending events like this is enough. Show your face too often, and people get bored.”  

“I hope we don’t disappoint them tonight.”  

“That won’t happen.”  

Jared turned his head toward her for the first time, meeting her gaze. Until now, he had been watching the view outside. The twilight had thickened, casting his eyes in shadow, making them look almost black.  

His gaze settled on her, moving from her eyes to her cheeks, then her lips, down to her jaw, and finally her neck.  

His scrutiny made her feel self-conscious. Even the evening gown that exposed her shoulders felt suddenly improper.  

“Don’t worry about that, Roselyn.”  

Jared’s eyes lingered on her dark blue dress; the color of House Glen. His mother, Lady Diane, had once complimented her, saying that her gray eyes suited this shade perfectly.  

“Everyone will say you exceeded expectations.”  

His eyes returned to hers.  

Roselyn smiled. If he expected her to exceed expectations, she would do just that. Perfectly. Without giving him a reason to be disappointed.  

I can do this.  

Jared silently observed her for a moment before turning back to the window.  

Beyond the glass, the darkness was receding, and the gas lamps of the city were growing denser. They had finally entered town.  



The Opera House was located west of Windburg Station, across from Irving Square. It was one of the largest and most beautiful buildings in the city. Originally owned by House Glen, Jared’s grandfather had donated it to the public. The city government had named it Flint Hall in honor of the 10th Duke’s generosity.  

“Welcome, Your Grace. We’ve been expecting you.”  

By the time they arrived, the hall was already packed. The mayor, having been informed in advance of their arrival, greeted them personally.  

“Mayor Grant. You’re looking well tonight.”  

“As are you, Your Grace. Is wedded bliss the secret?”  

“I wouldn’t deny it,” Jared replied smoothly.  

The mayor, Reiner Grant, was approaching sixty and had been in office for thirteen years. There were no term limits for elected officials in the Trissen Empire, as long as the voters continued to support them.  

“This is my wife,” Jared said.  

“Duchess.”  

“Thank you for the invitation, Mayor,” Roselyn replied graciously.  

“It’s an honor to have you.”  

The mayor kissed the back of her hand, and flash, the cameras went off.  

Roselyn remained composed. She hadn’t expected this event to be without photographers.  

“This is my wife, Rita,” the mayor introduced.  

“Good evening, Lady Grant.”  

“It’s an honor, Your Grace. You’re even more beautiful than in the newspapers.”  

The mayor’s wife, a slender woman with a gentle demeanor, curtsied and smiled warmly.  

After brief pleasantries, Mayor Grant turned toward the orchestra.  

“Now that the Duke and Duchess have arrived, it’s time to open the dance. My wife has been practicing for weeks. Perhaps she’s discovered a hidden talent.”  

“Oh, you,” Lady Grant playfully dismissed his words. “Please don’t misunderstand, Your Grace. I’m simply trying not to make a fool of myself.”  

“I’ll do my best to make sure you don’t,” Jared said with a smile, extending his hand toward her.  

Roselyn noticed the mix of surprise and delight on the couple’s faces.  

“My, my, Your Grace!”  

“I can finally ask a lady to dance without hesitation now that I have a partner of my own.”  

It was a gesture of respect, and, more importantly, it signified that Jared considered the mayor an important figure. It also confirmed something Roselyn had suspected; Jared had never brought a companion to such an event before.  

“If I may boast,” Jared said, glancing at Roselyn, “my wife is an excellent dancer. Perhaps too skilled a partner for you, Mayor Grant.”  

“I have no doubt, Your Grace. What an honor.”  

“Mind your steps, Roselyn.”  

Jared turned to her with a teasing smile. As the mayor and his wife laughed, Roselyn met his gaze.  

He was smiling warmly; a husband’s smile. For a brief moment, she was almost dazzled.  

The waltz began, and the two couples stepped onto the dance floor.  



Mayor Grant was a competent dancer. He did not step on her feet once and even had the composure to offer his congratulations on her marriage. He had not been invited to the wedding, the guest list had consisted solely of nobles.  

After the first dance, the real work began.  

A constant stream of people approached Jared, eager to greet him. He met each one with a pleasant attitude, exchanging introductions and pleasantries with ease.  

Most of them were commoners; self-made men who had risen in their respective fields.  

Throughout this endless “duty,” Roselyn remained by his side. She was introduced to strangers, listened to conversations she barely understood, and smiled as if she did. Over and over.  

The more she observed, the more impressed she was.  

Jared maintained his composure flawlessly. He never showed boredom or fatigue. On the contrary, he seemed genuinely interested, asking sharp yet thoughtful questions that pleased his audience.  

How does he sustain such focus?  

How is he so polished, so composed?  

The longer she stood beside him, the prouder she felt to be his wife. Even if she did nothing, even if she was merely an ornament standing by his side, she was content.  

No. More than that, she wanted to be part of those conversations.  

She wanted to be a woman he was proud of.  

A woman who could earn his admiration.  

Someone he genuinely enjoyed being with.  

That’s the kind of person I want to be.  

All night, she focused on listening, memorizing details, and perfecting her smile.  

By the time her face ached from forcing smiles, a familiar voice interrupted.  

“Cousin!”  

Jared’s older cousin, Alexander Glenn.  

“Brother Alexander.”  

“I’ve been trying to get to you all night, but people are swarming you like hawks after a rabbit. And we’re supposed to be the hawks.”  

Alexander grinned and embraced Jared casually.  

With their tall statures and dark hair, they could have been mistaken for brothers.  

“Duchess, you are stunning,” Alexander said, turning to Roselyn.  

“Lord Glenn.”  

He extended his hand in an exaggerated gesture, leaving her no choice but to respond. As he kissed her gloved hand, she smiled lightly.  

Thankfully, she was wearing silk gloves.  

“It’s a pleasure to see you here, Jared,” another voice chimed in.  

“Likewise, Lady Glen,” Jared responded politely.  

Naomi Glen. With her deep golden hair and striking blue eyes, she bore the same distinguished Glenn features as her husband.  

Roselyn had met them for the first time at the wedding.  

Alexander was second in line to inherit House Glen.  

She remembered being wary of him that day, even though she had no reason to be.  

Something about the way his bright blue eyes lingered on her had made her uneasy.

“My dear cousin finally brings his wife to an event. I’m truly moved.”

“How kind of you to be so concerned.”

“Naturally. You are, after all, the head of our family.”

Alexander spoke cheerfully, and Roselyn maintained a polite smile, shifting her gaze to Naomi. His wife met her eyes as if she had been waiting, smiling in return, like sisters-in-law getting along perfectly.  

Jared, too, smiled as he regarded his cousin; his potential successor. Alexander was robust, in perfect health, and already had two sons.  

“I think about you too, of course, brother. If anything happens to me, you’ll be the one to lead our family.”

For a brief moment, Roselyn nearly betrayed her discomfort.  

“I feel reassured knowing you’re here.”

“It’s my pleasure. Truly.”

Their conversation was spoken in casual tones, yet it unsettled her. She looked at the two cousins standing close together. They were completely different in appearance. Not a single feature resembled the other.  

The four of them exchanged pleasantries. Jared and Alexander discussed recent events, while Naomi spoke about their children. Roselyn remained on edge, but no tension arose. Jared maintained impeccable courtesy toward his cousin’s family.  

“Will you let me have the next dance with the Duchess?”  

Alexander’s request was made in a relaxed and amicable atmosphere.  

“You danced the first with the mayor. Surely I deserve a turn. I’ve been waiting since the wedding for the chance to hold the beautiful Duchess’s hand.”  

He smiled at her.  

Roselyn instinctively glanced at Naomi, but the woman appeared entirely unconcerned. She merely shook her head, smiling in amusement. Am I overreacting? Roselyn forced herself to respond with a small, polite smile.  

Jared, too, seemed unbothered by his cousin’s boldness.  

“Of course, brother. The day has finally come for us to exchange partners.”

“Indeed. It should’ve happened sooner.”

“Better late than never.”  

At that moment, the orchestra began a new piece. Alexander raised his eyebrows in delight.  

“Moderato! Excellent.”  

Satisfied, he turned toward Roselyn.  

She looked up at her husband. Jared was already gazing down at her. With a subtle nod of permission, he released her arm. Alexander extended his hand toward her.  

And then, Jared kissed her cheek.  

It happened without warning.  

His arm wrapped lightly around her waist, and before she even realized it, something soft and warm pressed against her left cheek.  

By the time Roselyn registered what had happened, he had already stepped back.  

Nobles rarely displayed affection in public. Even the closest of married couples considered it improper to show intimacy before others.  

She was stunned. Her face flushed, only adding to her bewilderment.  

“Take good care of my wife.”  

Jared’s voice came from above her. Roselyn saw a flicker of surprise pass over Alexander and Naomi’s faces.  

Did others see it too?  

Did everyone see him kiss me?  

“You needn’t worry, cousin.”  

Alexander grinned as he took Roselyn’s hand. His grip was firm, like Jared’s.  

Jared led Naomi onto the dance floor first, his black tuxedo and broad shoulders standing tall as they moved ahead. Roselyn, now with Alexander, followed behind.  

As the Duke and Duchess returned to the dance floor for the second time that evening, the crowd’s attention turned toward them.  

Most of the guests were commoners and unfamiliar with formal dancing, making the floor relatively sparse. This only made the attention on Roselyn all the more pronounced.  

“This is the charm of these events,” Alexander mused as he placed a hand on her waist.  

“You feel like the star of the show.”  

Roselyn straightened her back, pressing against the corset as she rested a hand on his shoulder. His cologne was strong. Too strong.  

“Not that you’d understand, Duchess. You’ll be the star wherever you go from now on.”  

He smiled down at her.  

Not knowing how to respond, Roselyn returned a small, polite smile.  

There was something about him she didn’t like.  

Even his easy conversation and casual laughter felt discomforting.  

If nothing else, at least the music had begun, forcing them into the dance.  

Alexander Glen was roughly the same height as Jared but was a more skilled dancer. Perhaps his years in high society had honed his abilities, he moved with the practiced ease of a man in his thirties.  

“Your husband seems quite devoted to you.”  

His voice was smooth, perfectly in sync with the dance’s moderate rhythm.  

“But of course, you’re newlyweds. It would be odd if he wasn’t.”  

He emphasized love with a slight smirk.  

Roselyn held back her irritation and smiled.  

“It’s only natural for a husband and wife to love each other.”  

“Is it?”  

“You and your wife seem close as well. How long have you been married?”  

“About ten years. Or was it eleven? It’s a difficult question. Once you pass three years, counting becomes meaningless.”  

He feigned regret, though his expression was exaggerated and theatrical.  

Roselyn found herself at a loss for words again.  

Some people were best handled with silence.  

“Enjoy it while you can, Duchess,” Alexander murmured as he spun her. His hand briefly pressed against her waist.  

“The honeymoon phase doesn’t last.”  

Unsolicited advice.  

She answered with a shallow smile and, instinctively, glanced toward Jared.  

He was dancing with Naomi, listening to her speak. His expression showed neither discomfort nor reluctance. He looked perfectly at ease.  

That, more than anything, unsettled Roselyn.  

What is he thinking?  

“Take good care of my wife.”  

Her left cheek still tingled slightly where he had kissed her.  

Jared seemed entirely unbothered by the situation.  

Yet Roselyn could sense something.  

There was an undercurrent, a quiet calculation in his actions, but she couldn’t decipher it.  

He was still the ruler of his kingdom.  

And she was still an outsider looking in.  

“You’ve married a difficult man,” Alexander said suddenly.  

Roselyn turned back to him, meeting his blue eyes. She nearly asked What do you mean? but stopped herself.  

He wouldn’t tell her the truth anyway.  

“Good luck,” he said with a gentlemanly smile.  

Roselyn, ever the noblewoman, smiled back.  

Feigning understanding was always the best strategy.  

Showing that you didn’t understand, that was the worst mistake of all.  



Over the past two Tuesdays, Jared had been forced to accept a truth; he had greatly overestimated his own sense of morality.  

He had always thought that being with a woman he didn’t love would be revolting.  

His hesitation toward marriage had largely stemmed from that belief.  

He had thought he could share a home with an appropriate wife, but not a bed.  

On their wedding night, he couldn’t even bring himself to do it sober.  

And after that failed attempt, he had even felt relieved.  

Surely it meant he wasn’t a beast.  

Surely it meant he was still a man of conscience.  

But after two Tuesdays, no, from the morning after his first successful effort, Jared had begun to reevaluate the line between man and beast.  

Now, he had to acknowledge the inevitability of his body.  

Any male of any species was capable of this.  

After all, if they weren’t, how had so many noble bloodlines persisted for centuries?  

“You were magnificent today. Everyone was watching you.”  

He hadn’t meant to say that the moment he walked into the bedroom. It was awkward.  

They had returned from the long evening, retreated to their respective dressing rooms and baths, and prepared for bed. Jared, by habit, poured himself a glass of whiskey from the cabinet.  

And just as he sealed the bottle, she walked in.  

Of all nights, it had to be Tuesday.  

“I hope I was of help to you.”  

“Of course, you were.”  

He nodded, and Roselyn smiled. Her face was fresh from washing, her white chemise modest but delicate.  

For some reason, his gaze drifted to her left cheek.  

Soft. Warm. The sensation of his lips against it lingered faintly.  

“Take good care of my wife.”  

Why did I do that?
